-- Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab) textbook MCQs, chapter 13 (Thermal Physics) PHY-0001 to PHY-0043, chapter 14 (Simple Harmonic Motion) PHY-0044 to PHY-0093, chapter 15 (Physical Optics) PHY-0094 to PHY-0136, chapter 16 (Electrostatics / Static Electricity) PHY-0137 to PHY-0183, chapter 17 (Alternating Current) PHY-0184 to PHY-0244, chapter 18 (Quantum Physics) PHY-0245 to PHY-0290, chapter 19 (Nuclear and Particle Physics) PHY-0291 to PHY-0338, chapter 20 (Medical Physics) PHY-0339 to PHY-0392, chapter 21 (Space and Environment) PHY-0393 to PHY-0446.
-- Original questions in the bank's template (stem, four options, key, why the key is right, why each other option is
-- wrong) with the source page in the textbook. Each question's own chapter is its MDCAT chapter (NUMS and AKU reuse it);
-- FSc Part 2 lists them in bank_questions under the textbook chapter. Not in the free set. INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the
-- file safe to run twice; if the site owner's admin account doesn't exist (a fresh local database) nothing is inserted.

-- Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0001', 'Thermodynamics', 'Thermal physics basics', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 1 (Introduction)', 'In thermal physics, which of these is a property of a microscopic system rather than of a macroscopic one?', '["The temperature of a gas sample", "The kinetic energy of a single molecule", "The pressure of a gas in a cylinder", "The volume of a gas in a container"]', 1, 'A microscopic system is a single atom or molecule, described by quantities such as its kinetic energy and momentum. Temperature, pressure and volume belong to the whole sample, a macroscopic system.', '{"0": "Temperature is a thermal property of the whole sample, so it is macroscopic.", "2": "Pressure is a property of the whole gas, so it is macroscopic.", "3": "The volume of a gas sample is a bulk (macroscopic) property."}'),
  ('PHY-0002', 'Thermodynamics', 'Brownian motion', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 2 (§13.1)', 'According to the book, how does the Brownian motion of suspended particles depend on the fluid they are in?', '["It is faster in a fluid of higher viscosity.", "It does not depend on the viscosity of the fluid.", "It is faster in a fluid of lower viscosity.", "It stops completely in a fluid of low viscosity."]', 2, 'The speed of Brownian particles is inversely related to the viscosity of the fluid, so a less viscous fluid gives faster Brownian movement.', '{"0": "Higher viscosity means more internal friction, which slows the particles down.", "1": "The book states that the speed depends inversely on viscosity.", "3": "Brownian motion never stops; low viscosity makes it faster, not zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0003', 'Thermodynamics', 'Brownian motion', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 2 (§13.1)', 'In Brownian motion, why do lighter particles move faster than heavier ones?', '["Lighter particles are hit by fewer molecules.", "Lighter particles experience more viscosity.", "Heavier particles absorb more heat from the fluid.", "The same momentum transfer from collisions gives a lighter particle a greater speed."]', 3, 'Collisions with fluid molecules transfer momentum to a particle. For a given momentum p = mv, a smaller mass gives a larger speed, so lighter particles move faster.', '{"0": "Fewer collisions would give less motion, not more.", "1": "Viscosity belongs to the fluid, not to the particle''s mass, and more of it would slow the particle.", "2": "Brownian motion is explained by molecular collisions, not by heat absorbed by the particle."}'),
  ('PHY-0004', 'Thermodynamics', 'Brownian motion', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 2 (§13.1 (For your information))', 'Who first explained the movement of pollen grains in a liquid in terms of molecular collisions (1905), an explanation later verified experimentally by J. Perrin?', '["Albert Einstein", "Robert Brown", "Ludwig Boltzmann", "James Clerk Maxwell"]', 0, 'Einstein explained Brownian movement in 1905 as the result of collisions with the liquid''s molecules. Perrin verified it experimentally in 1908 and won the 1926 Nobel Prize.', '{"1": "Robert Brown, a botanist, observed the motion but did not explain it.", "2": "Boltzmann developed statistical mechanics and the Boltzmann distribution law.", "3": "Maxwell is credited in the book with the distribution of molecular speeds."}'),
  ('PHY-0005', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory of gases', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 2 (§13.2)', 'Which of these is an assumption of the kinetic theory of an ideal gas?', '["Gas particles attract each other strongly between collisions.", "Collisions between gas particles and with the container walls are perfectly elastic.", "The volume of the gas particles is large compared with the volume of the container.", "Gas particles all move with the same speed in one direction."]', 1, 'Kinetic theory assumes perfectly elastic collisions, so no kinetic energy is lost when particles collide with each other or with the walls.', '{"0": "The theory assumes no forces of attraction or repulsion between particles except during collisions.", "2": "The particles'' own volume is taken as negligible compared with the volume of the gas.", "3": "The particles are assumed to move randomly in all directions with a range of speeds."}'),
  ('PHY-0006', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory of gases', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 3 (§13.2 (sidebar))', 'The average distance a gas molecule travels before a collision significantly changes its direction or energy is called its', '["root-mean-square displacement", "wavelength", "mean free path", "amplitude"]', 2, 'The mean free path is the average distance a moving particle covers between collisions that noticeably alter its direction or energy.', '{"0": "rms values describe the average of squared quantities, not the distance between collisions.", "1": "Wavelength describes a wave, not the path of a gas molecule.", "3": "Amplitude is the maximum displacement in an oscillation."}'),
  ('PHY-0007', 'Thermodynamics', 'Pressure of a gas', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 4 (§13.3)', 'A gas molecule of mass m moving with velocity component vₓ hits a wall at right angles and rebounds elastically. What is the magnitude of its change in momentum?', '["mvₓ", "zero", "½mvₓ²", "2mvₓ"]', 3, 'Its momentum changes from +mvₓ to −mvₓ, so Δp = −mvₓ − mvₓ = −2mvₓ. The magnitude is 2mvₓ.', '{"0": "mvₓ is the momentum before (or after) the collision, not the change.", "1": "The speed is unchanged, but the momentum reverses direction, so the change is not zero.", "2": "½mvₓ² is kinetic energy, not momentum."}'),
  ('PHY-0008', 'Thermodynamics', 'Pressure of a gas', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 4 (§13.3)', 'In the derivation of gas pressure, a molecule moves back and forth with velocity component vₓ in a cube of side L. What is the time between two successive collisions with the same wall?', '["2L/vₓ", "L/vₓ", "vₓ/2L", "L²/vₓ"]', 0, 'Between two hits on the same wall the molecule travels to the opposite wall and back, a distance 2L. Time = distance ÷ speed = 2L/vₓ.', '{"1": "L/vₓ is the time to cross the box once; the molecule must come back before it hits the same wall again.", "2": "vₓ/2L is the number of collisions per second (a frequency), not a time.", "3": "L²/vₓ has units of m s, not seconds."}'),
  ('PHY-0009', 'Thermodynamics', 'Pressure of a gas', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 4 (§13.3)', 'Using the change in momentum 2mvₓ and the time 2L/vₓ between collisions, what is the average force one molecule exerts on a wall of a cube of side L?', '["2mvₓ/L", "mvₓ²/L", "mvₓ/2L", "mvₓ²/L²"]', 1, 'Force = rate of change of momentum = 2mvₓ ÷ (2L/vₓ) = mvₓ²/L.', '{"0": "2mvₓ/L leaves out one factor of vₓ from dividing by the time 2L/vₓ.", "2": "mvₓ/2L does not have the units of force once vₓ is squared correctly.", "3": "mvₓ²/L² is the pressure (force ÷ area L²), not the force."}'),
  ('PHY-0010', 'Thermodynamics', 'Pressure of a gas', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 5 (§13.3)', 'Because molecules move randomly with no preferred direction, the mean-square velocity component along one axis, <vₓ²>, is related to the mean-square speed <v²> by', '["<vₓ²> = <v²>", "<vₓ²> = 3<v²>", "<vₓ²> = <v²>/3", "<vₓ²> = <v²>/2"]', 2, '<v²> = <vₓ²> + <v_y²> + <v_z²>, and the three are equal for random motion, so <vₓ²> = <v²>/3. This gives the factor ⅓ in P = ⅓ρ<v²>.', '{"0": "The total mean-square speed is shared among three directions, so one component is smaller than <v²>.", "1": "A single component cannot be larger than the total.", "3": "Motion is in three dimensions, not two."}'),
  ('PHY-0011', 'Thermodynamics', 'Pressure of a gas', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 5 (§13.3)', 'The kinetic theory expression for the pressure of a gas of density ρ in terms of the mean-square speed of its molecules is', '["P = ½ρ<v²>", "P = ρ<v²>", "P = ⅔ρ<v²>", "P = ⅓ρ<v²>"]', 3, 'Adding the contributions of all molecules and using <vₓ²> = <v²>/3 gives P = ⅓ρ<v²>, which is the same as PV = ⅓Nm<v²>.', '{"0": "½ρv² looks like kinetic energy per unit volume, not pressure.", "1": "This misses the factor ⅓ that comes from three equal directions of motion.", "2": "⅔ appears in P = ⅔(N/V)<K.E.>, not in the expression with ρ<v²>."}'),
  ('PHY-0012', 'Thermodynamics', 'Pressure of a gas', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 5 (§13.3)', 'According to PV = ⅓Nm<v²>, if the number of molecules in a fixed volume is doubled at the same temperature, the pressure', '["doubles", "halves", "stays the same", "becomes four times"]', 0, 'At the same temperature <v²> is unchanged, and V is fixed, so P is directly proportional to N. Doubling N doubles the pressure, which is why pumping air into a tyre raises its pressure.', '{"1": "More molecules mean more collisions per second with the walls, so the pressure rises, not falls.", "2": "P depends on N directly, so it cannot stay the same.", "3": "P is proportional to N, not to N²."}'),
  ('PHY-0013', 'Thermodynamics', 'Root-mean-square speed', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 6 (§13.3 (Example 13.1))', 'A gas has a pressure of 1.2 × 10⁵ Pa and a density of 1.6 kg m⁻³. What is the rms speed of its molecules?', '["about 274 m s⁻¹", "about 474 m s⁻¹", "about 387 m s⁻¹", "about 2.25 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'From P = ⅓ρ<v²>, <v²> = 3P/ρ = 3 × 1.2 × 10⁵ ÷ 1.6 = 2.25 × 10⁵ m² s⁻². v_rms = √(2.25 × 10⁵) ≈ 474 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "274 m s⁻¹ is √(P/ρ), which leaves out the factor 3.", "2": "387 m s⁻¹ is √(2P/ρ), which uses 2 instead of 3.", "3": "2.25 × 10⁵ is the mean-square speed in m² s⁻²; its square root must be taken."}'),
  ('PHY-0014', 'Thermodynamics', 'Average kinetic energy', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 7 (§13.4)', 'The average translational kinetic energy of a molecule of an ideal gas at absolute temperature T is', '["(1/2)k_BT", "(2/3)k_BT", "(3/2)k_BT", "3k_BT"]', 2, 'Combining PV = ⅓Nm<v²> with PV = Nk_BT gives ½m<v²> = (3/2)k_BT.', '{"0": "½k_BT is the energy for one direction of motion only, not for all three.", "1": "⅔ is the factor in P = ⅔(N/V)<K.E.>, not in the energy.", "3": "3k_BT equals m<v²>, which is twice the kinetic energy."}'),
  ('PHY-0015', 'Thermodynamics', 'Average kinetic energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 7 (§13.4)', 'What is the average translational kinetic energy of a gas molecule at 300 K? (k_B = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹)', '["2.07 × 10⁻²¹ J", "4.14 × 10⁻²¹ J", "6.21 × 10⁻²¹ J", "1.24 × 10⁻²⁰ J"]', 2, '<K.E.> = (3/2)k_BT = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 = 6.21 × 10⁻²¹ J.', '{"0": "2.07 × 10⁻²¹ J is ½k_BT, the share of one direction only.", "1": "4.14 × 10⁻²¹ J is k_BT; the factor 3/2 is missing.", "3": "1.24 × 10⁻²⁰ J is 3k_BT, twice the correct value."}'),
  ('PHY-0016', 'Thermodynamics', 'Average kinetic energy', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 7 (§13.4)', 'According to kinetic theory, the absolute temperature of a gas is a direct measure of', '["the average translational kinetic energy of its molecules", "the total number of molecules in the gas", "the potential energy between molecules", "the mass of each molecule"]', 0, '<K.E.> = (3/2)k_BT, so temperature is directly proportional to the average translational kinetic energy of the molecules.', '{"1": "The number of molecules affects pressure, not temperature.", "2": "An ideal gas has no intermolecular forces, so there is no such potential energy in the model.", "3": "Molecular mass is a property of the gas and is not measured by temperature."}'),
  ('PHY-0017', 'Thermodynamics', 'Average kinetic energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 7 (§13.4)', 'Helium and oxygen are kept at the same temperature. Which statement about their molecules is correct?', '["They have the same rms speed, but oxygen has more kinetic energy.", "Oxygen has both the larger kinetic energy and the larger rms speed.", "Helium has the larger kinetic energy because it is lighter.", "They have the same average translational kinetic energy, but helium has the larger rms speed."]', 3, '<K.E.> = (3/2)k_BT depends only on temperature, so it is the same for both. Since ½mv²_rms is equal, the lighter helium atom has the larger rms speed.', '{"0": "Equal kinetic energy with different masses means the speeds cannot be equal.", "1": "At the same temperature the average kinetic energies are equal.", "2": "Average kinetic energy depends only on T, not on mass."}'),
  ('PHY-0018', 'Thermodynamics', 'Root-mean-square speed', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 7 (§13.4)', 'The rms speed of the molecules of a gas is v at absolute temperature T. If the absolute temperature is made four times larger, the rms speed becomes', '["4v", "16v", "v/2", "2v"]', 3, 'v_rms = √(3k_BT/m), so v_rms ∝ √T. Four times the temperature gives √4 = 2 times the speed.', '{"0": "Speed varies with √T, not with T.", "1": "16v would need T to be multiplied by 256.", "2": "Raising the temperature increases the speed; it cannot halve it."}'),
  ('PHY-0019', 'Thermodynamics', 'Root-mean-square speed', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 7 (§13.4)', 'Gas X has a molecular mass four times that of gas Y. At the same temperature, the ratio v_rms(X) : v_rms(Y) is', '["1 : 2", "2 : 1", "1 : 4", "4 : 1"]', 0, 'v_rms = √(3k_BT/m) ∝ 1/√m. With m_X = 4m_Y, v_rms(X)/v_rms(Y) = √(1/4) = 1/2.', '{"1": "The heavier gas moves more slowly, not faster.", "2": "Speed depends on 1/√m, not on 1/m.", "3": "This inverts the ratio and also forgets the square root."}'),
  ('PHY-0020', 'Thermodynamics', 'Root-mean-square speed', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 6 (§13.3 (Example 13.2))', 'What is the rms speed of nitrogen molecules (28 u) at 300 K? (k_B = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹, 1 u = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg)', '["about 299 m s⁻¹", "about 422 m s⁻¹", "about 517 m s⁻¹", "about 2.7 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'm = 28 × 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ = 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ kg. v_rms = √(3k_BT/m) = √(3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 ÷ 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶) ≈ 517 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "299 m s⁻¹ is √(k_BT/m); the factor 3 is missing.", "1": "422 m s⁻¹ is √(2k_BT/m), which uses 2 instead of 3.", "3": "2.7 × 10⁵ is the mean-square speed in m² s⁻², not its square root."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0021', 'Thermodynamics', 'Root-mean-square speed', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 6 (§13.3 (Example 13.2))', 'The book compares the rms speed of air molecules at room temperature (about 480 to 520 m s⁻¹) with the speed of sound in air (about 340 m s⁻¹). Which is true?', '["The rms speed of the molecules is much smaller than the speed of sound.", "The rms speed of the molecules is greater than the speed of sound.", "The two speeds are exactly equal.", "Air molecules at room temperature are almost at rest."]', 1, 'At 298 K the book finds about 481 m s⁻¹ for O₂ and 515 m s⁻¹ for N₂, both greater than the speed of sound (about 340 m s⁻¹).', '{"0": "The molecules move faster, not slower, than sound.", "2": "Their values differ (about 500 vs 340 m s⁻¹).", "3": "Molecules at room temperature move at hundreds of metres per second."}'),
  ('PHY-0022', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory and gas laws', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 3 (§13.3)', 'The Boltzmann constant k_B is related to the general gas constant R and Avogadro''s number N_A by', '["k_B = R × N_A", "k_B = N_A/R", "k_B = R + N_A", "k_B = R/N_A"]', 3, 'R = N_A k_B, so k_B = R/N_A = 8.31 ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ ≈ 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹. It is the gas constant per molecule.', '{"0": "R × N_A would be a huge number, not 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.", "1": "This inverts the relationship.", "2": "Quantities with different units cannot be added."}'),
  ('PHY-0023', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory and gas laws', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 3 (§13.3)', 'Using PV = Nk_BT, how many gas molecules are there per cubic metre at a pressure of 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa and a temperature of 300 K? (k_B = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹)', '["2.4 × 10²³", "2.4 × 10²⁵", "6.0 × 10²³", "7.3 × 10²⁷"]', 1, 'N/V = P/(k_BT) = 1.01 × 10⁵ ÷ (1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300) ≈ 2.4 × 10²⁵ molecules per m³.', '{"0": "2.4 × 10²³ has the power of ten wrong by 100.", "2": "6.0 × 10²³ is Avogadro''s number, the number in one mole, not per cubic metre here.", "3": "7.3 × 10²⁷ comes from P × T ÷ (k_B × 10⁴); the temperature must divide, not multiply."}'),
  ('PHY-0024', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory and gas laws', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 8 (§13.4)', 'The book quotes the Boltzmann constant as 8.62 × 10⁻⁵ eV K⁻¹. What is k_BT at room temperature (300 K) in electronvolts?', '["about 0.026 eV", "about 0.26 eV", "about 2.6 eV", "about 0.0026 eV"]', 0, 'k_BT = 8.62 × 10⁻⁵ eV K⁻¹ × 300 K ≈ 0.026 eV, a very small energy, which is why electronvolts are used.', '{"1": "0.26 eV is ten times too large.", "2": "2.6 eV is a hundred times too large.", "3": "0.0026 eV is ten times too small."}'),
  ('PHY-0025', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory and gas laws', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 8 (§13.4)', 'One electronvolt is', '["the charge on one electron", "the kinetic energy gained by an electron accelerated through a potential difference of 1 V", "the energy of one mole of electrons", "the potential difference across a 1 Ω resistor carrying 1 A"]', 1, 'The electronvolt is the energy an electron gains when accelerated through 1 V: 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.', '{"0": "The electron''s charge is 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, a charge, not an energy.", "2": "The electronvolt refers to a single electron, not a mole.", "3": "1 Ω × 1 A gives 1 V, a potential difference, not an energy."}'),
  ('PHY-0026', 'Thermodynamics', 'Daily-life examples', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 8 (§13.4 (Daily life examples))', 'When air is pumped into a car tyre, its pressure rises mainly because', '["each molecule becomes heavier", "the molecules stop moving randomly", "more molecules are present, so they strike the tyre walls more often", "the volume of each molecule increases"]', 2, 'Pumping adds air molecules to nearly the same volume, so collisions with the walls become more frequent and the pressure rises.', '{"0": "The mass of each air molecule does not change.", "1": "The molecules keep moving randomly; that is what produces pressure.", "3": "The size of the molecules does not change."}'),
  ('PHY-0027', 'Thermodynamics', 'Statistical physics', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 8 (§13.5)', 'According to the Boltzmann distribution law, N₂/N₁ = e^(−ΔE/k_BT), where N₂ is the population of the higher energy level. This means that in thermal equilibrium', '["more particles are in the higher energy level", "both levels always hold equal numbers", "no particles can occupy the higher level", "more particles are in the lower energy level than in the higher one"]', 3, 'For ΔE > 0, e^(−ΔE/k_BT) is less than 1, so N₂ < N₁: more particles stay in the lower energy state.', '{"0": "The exponential factor is below 1, so the higher level holds fewer particles.", "1": "Equal populations would need the exponential to be exactly 1, i.e. ΔE = 0.", "2": "The factor is small but not zero, so some particles do occupy the higher level."}'),
  ('PHY-0028', 'Thermodynamics', 'Statistical physics', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 8 (§13.5)', 'Two energy levels are separated by an energy exactly equal to k_BT. By the Boltzmann distribution law, the ratio of the population of the upper level to that of the lower level is about', '["0.37", "2.72", "1.00", "0.50"]', 0, 'N₂/N₁ = e^(−ΔE/k_BT) = e^(−1) ≈ 0.37.', '{"1": "2.72 is e⁺¹; the exponent is negative, so the upper level has fewer particles.", "2": "1.00 would need ΔE = 0.", "3": "0.50 would need ΔE = k_BT ln 2 ≈ 0.69 k_BT."}'),
  ('PHY-0029', 'Thermodynamics', 'Statistical physics', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 8 (§13.5 (For your information))', 'The distribution of molecular speeds in a gas, mentioned in the book as an early prediction of statistical physics, was described by', '["Robert Brown", "James Clerk Maxwell", "Jean Perrin", "Isaac Newton"]', 1, 'Maxwell described the distribution of molecular speeds in a gas (around 1860); later experiments confirmed his predictions.', '{"0": "Brown observed Brownian motion under a microscope.", "2": "Perrin verified Einstein''s explanation of Brownian motion.", "3": "Newton''s laws are used in the pressure derivation, but he did not describe the speed distribution."}'),
  ('PHY-0030', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 9 (§13.6)', 'Stars form from clouds of gas and dust in space. As such a cloud contracts under gravity, its', '["density and temperature decrease", "density decreases but temperature increases", "density and temperature increase", "temperature drops to absolute zero"]', 2, 'Contraction packs the particles closer (higher density) and raises their kinetic energy, so the temperature rises (K.E. ∝ T), forming a protostar.', '{"0": "Contraction cannot lower the density.", "1": "Contraction raises the density, not lowers it.", "3": "The cloud heats up as it contracts."}'),
  ('PHY-0031', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 9 (§13.6)', 'In the hydrostatic equation for a star, ΔP/Δr = −GM_rρ_r/r², what does the negative sign show?', '["Pressure increases from the centre outward.", "Gravity pushes the gas outward.", "The density of the star is negative.", "Pressure decreases from the centre outward."]', 3, 'The negative sign shows that pressure falls as r increases, while gravity pulls inward; the outward pressure gradient balances gravity in a stable star.', '{"0": "This is the opposite of what the negative sign means.", "1": "Gravity pulls inward; it is the pressure that pushes outward.", "2": "Density is always positive; the sign belongs to the pressure gradient."}'),
  ('PHY-0032', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 9 (§13.6)', 'A star stays stable, neither collapsing nor expanding indefinitely, when', '["the inward gravitational force is balanced by the outward pressure", "its outward pressure is zero", "its gravity is much stronger than its pressure", "nuclear fusion has completely stopped"]', 0, 'A stable star is in hydrostatic equilibrium: the inward pull of gravity is exactly balanced by the outward pressure of its hot gas.', '{"1": "With no outward pressure, gravity would make the star collapse.", "2": "If gravity wins, the star contracts.", "3": "Fusion produces the energy that keeps the pressure up; when it stops the star can no longer support itself."}'),
  ('PHY-0033', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 12 (§13.6 (Example 13.3))', 'Using P ≈ GM²/R⁴, estimate the gravitational pressure in a star of mass 4 × 10³⁰ kg and radius 2 × 10⁹ m. (G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻²)', '["66.7 Pa", "6.7 × 10¹³ Pa", "1.3 × 10²³ Pa", "2.7 × 10³² Pa"]', 1, 'P ≈ GM²/R⁴ = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × (4 × 10³⁰)² ÷ (2 × 10⁹)⁴ = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 1.6 × 10⁶¹ ÷ 1.6 × 10³⁷ ≈ 6.7 × 10¹³ Pa.', '{"0": "66.7 comes from GM/R², which is the gravitational field strength at the surface (N kg⁻¹), not a pressure.", "2": "1.3 × 10²³ Pa divides by R³ instead of R⁴.", "3": "2.7 × 10³² Pa divides by R² instead of R⁴."}'),
  ('PHY-0034', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 12 (§13.6 (Example 13.5))', 'If the internal pressure of a star is greater than its gravitational pressure, the book says the star will', '["contract and may collapse to a black hole", "stay exactly the same size", "expand and may evolve into a red giant", "immediately become a neutron star"]', 2, 'When internal pressure dominates gravity, the star expands; the book''s example concludes it may evolve into a red giant.', '{"0": "Contraction and collapse happen when gravity is stronger than the internal pressure.", "1": "Equal size needs the two pressures to balance.", "3": "Neutron stars form from collapse, which needs gravity to win."}'),
  ('PHY-0035', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 10 (§13.6)', 'What nuclear process begins when the core of a young star becomes hot enough, and powers it for most of its life?', '["Fission of uranium into lighter nuclei", "Fusion of iron into heavier elements", "Radioactive beta decay of carbon", "Fusion of hydrogen nuclei into helium"]', 3, 'When the core temperature is high enough, hydrogen nuclei fuse to form helium, releasing the energy that sustains the star.', '{"0": "Stars are powered by fusion, not by fission of heavy nuclei.", "1": "Fusion builds up only as far as iron; fusing iron does not release energy.", "2": "Beta decay of carbon is not what powers a star."}'),
  ('PHY-0036', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 10 (§13.6)', 'In the most massive stars, successive stages of fusion build up heavier and heavier nuclei. According to the book, this process continues up to', '["iron", "helium", "carbon", "uranium"]', 0, 'Massive stars keep fusing heavier elements in stages, forming increasingly complex nuclei up to iron, after which the inert iron core collapses.', '{"1": "Helium is made in the first stage, from hydrogen.", "2": "Carbon (and oxygen) come from helium fusion, an intermediate stage.", "3": "Elements beyond iron such as uranium are not made by these core fusion stages."}'),
  ('PHY-0037', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 10 (§13.6)', 'According to the book, stars with roughly ten times the mass of the Sun end their lives in', '["a planetary nebula and a white dwarf", "a supernova explosion, leaving a neutron star or black hole", "a quiet red dwarf stage lasting forever", "a protostar stage"]', 1, 'Very massive stars undergo a supernova; the iron core collapses into a neutron star or a black hole.', '{"0": "A white dwarf and planetary nebula are the end of low and intermediate mass stars.", "2": "Red dwarfs are low-mass stars, not the fate of massive ones.", "3": "A protostar is the beginning of a star''s life, not its end."}'),
  ('PHY-0038', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 10 (§13.6)', 'When a low or intermediate mass star exhausts its nuclear fuel, its core contracts into a', '["black hole", "neutron star after a supernova", "white dwarf, while its outer layers form a planetary nebula", "new protostar"]', 2, 'In low and intermediate mass stars the core becomes a dense white dwarf and the expelled outer layers form a planetary nebula.', '{"0": "Black holes come from the collapse of very massive stars.", "1": "Supernovae and neutron stars are for stars about ten times the Sun''s mass.", "3": "A protostar forms from a contracting cloud, not from a dying star."}'),
  ('PHY-0039', 'Thermodynamics', 'Neutron stars', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 11 (§13.6 (Neutron stars))', 'Rapidly rotating young neutron stars that emit beams of radiation are known as', '["red giants", "white dwarfs", "nebulae", "pulsars"]', 3, 'Many young neutron stars rotate rapidly and emit beams of radiation; these are called pulsars.', '{"0": "Red giants are expanded stars late in their lives, not neutron stars.", "1": "White dwarfs are the remnants of low-mass stars.", "2": "Nebulae are clouds of gas and dust."}'),
  ('PHY-0040', 'Thermodynamics', 'Neutron stars', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 11 (§13.6 (Neutron stars))', 'When a star''s core collapses to form a neutron star, its magnetic field becomes much stronger because', '["the magnetic field lines are squeezed closer together", "the star gains extra mass from space", "the neutron star stops rotating", "the core cools to absolute zero"]', 0, 'During the collapse the field lines are compressed into a much smaller region; closer field lines mean a stronger magnetic field.', '{"1": "The field grows because of compression, not because of extra mass.", "2": "Neutron stars rotate rapidly; they do not stop.", "3": "The collapse does not cool the core to absolute zero."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0041', 'Thermodynamics', 'The Sun', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 11 (§13.6 (Our star))', 'According to the book, in which stage of its life is the Sun at present?', '["Red giant", "Main sequence", "White dwarf", "Planetary nebula"]', 1, 'The Sun is in its main-sequence stage, producing heat and light by converting hydrogen into helium.', '{"0": "The red giant stage comes after the Sun runs low on hydrogen in its core.", "2": "A white dwarf is the Sun''s final stage.", "3": "The planetary nebula stage comes after the red giant stage."}'),
  ('PHY-0042', 'Thermodynamics', 'The Sun', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 11 (§13.6 (Our star))', 'Which sequence correctly shows the life stages of the Sun given in the book?', '["Protostar → nebula → red giant → main sequence → white dwarf → planetary nebula", "Nebula → main sequence → protostar → white dwarf → red giant", "Nebula → protostar → main sequence → red giant → planetary nebula → white dwarf", "Nebula → protostar → main sequence → supernova → neutron star"]', 2, 'The Sun formed from a nebula, became a protostar, is now on the main sequence, and will become a red giant, throw off a planetary nebula and end as a white dwarf.', '{"0": "A protostar forms from a nebula, so the nebula comes first; the main sequence comes before the red giant.", "1": "The protostar comes before the main sequence, and the red giant before the white dwarf.", "3": "The Sun is not massive enough for a supernova and a neutron star."}'),
  ('PHY-0043', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 10 (§13.6 (So you know?))', 'One parsec, a unit of distance used by astronomers, is approximately equal to', '["1 light-year", "1 astronomical unit", "100 light-years", "3.26 light-years"]', 3, 'The book notes that a parsec, used as an alternative to the light-year, is about 3.26 light-years (about 31 trillion km).', '{"0": "A parsec is longer than a light-year.", "1": "An astronomical unit is the Earth–Sun distance, far smaller than a parsec.", "2": "100 light-years is about 31 parsecs."}'),
  ('PHY-0044', 'Waves', 'Oscillatory motion', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 16 (§14.1)', 'Which of these is NOT an example of oscillatory motion?', '["A car moving along a straight road at constant speed", "A vibrating tuning fork", "Atoms vibrating about their mean positions in a solid", "A child on a swing"]', 0, 'Oscillatory motion is to-and-fro motion about a mean position that repeats at equal time intervals. A car moving steadily in a straight line has no mean position to return to.', '{"1": "A tuning fork''s prongs vibrate to and fro about their mean positions.", "2": "The book lists atoms oscillating about their mean positions in a solid as oscillatory motion.", "3": "A swing moves to and fro about its lowest (mean) position."}'),
  ('PHY-0045', 'Waves', 'Terms of oscillation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 17 (§14.1)', 'For a vibrating simple pendulum, the amplitude is', '["the total distance covered by the bob in one vibration", "the magnitude of the maximum displacement of the bob from its mean position", "the length of the string", "the time taken for one vibration"]', 1, 'Amplitude x₀ is the magnitude of the maximum displacement on either side of the mean position (OA or OB in the book''s figure).', '{"0": "In one vibration the bob covers four amplitudes (A → O → B → O → A), so this is 4x₀.", "2": "String length sets the period; it is not the amplitude.", "3": "The time for one vibration is the time period."}'),
  ('PHY-0046', 'Waves', 'Terms of oscillation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 17 (§14.1)', 'A body makes 30 complete vibrations in 12 s. What are its frequency and time period?', '["f = 0.4 Hz, T = 2.5 s", "f = 360 Hz, T = 0.0028 s", "f = 2.5 Hz, T = 0.4 s", "f = 2.5 Hz, T = 2.5 s"]', 2, 'f = number of vibrations ÷ time = 30 ÷ 12 = 2.5 Hz. T = 1/f = 0.4 s.', '{"0": "This swaps the two: 12 ÷ 30 = 0.4 is the period, not the frequency.", "1": "360 comes from multiplying 30 by 12 instead of dividing.", "3": "T must be the reciprocal of f; 2.5 × 2.5 is not 1."}'),
  ('PHY-0047', 'Waves', 'Terms of oscillation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 17 (§14.1)', 'The SI unit and dimension of angular frequency ω are', '["Hz and [T]", "rad and [T⁻¹]", "m s⁻¹ and [LT⁻¹]", "rad s⁻¹ and [T⁻¹]"]', 3, 'Angular frequency is angular displacement per unit time, so its unit is rad s⁻¹. The radian has no dimension, so its dimension is [T⁻¹].', '{"0": "[T] is the dimension of time period, not of a frequency.", "1": "The radian is a unit of angle; angular frequency is angle per second.", "2": "m s⁻¹ is the unit of linear velocity."}'),
  ('PHY-0048', 'Waves', 'Terms of oscillation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 17 (§14.1)', 'An oscillator has a time period of 0.5 s. Its angular frequency is about', '["12.6 rad s⁻¹", "2 rad s⁻¹", "3.14 rad s⁻¹", "0.08 rad s⁻¹"]', 0, 'ω = 2π/T = 2π ÷ 0.5 = 4π ≈ 12.6 rad s⁻¹.', '{"1": "2 is the frequency in hertz (1/T), not the angular frequency.", "2": "3.14 rad s⁻¹ is π, which would be 2π ÷ 2 s.", "3": "0.08 comes from T ÷ 2π, which inverts the formula."}'),
  ('PHY-0049', 'Waves', 'Conditions for SHM', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 18 (§14.2)', 'A body performs simple harmonic motion when its acceleration is', '["constant in magnitude and direction", "directly proportional to its displacement from the mean position and directed towards that position", "directly proportional to its velocity", "inversely proportional to its displacement"]', 1, 'The condition for SHM is a ∝ −x: acceleration is proportional to displacement, and the negative sign shows it always points towards the mean position.', '{"0": "Constant acceleration gives uniformly accelerated motion, not oscillation.", "2": "In SHM acceleration depends on displacement, not velocity.", "3": "An inverse relation would make the acceleration largest near the mean position, the opposite of SHM."}'),
  ('PHY-0050', 'Waves', 'Conditions for SHM', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 18 (§14.2 (Interesting information))', 'The book notes that the trace of an electrocardiogram is periodic but is NOT simple harmonic motion. Why?', '["It does not repeat at regular intervals.", "It has no mean position at all.", "Its restoring force is not proportional to the displacement from the mean position.", "Its period is too short to measure."]', 2, 'Every SHM is periodic, but a periodic motion is SHM only if the restoring force (and so acceleration) is proportional to displacement. That is not true for the ECG recorder''s motion.', '{"0": "The ECG trace does repeat; it is periodic.", "1": "The book''s point is about the restoring force, not about lacking a mean position.", "3": "Period length has nothing to do with whether motion is SHM."}'),
  ('PHY-0051', 'Waves', 'Mass-spring system', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 19 (§14.3)', 'For a mass m on a spring of spring constant k, the equation ma = −kx shows that the angular frequency of oscillation is', '["ω = √(m/k)", "ω = k/m", "ω = 2π√(k/m)", "ω = √(k/m)"]', 3, 'Comparing a = −(k/m)x with a = −ω²x gives ω² = k/m, so ω = √(k/m).', '{"0": "This is inverted; a stiffer spring (larger k) must give faster oscillation.", "1": "ω² equals k/m; the square root is missing.", "2": "2π√(k/m) is not ω; the period is T = 2π√(m/k)."}'),
  ('PHY-0052', 'Waves', 'Mass-spring system', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 19 (§14.3)', 'A 0.8 kg mass oscillates on a spring of spring constant 20 N m⁻¹. What is its time period?', '["about 1.26 s", "about 0.80 s", "about 5.0 s", "about 0.20 s"]', 0, 'ω = √(k/m) = √(20 ÷ 0.8) = 5 rad s⁻¹. T = 2π/ω = 2π ÷ 5 ≈ 1.26 s.', '{"1": "0.80 s is just the mass value; T = 2π√(m/k).", "2": "5.0 is the angular frequency in rad s⁻¹, not the period.", "3": "0.20 s is 1/ω; the factor 2π is missing."}'),
  ('PHY-0053', 'Waves', 'Mass-spring system', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 19 (§14.3)', 'A 0.25 kg mass on a spring of constant 100 N m⁻¹ oscillates with a frequency of about', '["20 Hz", "3.2 Hz", "0.31 Hz", "126 Hz"]', 1, 'ω = √(100 ÷ 0.25) = 20 rad s⁻¹, and f = ω/2π = 20 ÷ 6.28 ≈ 3.2 Hz.', '{"0": "20 is ω in rad s⁻¹; it must be divided by 2π.", "2": "0.31 s is the period (1/f), not the frequency.", "3": "126 comes from multiplying ω by 2π instead of dividing."}'),
  ('PHY-0054', 'Waves', 'Mass-spring system', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 19 (§14.3)', 'If the mass on a spring is made four times larger, the time period of oscillation', '["becomes four times", "halves", "doubles", "does not change"]', 2, 'T = 2π√(m/k), so T ∝ √m. Four times the mass gives √4 = 2 times the period.', '{"0": "The period depends on √m, not on m.", "1": "A heavier mass oscillates more slowly, so the period grows.", "3": "Unlike a pendulum, a spring''s period does depend on the mass."}'),
  ('PHY-0055', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 20 (§14.3 (Simple pendulum))', 'For a simple pendulum displaced through angle θ, which force provides the restoring force towards the mean position?', '["mg cos θ", "the tension in the string", "mg tan θ", "mg sin θ"]', 3, 'Along the string, mg cos θ is balanced by the tension. The component mg sin θ along the arc pulls the bob back towards the mean position.', '{"0": "mg cos θ acts along the string and is cancelled by the tension.", "1": "The tension acts along the string towards the support, not along the path.", "2": "mg tan θ is not a component of the weight in this resolution."}'),
  ('PHY-0056', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 21 (§14.3 (Simple pendulum))', 'The motion of a simple pendulum is SHM only for small angles (about 10° or less) because', '["only then is sin θ approximately equal to θ in radians, making the restoring force proportional to displacement", "the string becomes slack at large angles", "the mass of the bob changes at large angles", "gravity does not act at large angles"]', 0, 'For small θ, sin θ ≈ θ (e.g. at 5°, θ = 0.0873 rad and sin θ = 0.0872), so a = −(g/ℓ)x, which is the SHM condition.', '{"1": "The string stays taut in normal swings; the issue is the sin θ approximation.", "2": "The mass of the bob does not depend on the angle.", "3": "Gravity always acts on the bob."}'),
  ('PHY-0057', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 22 (§14.3 (Simple pendulum))', 'For small oscillations, the time period of a simple pendulum does NOT depend on', '["the length of the string", "the mass of the bob", "the acceleration due to gravity", "the place where it is used"]', 1, 'T = 2π√(ℓ/g) contains only length and g. The period is independent of the bob''s mass (and of a small amplitude).', '{"0": "T ∝ √ℓ, so a longer string gives a longer period.", "2": "T ∝ 1/√g, so g matters.", "3": "g varies from place to place, so the period can change with location."}'),
  ('PHY-0058', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 22 (§14.3 (Simple pendulum))', 'What is the time period of a simple pendulum of length 2.45 m where g = 9.8 m s⁻²?', '["about 1.57 s", "about 0.50 s", "about 3.14 s", "about 6.28 s"]', 2, 'T = 2π√(ℓ/g) = 2π√(2.45 ÷ 9.8) = 2π × 0.5 = π ≈ 3.14 s.', '{"0": "1.57 s is π/2; the factor 2π has been halved.", "1": "0.50 is √(ℓ/g), which still has to be multiplied by 2π.", "3": "6.28 s is 2π, which leaves out the √(ℓ/g) = 0.5 factor."}'),
  ('PHY-0059', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 22 (§14.3 (Simple pendulum))', 'If the length of a simple pendulum is made four times larger, its time period', '["becomes four times", "halves", "becomes sixteen times", "doubles"]', 3, 'T ∝ √ℓ, so multiplying ℓ by 4 multiplies T by √4 = 2.', '{"0": "The period depends on √ℓ, not on ℓ.", "1": "A longer pendulum swings more slowly, so the period grows.", "2": "16 times would need ℓ to be 256 times larger."}'),
  ('PHY-0060', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 22 (§14.3 (Simple pendulum))', 'A pendulum is taken to the Moon, where g is about one-sixth of its value on Earth. With the same length, its period becomes about', '["2.45 times longer", "6 times longer", "6 times shorter", "unchanged"]', 0, 'T ∝ 1/√g. If g becomes g/6, T becomes √6 ≈ 2.45 times the Earth value.', '{"1": "The period depends on 1/√g, so the factor is √6, not 6.", "2": "Smaller g makes the pendulum swing more slowly, so the period increases.", "3": "T = 2π√(ℓ/g) changes when g changes."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0061', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 22 (§14.3 (Second pendulum))', 'A ''second pendulum'' is a simple pendulum whose', '["time period is 1 s", "time period is 2 s", "frequency is 2 Hz", "length is exactly 1 s × g"]', 1, 'The book defines a second pendulum as one that completes one vibration in two seconds (T = 2 s); at g = 9.8 m s⁻² its length is about 0.994 m.', '{"0": "A 1 s period would need a length of only about 0.25 m.", "2": "A period of 2 s means a frequency of 0.5 Hz.", "3": "Length is in metres; ℓ = gT²/4π², not ''g × 1 s''."}'),
  ('PHY-0062', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 22 (§14.3 (Measuring g))', 'To find g with a simple pendulum, a student measures the length ℓ and the period T. Which formula is used?', '["g = 2πℓ/T", "g = T²/4π²ℓ", "g = 4π²ℓ/T²", "g = 4π²T²/ℓ"]', 2, 'Squaring T = 2π√(ℓ/g) gives T² = 4π²ℓ/g, so g = 4π²ℓ/T².', '{"0": "This drops the squares from T = 2π√(ℓ/g).", "1": "This is the reciprocal of the correct expression.", "3": "T² must divide, not multiply."}'),
  ('PHY-0063', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 20 (§14.3 (Interesting information))', 'Who patented the first pendulum clock in 1656?', '["Galileo Galilei", "Robert Hooke", "Isaac Newton", "Christiaan Huygens"]', 3, 'The Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens patented the first pendulum clock in 1656 and finalized the formula linking pendulum length to time.', '{"0": "Galileo studied pendulums, but the book credits the 1656 patent to Huygens.", "1": "Hooke is known for the law of elasticity, F = −kx.", "2": "Newton is not credited with the pendulum clock."}'),
  ('PHY-0064', 'Waves', 'SHM and circular motion', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 22 (§14.4)', 'A ball moves with uniform speed in a circle on a rotating turntable. The shadow of the ball on a screen moves', '["with simple harmonic motion along a straight line", "in a circle at constant speed", "with constant velocity", "with uniformly increasing speed"]', 0, 'The projection of uniform circular motion on a diameter is SHM, so the shadow oscillates to and fro like a mass on a spring.', '{"1": "The screen shows a projection on a line, not the circle itself.", "2": "The shadow speeds up towards the centre and slows down at the ends.", "3": "Its speed rises and falls periodically; it does not keep increasing."}'),
  ('PHY-0065', 'Waves', 'Displacement, velocity, acceleration', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 24 (§14.4)', 'In simple harmonic motion, the speed of the body is maximum', '["at the extreme positions", "at the mean position", "halfway between the mean and extreme positions", "everywhere equal"]', 1, 'v = ω√(x₀² − x²) is largest when x = 0, i.e. at the mean position, where v_max = x₀ω.', '{"0": "At the extreme positions x = x₀, so the speed is zero.", "2": "At x = x₀/2 the speed is only about 0.87 of the maximum.", "3": "The speed changes continuously during the motion."}'),
  ('PHY-0066', 'Waves', 'Displacement, velocity, acceleration', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 25 (§14.4 (Example))', 'A body in SHM has amplitude 0.10 m and angular frequency 10 rad s⁻¹. What is its speed when the displacement is 0.06 m?', '["1.0 m s⁻¹", "0.6 m s⁻¹", "0.8 m s⁻¹", "0.4 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'v = ω√(x₀² − x²) = 10 × √(0.01 − 0.0036) = 10 × 0.08 = 0.8 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "1.0 m s⁻¹ is the maximum speed x₀ω, reached only at the mean position.", "1": "0.6 m s⁻¹ is ωx, which is not the formula for speed.", "3": "0.4 m s⁻¹ comes from ω(x₀ − x), subtracting before squaring."}'),
  ('PHY-0067', 'Waves', 'Displacement, velocity, acceleration', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 24 (§14.4)', 'A body in SHM has angular frequency 5 rad s⁻¹. What is its acceleration when its displacement is +0.04 m?', '["+1.0 m s⁻²", "−0.2 m s⁻²", "−25 m s⁻²", "−1.0 m s⁻²"]', 3, 'a = −ω²x = −(5)² × 0.04 = −1.0 m s⁻². The minus sign shows it points towards the mean position.', '{"0": "The acceleration is opposite to the displacement, so it must be negative here.", "1": "−0.2 uses ω instead of ω².", "2": "−25 is −ω² alone; it must be multiplied by x."}'),
  ('PHY-0068', 'Waves', 'Displacement, velocity, acceleration', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 28 (§14.6)', 'A body in SHM has amplitude 0.05 m and angular frequency 20 rad s⁻¹. Its maximum speed and maximum acceleration are', '["1.0 m s⁻¹ and 20 m s⁻²", "20 m s⁻¹ and 1.0 m s⁻²", "1.0 m s⁻¹ and 1.0 m s⁻²", "0.05 m s⁻¹ and 400 m s⁻²"]', 0, 'v_max = x₀ω = 0.05 × 20 = 1.0 m s⁻¹ at the mean position. a_max = x₀ω² = 0.05 × 400 = 20 m s⁻² at the extreme positions.', '{"1": "This swaps the two values.", "2": "The maximum acceleration uses ω², giving 20, not 1.0.", "3": "0.05 is the amplitude itself, and 400 is ω² without multiplying by x₀."}'),
  ('PHY-0069', 'Waves', 'Graphs of SHM', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 28 (§14.6)', 'When x = x₀ cos ωt, the velocity is v = −x₀ω sin ωt. What is the phase difference between displacement and velocity in SHM?', '["zero", "π/2 (90°)", "π (180°)", "2π (360°)"]', 1, 'The book shows from the graphs that velocity and displacement differ in phase by π/2: velocity is maximum when displacement is zero, and zero when displacement is maximum.', '{"0": "If they were in phase, velocity would be largest at the extreme positions, which is wrong.", "2": "A π difference would make velocity zero at the mean position, which is wrong.", "3": "A 2π difference is the same as being in phase."}'),
  ('PHY-0070', 'Waves', 'Phase', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 26 (§14.5)', 'Two identical oscillators move according to x₁ = x₀ cos ωt and x₂ = x₀ cos(ωt + 180°). They are', '["oscillating in phase", "a quarter of a cycle apart", "oscillating out of phase", "not in SHM"]', 2, 'A phase difference of 180° means that when one is at +x₀ the other is at −x₀. The book calls such oscillators out of phase.', '{"0": "In phase needs a phase difference of 0° or 360°.", "1": "A quarter cycle is a 90° phase difference.", "3": "Both equations are of the SHM form x = x₀ cos(ωt + φ)."}'),
  ('PHY-0071', 'Waves', 'Phase', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 26 (§14.5)', 'For x = x₀ cos(ωt + φ) with φ = 90°, where is the body at t = 0?', '["At the positive extreme position", "At the negative extreme position", "Halfway to the extreme position", "At the mean position"]', 3, 'At t = 0, x = x₀ cos 90° = 0, so the body is at the mean position. The book''s graph for φ = 90° starts at x = 0.', '{"0": "With φ = 0 the body starts at +x₀; here cos 90° = 0.", "1": "With φ = 180° it would start at −x₀.", "2": "Halfway would need cos φ = 0.5, i.e. φ = 60°."}'),
  ('PHY-0072', 'Waves', 'Phase', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 25 (§14.5)', 'In SHM, the quantity (ωt + φ) that describes the state of motion (position and direction) of the oscillator at any instant is called its', '["phase", "amplitude", "angular frequency", "time period"]', 0, 'The time-varying angle (ωt + φ) is the phase. φ, its value at t = 0, is the phase constant.', '{"1": "Amplitude is the maximum displacement.", "2": "ω is the rate at which the phase changes.", "3": "Time period is the time for one cycle."}'),
  ('PHY-0073', 'Waves', 'Energy in SHM', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 29 (§14.7)', 'A spring of spring constant 200 N m⁻¹ oscillates with amplitude 0.10 m. What is its kinetic energy when the displacement is 0.05 m?', '["0.25 J", "0.75 J", "1.0 J", "0.50 J"]', 1, 'Total energy E = ½kx₀² = ½ × 200 × 0.01 = 1.0 J. P.E. at x = 0.05 m is ½kx² = 0.25 J. K.E. = 1.0 − 0.25 = 0.75 J.', '{"0": "0.25 J is the potential energy at x = 0.05 m, not the kinetic energy.", "2": "1.0 J is the total energy, all kinetic only at the mean position.", "3": "0.50 J assumes energy is shared in proportion to distance, but P.E. depends on x²."}'),
  ('PHY-0074', 'Waves', 'Energy in SHM', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 30 (§14.7)', 'At what displacement x are the kinetic and potential energies of a body in SHM equal? (amplitude x₀)', '["x = x₀/2", "x = x₀/4", "x = x₀/√2", "x = x₀"]', 2, 'P.E. = ½kx² and total E = ½kx₀². Equal shares means ½kx² = ½ × ½kx₀², so x² = x₀²/2 and x = x₀/√2 ≈ 0.71x₀.', '{"0": "At x₀/2, P.E. is only a quarter of the total, so K.E. is three times P.E.", "1": "At x₀/4, P.E. is 1/16 of the total.", "3": "At x₀ all the energy is potential and K.E. is zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0075', 'Waves', 'Energy in SHM', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 30 (§14.7)', 'A 0.2 kg body performs SHM with angular frequency 10 rad s⁻¹ and amplitude 0.05 m. What is its total energy?', '["0.05 J", "0.25 J", "0.0025 J", "0.025 J"]', 3, 'E = ½mω²x₀² = ½ × 0.2 × 100 × 0.0025 = 0.025 J.', '{"0": "0.05 J leaves out the ½.", "1": "0.25 J takes x₀² as 0.025 instead of 0.0025.", "2": "0.0025 J uses ω instead of ω²."}'),
  ('PHY-0076', 'Waves', 'Energy in SHM', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 30 (§14.7)', 'If the amplitude of a mass-spring oscillator is doubled, its total energy becomes', '["four times as large", "twice as large", "half as large", "unchanged"]', 0, 'Total energy E = ½kx₀² is proportional to the square of the amplitude, so doubling x₀ gives 2² = 4 times the energy.', '{"1": "Energy depends on x₀², not on x₀.", "2": "A larger amplitude stores more energy, not less.", "3": "E depends on the amplitude, so it changes."}'),
  ('PHY-0077', 'Waves', 'Energy in SHM', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 30 (§14.7)', 'In a mass-spring system oscillating without friction, which statement about energy is correct?', '["K.E. is maximum at the extreme positions.", "K.E. is maximum and P.E. is zero at the mean position.", "P.E. is maximum at the mean position.", "The total energy is maximum at the mean position and zero at the extremes."]', 1, 'At the mean position x = 0, so elastic P.E. is zero and the speed (and K.E.) is maximum. The energy keeps changing form but the total stays constant.', '{"0": "At the extreme positions the body stops, so K.E. is zero.", "2": "P.E. = ½kx² is zero at x = 0.", "3": "The total energy is constant at every position."}'),
  ('PHY-0078', 'Waves', 'Free and forced oscillations', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 32 (§14.8)', 'A body oscillating at its natural frequency without any external periodic force is said to perform', '["forced oscillations", "damped forced oscillations", "free oscillations", "resonant oscillations"]', 2, 'Free vibrations happen at the natural frequency with no outside interference, like a simple pendulum displaced slightly and released.', '{"0": "Forced oscillations need an external periodic force.", "1": "A damped forced oscillation still has an external driving force.", "3": "Resonance needs a driving force at the natural frequency."}'),
  ('PHY-0079', 'Waves', 'Free and forced oscillations', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 32 (§14.8)', 'The vibration of a factory floor caused by heavy machinery running on it is an example of', '["free vibration", "critical damping", "simple pendulum motion", "forced vibration"]', 3, 'The machinery applies a periodic external force to the floor, so the floor vibrates at the driving frequency: a forced vibration.', '{"0": "Free vibration has no external periodic force.", "1": "Critical damping is a degree of damping, not a type of driven motion.", "2": "The floor is not a pendulum."}'),
  ('PHY-0080', 'Waves', 'Damping', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 32 (§14.9)', 'Oscillations whose amplitude decreases with time because of resistive forces such as air resistance and friction are called', '["damped oscillations", "free oscillations", "forced oscillations", "resonant oscillations"]', 0, 'Resistive (damping) forces drain energy, so the amplitude gradually decreases and the body finally comes to rest.', '{"1": "Ideal free oscillations keep a constant amplitude.", "2": "Forced oscillations are driven by an outside periodic force.", "3": "At resonance the amplitude grows large, not smaller."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0081', 'Waves', 'Damping', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 32 (§14.9 (Interesting information))', 'A car''s shock absorbers are designed so that, after a bump, the car returns to its rest position in the shortest possible time without oscillating. This is', '["light damping", "critical damping", "heavy damping", "no damping"]', 1, 'In critical damping the object returns to equilibrium in the shortest possible time; the book''s example is shock absorbers in a car.', '{"0": "Light damping lets the car bounce up and down several times.", "2": "Heavy damping returns it without oscillating, but more slowly.", "3": "With no damping the car would keep bouncing."}'),
  ('PHY-0082', 'Waves', 'Damping', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 32 (§14.9 (Interesting information))', 'A pendulum swinging in thick oil returns to rest slowly without oscillating. This is an example of', '["light damping", "critical damping", "heavy damping", "resonance"]', 2, 'The thick oil provides large resistance, so the system is heavily damped: it takes a long time to settle and does not oscillate.', '{"0": "A lightly damped system (like a playground swing) keeps oscillating with slowly falling amplitude.", "1": "Critical damping returns the system in the shortest possible time, faster than in thick oil.", "3": "Resonance needs a periodic driving force."}'),
  ('PHY-0083', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 33 (§14.10)', 'Resonance occurs when', '["the damping force becomes zero", "the amplitude of the driving force is very small", "the body oscillates freely with no external force", "the frequency of the driving force equals the natural frequency of the oscillating body"]', 3, 'When the driving frequency matches the natural frequency, energy is transferred most efficiently and the amplitude becomes very large.', '{"0": "Damping affects the size of the resonant peak but does not cause resonance.", "1": "Resonance depends on the frequency of the driving force, not on its being small.", "2": "Free oscillation has no driving force, so there is nothing to resonate with."}'),
  ('PHY-0084', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 34 (§14.10)', 'In the book''s set of pendulums hung from a common rod, pendulum P is set swinging. Which pendulums start swinging with a large amplitude?', '["Those with the same length as P", "Those that are longer than P", "Those that are shorter than P", "All pendulums equally"]', 0, 'Pendulums with the same length as P have the same natural frequency, so P''s vibrations, passed through the rod, drive them at resonance.', '{"1": "Longer pendulums have lower natural frequencies, so they stay nearly at rest.", "2": "Shorter pendulums have higher natural frequencies, so they stay nearly at rest.", "3": "Only the ones whose natural frequency matches P''s respond strongly."}'),
  ('PHY-0085', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 35 (§14.10)', 'Soldiers are asked to break step while crossing a bridge because', '["marching in step increases the weight on the bridge", "the frequency of their footsteps might match the bridge''s natural frequency and cause resonance", "breaking step makes the soldiers walk faster", "the bridge is critically damped"]', 1, 'If marching footsteps match the bridge''s natural frequency, resonance can make the bridge vibrate with a large amplitude, and it may collapse.', '{"0": "The soldiers'' weight is the same whether or not they march in step.", "2": "Breaking step is about frequency, not walking speed.", "3": "The concern is resonance; critical damping would actually prevent oscillation."}'),
  ('PHY-0086', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 34 (§14.10 (Radio tuning))', 'When we turn the tuning knob of an analogue radio, we are', '["changing the frequency of the radio station", "increasing the damping of the circuit", "changing the natural frequency of the receiver circuit to match the frequency of the station", "changing the amplitude of the transmitted wave"]', 2, 'Tuning changes the natural frequency of the receiver''s circuit. When it equals the transmission frequency, resonance occurs and that station is received.', '{"0": "The station''s transmitting frequency is fixed by the station, not by our radio.", "1": "Tuning is about matching frequency, not adding damping.", "3": "The receiver cannot change the transmitted wave."}'),
  ('PHY-0087', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 36 (§14.10 (Interesting information))', 'The Tacoma Narrows Bridge (USA), opened in 1940, broke a few months later. According to the book, the cause was', '["an earthquake", "an overloaded truck", "soldiers marching across it", "resonant vibrations set up by a mild wind"]', 3, 'A mild wind set the bridge into resonant vibrations; the amplitude grew so large within hours that part of the bridge broke off.', '{"0": "The book does not mention an earthquake for this bridge.", "1": "The failure was caused by resonance, not a heavy load.", "2": "The marching-soldiers warning is a separate example."}'),
  ('PHY-0088', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 35 (§14.11)', 'How does damping affect resonance?', '["Less damping gives a larger amplitude and a sharper resonance peak.", "More damping gives a larger amplitude and a sharper peak.", "Damping has no effect on the resonance curve.", "Less damping makes the resonance curve flat."]', 0, 'The smaller the damping, the larger the amplitude at resonance and the sharper the peak. A heavily damped system has a fairly flat resonance curve.', '{"1": "More damping lowers and broadens the peak.", "2": "Both the amplitude and the sharpness depend on damping.", "3": "A flat curve is the sign of heavy damping."}'),
  ('PHY-0089', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 36 (§14.11)', 'A pith-ball pendulum and a lead-bob pendulum of the same length and size are driven at resonance from the same rod. The lead bob swings with a much larger amplitude because', '["the lead bob has a higher natural frequency", "air resistance has a much greater damping effect on the light pith ball", "the pith ball is not at resonance", "lead is a better conductor of heat"]', 1, 'Both have the same length, so the same natural frequency. Air resistance damps the light pith ball far more, so its resonant amplitude is smaller.', '{"0": "Equal length means equal natural frequency for a pendulum; mass does not change it.", "2": "Both are driven at their natural frequency.", "3": "Thermal conductivity has nothing to do with the amplitude of oscillation."}'),
  ('PHY-0090', 'Waves', 'Standing waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 37 (§14.11 (Rubens tube))', 'In a Rubens tube, sound of a resonant frequency forms a standing wave in a gas-filled pipe with holes along the top. Where are the flames highest?', '["Where the gas pressure is lowest", "Only at the end near the loudspeaker", "Where the gas pressure is high", "At equal height everywhere along the tube"]', 2, 'More gas escapes where the pressure is high, so the flames are taller there and lower where the pressure is low. The flame pattern shows the standing wave.', '{"0": "Less gas escapes where the pressure is low, so the flames are lower there.", "1": "The flames vary along the whole length of the tube.", "3": "The standing wave makes the flame heights vary."}'),
  ('PHY-0091', 'Waves', 'Standing waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 37 (§14.11 (Chladni plate))', 'When a Chladni plate sprinkled with sand vibrates at a resonant frequency, the sand collects along lines where the plate', '["vibrates with maximum amplitude (the antinodes)", "is hottest", "is thickest", "does not vibrate (the nodes)"]', 3, 'Sand is shaken away from the vibrating antinodes and settles at the nodes, which are stationary, so the pattern shows the nodal lines.', '{"0": "Sand is thrown off the antinodes where the plate vibrates most.", "1": "The pattern is caused by vibration, not temperature.", "2": "The plate has the same thickness throughout."}'),
  ('PHY-0092', 'Waves', 'Standing waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 37 (§14.11 (Acoustic levitation))', 'In acoustic levitation, small bits of styrofoam are suspended in air by', '["being trapped at the nodes of a standing ultrasonic wave between a transducer and a reflector", "a strong magnetic field", "electric charges on the styrofoam", "the heat from the sound waves"]', 0, 'Ultrasonic waves from the transducer reflect and form a standing wave. The pressure difference around the nodes holds the particles there against gravity.', '{"1": "No magnetic field is used; the force comes from sound pressure.", "2": "Electrostatic forces are not involved.", "3": "The particles are held by radiation pressure, not by heat."}'),
  ('PHY-0093', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 35 (§14.10 (Interesting information))', 'The wooden sound box of a guitar or violin makes the sound louder and richer because', '["it damps the vibrations of the strings", "it resonates with the vibrations of the strings", "it stores the sound and releases it later", "it changes the frequency of the strings"]', 1, 'The string''s vibrations pass to the hollow box, which resonates and amplifies the sound waves before they are emitted.', '{"0": "Damping would make the sound quieter, not louder.", "2": "Sound is not stored and released later.", "3": "The pitch is set by the strings; the box amplifies it."}'),
  ('PHY-0094', 'Physical optics', 'Nature of light', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 42 (Introduction)', 'Which experiment, performed in 1801, provided strong evidence for the wave nature of light and established Huygens'' wave theory?', '["Newton''s prism experiment", "Bragg''s X-ray diffraction", "Young''s double-slit experiment", "The photoelectric effect"]', 2, 'Thomas Young''s interference experiment of 1801 showed that light forms interference fringes, which only waves can do.', '{"0": "Newton supported a corpuscular (particle) theory of light.", "1": "X-ray diffraction by crystals was studied by the Braggs more than a century later.", "3": "The photoelectric effect supports the particle (photon) nature of light."}'),
  ('PHY-0095', 'Physical optics', 'Nature of light', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 42 (Introduction)', 'According to the modern view described in the book, light', '["is only a stream of particles", "is only a mechanical wave that needs a medium", "behaves only as a wave in every experiment", "propagates as an electromagnetic wave but interacts with matter as discrete, massless photons"]', 3, 'Quantum mechanics showed the dual nature of light: it travels as an electromagnetic wave but exchanges energy with matter in photons.', '{"0": "Interference and diffraction show that light also behaves as a wave.", "1": "Light is an electromagnetic wave and can travel through a vacuum.", "2": "Some experiments (such as the photoelectric effect) show particle behaviour."}'),
  ('PHY-0096', 'Physical optics', 'Wavefronts', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 42 (§15.1)', 'A wavefront is', '["an imaginary surface joining all points where the waves have the same phase", "the line along which a wave''s energy is lost", "the boundary between two different media", "the maximum displacement of a wave"]', 0, 'A wavefront connects all points of a wave that are in the same phase.', '{"1": "A wavefront has nothing to do with energy loss.", "2": "That is an interface between media, not a wavefront.", "3": "The maximum displacement is the amplitude."}'),
  ('PHY-0097', 'Physical optics', 'Wavefronts', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 43 (§15.1)', 'Light from a very distant star reaching the Earth can be treated as having', '["spherical wavefronts of small radius", "plane wavefronts", "circular wavefronts", "no wavefronts"]', 1, 'A point source makes spherical wavefronts, but very far from the source a small part of a huge sphere is practically flat, so the wavefronts are plane.', '{"0": "Near a point source the wavefronts are strongly curved, but at a great distance they flatten.", "2": "Circular wavefronts are the two-dimensional case, such as ripples from a point in a pond.", "3": "Every travelling wave has wavefronts."}'),
  ('PHY-0098', 'Physical optics', 'Wavefronts', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 43 (§15.1)', 'How is the direction in which a wave carries energy (a ray) related to its wavefront?', '["It is parallel to the wavefront.", "It makes 45° with the wavefront.", "It is perpendicular to the wavefront.", "It has no fixed relation to the wavefront."]', 2, 'Energy travels at right angles to the wavefront. A line drawn at right angles to the wavefronts, showing the direction of travel, is called a ray.', '{"0": "A ray crosses the wavefronts; it does not run along them.", "1": "The angle is always 90°.", "3": "The book states the direction of energy transfer is always perpendicular to the wavefront."}'),
  ('PHY-0099', 'Physical optics', 'Wavefronts', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 43 (§15.1)', 'The shortest distance between two successive wavefronts that are in the same phase is equal to', '["half a wavelength", "the amplitude", "the frequency", "one wavelength"]', 3, 'Successive wavefronts of the same phase are one wavelength apart.', '{"0": "Points half a wavelength apart are in opposite phase.", "1": "Amplitude is a displacement, not a spacing between wavefronts.", "2": "Frequency is not a distance."}'),
  ('PHY-0100', 'Physical optics', 'Wavefronts', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 43 (§15.1)', 'A point source of light is placed at the focal point of a convex lens. The wavefronts on the other side of the lens are', '["plane", "spherical and spreading out", "circular in two dimensions only", "irregular"]', 0, 'Light from a source at the focal point leaves the lens as a parallel beam, so its wavefronts are plane.', '{"1": "The lens converts the spreading spherical wavefronts into plane ones.", "2": "In three-dimensional space they are surfaces, here plane surfaces.", "3": "A lens produces a regular, parallel beam in this case."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0101', 'Physical optics', 'Huygens'' principle', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 43 (§15.2)', 'According to Huygens'' principle, every point on a wavefront', '["absorbs the wave completely", "acts as a source of secondary wavelets that spread out with the speed of the wave", "reflects the wave back to the source", "moves faster than the wave itself"]', 1, 'Huygens assumed each point on a wavefront sends out secondary wavelets in all directions at the wave speed.', '{"0": "The points pass the wave on; they do not absorb it.", "2": "Huygens'' principle describes how the wave moves forward, not reflection back to the source.", "3": "The wavelets spread at the same speed as the wave."}'),
  ('PHY-0102', 'Physical optics', 'Huygens'' principle', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 44 (§15.2)', 'Using Huygens'' principle, the new position of a wavefront after a time t is found by', '["drawing wavelets of radius v/t and joining their centres", "moving the old wavefront back by vt", "drawing wavelets of radius vt from points on the old wavefront and taking the surface tangent to them (their envelope)", "drawing one wavelet of radius vt from the source only"]', 2, 'Each wavelet travels vt in time t. The new wavefront is the envelope, the common tangent surface, of all these wavelets.', '{"0": "v/t has the wrong units for a distance, and joining centres just gives the old wavefront.", "1": "The wavefront moves forward, away from the source.", "3": "Huygens'' method uses wavelets from every point on the wavefront, not just the source."}'),
  ('PHY-0103', 'Physical optics', 'Interference', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 44 (§15.3)', 'Two waves of equal amplitude arrive at a point exactly in phase. By the principle of superposition, the resultant amplitude is', '["zero", "equal to the amplitude of one wave", "half the amplitude of either wave", "twice the amplitude of either wave"]', 3, 'In phase, crests meet crests, so the displacements add: the resultant amplitude is twice that of a single wave (constructive interference).', '{"0": "Zero happens when they meet in antiphase (destructive interference).", "1": "In phase, the two displacements add rather than leaving just one.", "2": "Adding two equal displacements cannot give half of one."}'),
  ('PHY-0104', 'Physical optics', 'Interference', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 45 (§15.3)', 'Which pair of waves can produce a sustained interference pattern?', '["Two sound waves of the same frequency from loudspeakers connected to the same signal generator", "A sound wave and a light wave of the same frequency", "Light from two separate ordinary lamps", "Two waves moving in opposite directions far apart in space"]', 0, 'Interference needs waves of the same type that are coherent and travel in nearly the same direction. Loudspeakers on one generator are coherent sources.', '{"1": "Sound can only interfere with sound; the waves must be of the same type.", "2": "Separate lamps are not coherent; their phase difference keeps changing.", "3": "The waves must overlap while moving in the same or nearly the same direction."}'),
  ('PHY-0105', 'Physical optics', 'Coherence', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 45 (§15.3 (Coherence))', 'Two loudspeakers are connected to two different signal generators of slightly different frequencies. A listener in front of them hears', '["a steady pattern of loud and quiet places that never changes", "the sound get loud, then soft, then loud again as the pattern keeps shifting", "complete silence everywhere", "a sound of exactly double loudness everywhere"]', 1, 'With slightly different frequencies the waves drift in and out of phase, so the sources are not coherent and the interference pattern keeps moving around the room.', '{"0": "A steady pattern needs coherent sources with a constant phase difference.", "2": "Destructive interference everywhere is impossible.", "3": "Doubling everywhere would need the waves to stay in phase at every point."}'),
  ('PHY-0106', 'Physical optics', 'Coherence', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 45 (§15.3 (Coherence))', 'Two sources are called coherent when they', '["emit waves of the same amplitude", "are placed very close together", "emit waves with a constant phase difference, which needs the same frequency", "emit very bright light"]', 2, 'Coherent sources keep a constant phase difference. That is possible only if their frequencies are the same and stay constant.', '{"0": "Different amplitudes can still interfere; the book says this only lowers the contrast.", "1": "Distance between sources does not make them coherent.", "3": "Brightness has nothing to do with coherence."}'),
  ('PHY-0107', 'Physical optics', 'Coherence', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 46 (§15.3 (Coherence))', 'Why does Young''s experiment with lasers use one laser and two slits rather than two separate lasers?', '["Two lasers would be too bright for the screen.", "A single laser gives light of many wavelengths.", "Two lasers would produce sound instead of light.", "Light from two separate lasers cannot be guaranteed to stay in phase, but the two slits lit by one laser act as coherent sources."]', 3, 'Laser light comes in separate bursts. Bursts from two lasers do not keep a fixed phase relation, so dividing one beam with two slits is used to get coherent sources.', '{"0": "Brightness is not the reason; coherence is.", "1": "A laser gives nearly a single wavelength (monochromatic) light.", "2": "Lasers produce light, not sound."}'),
  ('PHY-0108', 'Physical optics', 'Interference', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 45 (§15.3)', 'Two coherent waves of different amplitudes interfere. Compared with the case of equal amplitudes, the interference pattern', '["still forms, but the contrast between bright and dark regions is reduced", "does not form at all", "has bright and dark regions with greater contrast", "has its fringes in completely different positions"]', 0, 'Waves of different amplitudes can still interfere, but they cannot cancel completely, so the dark regions are not fully dark and the contrast falls.', '{"1": "Equal amplitudes are not a necessary condition for interference.", "2": "Unequal amplitudes reduce, not increase, the contrast.", "3": "Fringe positions depend on path difference, not on the amplitudes."}'),
  ('PHY-0109', 'Physical optics', 'Interference of microwaves', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 46 (§15.4)', 'In the book''s microwave interference experiment, microwaves of wavelength 2.8 cm pass through a double gap in a metal barrier. A probe receiver moved around beyond the barrier detects', '["the same intensity everywhere", "regions of high and low intensity due to constructive and destructive interference", "no microwaves at all, because metal blocks them completely", "only one region of high intensity directly behind each gap"]', 1, 'The microwaves diffract through the two gaps and overlap. The probe finds alternating regions of high intensity (constructive) and low intensity (destructive interference).', '{"0": "The overlapping waves interfere, so the intensity varies from place to place.", "2": "The waves pass through the gaps and spread out.", "3": "Because they spread out and overlap, there is a whole pattern of maxima and minima."}'),
  ('PHY-0110', 'Physical optics', 'Young''s double-slit experiment', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 47 (§15.4)', 'In a double-slit experiment with laser light, one of the two slits is covered. What happens?', '["The bright fringes become twice as bright.", "The fringe pattern stays exactly the same.", "The fringes disappear and a broad band of light appears; a point that was dark becomes bright.", "The screen becomes completely dark."]', 2, 'With one slit there is no second wave to interfere with, so only the broad single-slit diffraction band remains. Points that were dark through cancellation now receive light.', '{"0": "Covering a slit removes light; the fringes do not get brighter.", "1": "The interference fringes depend on light from both slits.", "3": "Light still passes through the open slit."}'),
  ('PHY-0111', 'Physical optics', 'Young''s double-slit experiment', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 48 (§15.5)', 'In Young''s experiment, the central point on the screen, equidistant from both slits, is', '["dark, because the path difference is zero", "dark, because the path difference is half a wavelength", "bright, because the path difference is half a wavelength", "bright, because the path difference is zero"]', 3, 'Both rays travel equal distances, so they arrive in phase and interfere constructively, giving the central bright fringe.', '{"0": "Zero path difference gives constructive, not destructive, interference.", "1": "At the central point the path difference is zero, not λ/2.", "2": "A path difference of λ/2 gives a dark fringe."}'),
  ('PHY-0112', 'Physical optics', 'Young''s double-slit experiment', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 48 (§15.5)', 'At the centre of the first dark fringe beside the central bright fringe, the path difference between the waves from the two slits is', '["λ/2", "zero", "λ", "2λ"]', 0, 'The two waves arrive in antiphase (180° apart), which needs a path difference of half a wavelength.', '{"1": "Zero path difference gives the central bright fringe.", "2": "A path difference of λ gives the first bright fringe beside the centre.", "3": "2λ gives the second bright fringe."}'),
  ('PHY-0113', 'Physical optics', 'Young''s double-slit experiment', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 49 (§15.5)', 'For two slits a distance d apart, the condition for a dark fringe at angle θ is', '["d sin θ = mλ", "d sin θ = (m + ½)λ", "d cos θ = mλ", "2d sin θ = mλ"]', 1, 'Destructive interference needs a path difference of a half-integer number of wavelengths, d sin θ = (m + ½)λ with m = 0, 1, 2, …', '{"0": "d sin θ = mλ is the condition for bright fringes.", "2": "The path difference is d sin θ, not d cos θ.", "3": "2d sin θ = nλ is Bragg''s equation for X-rays reflected from crystal planes."}'),
  ('PHY-0114', 'Physical optics', 'Fringe spacing', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 49 (§15.5)', 'In a Young''s double-slit experiment, light of wavelength 600 nm passes through slits 0.30 mm apart, and the screen is 1.5 m away. What is the fringe spacing?', '["0.3 mm", "30 mm", "3.0 mm", "1.5 mm"]', 2, 'Δy = λL/d = (600 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.5) ÷ (0.30 × 10⁻³) = 3.0 × 10⁻³ m = 3.0 mm.', '{"0": "0.3 mm is the slit separation, not the fringe spacing.", "1": "30 mm is ten times too large; check the powers of ten.", "3": "1.5 mm is half the fringe spacing, the distance from a bright fringe to the next dark fringe."}'),
  ('PHY-0115', 'Physical optics', 'Fringe spacing', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 49 (§15.5)', 'Fringes 2.0 mm apart are formed on a screen 1.0 m from two slits 0.25 mm apart. What is the wavelength of the light?', '["50 nm", "250 nm", "5000 nm", "500 nm"]', 3, 'λ = Δy d/L = (2.0 × 10⁻³ × 0.25 × 10⁻³) ÷ 1.0 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ m = 500 nm.', '{"0": "50 nm is ten times too small; check the powers of ten.", "1": "250 nm uses half the fringe spacing (bright to next dark) as Δy.", "2": "5000 nm is ten times too large; check the powers of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-0116', 'Physical optics', 'Fringe spacing', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 49 (§15.5)', 'Light of wavelength 500 nm falls on two slits 0.50 mm apart, and the screen is 2.0 m away. How far from the centre of the pattern is the third bright fringe (m = 3)?', '["6.0 mm", "2.0 mm", "3.0 mm", "7.0 mm"]', 0, 'y_m = mλL/d = 3 × 500 × 10⁻⁹ × 2.0 ÷ 0.50 × 10⁻³ = 6.0 × 10⁻³ m = 6.0 mm.', '{"1": "2.0 mm is the fringe spacing, the position of the first bright fringe only.", "2": "3.0 mm would be 1.5 fringe spacings; m = 3 means three spacings.", "3": "7.0 mm is (3 + ½) fringe spacings from the centre, which is a dark fringe."}'),
  ('PHY-0117', 'Physical optics', 'Fringe spacing', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 49 (§15.5)', 'In Young''s experiment, what happens to the fringe pattern if the two slits are moved closer together (all else unchanged)?', '["The fringes move closer together.", "The fringes spread further apart.", "The fringe spacing does not change.", "The fringes disappear."]', 1, 'Fringe spacing Δy = λL/d is inversely proportional to d. The closer the slits, the more the pattern spreads out.', '{"0": "That happens when the slits are moved further apart.", "2": "Δy depends on d.", "3": "The slits are still coherent sources, so the fringes remain."}'),
  ('PHY-0118', 'Physical optics', 'Fringe spacing', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 49 (§15.5)', 'In a double-slit experiment, red light is replaced by blue light (all else unchanged). The fringes', '["spread further apart, because blue light has a shorter wavelength", "stay the same, because the slits are unchanged", "move closer together, because blue light has a shorter wavelength", "disappear, because blue light is not coherent"]', 2, 'Δy = λL/d. Blue light has a shorter wavelength than red, so the fringe spacing decreases.', '{"0": "A shorter wavelength gives a smaller fringe spacing, not a larger one.", "1": "The spacing depends on the wavelength as well as the slits.", "3": "Light of any colour from one source through two slits is coherent."}'),
  ('PHY-0119', 'Physical optics', 'Thin films', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 50 (§15.6)', 'The coloured bands seen on a soap bubble or a thin oil layer on water are caused by interference between light waves', '["refracted by many tiny prisms in the film", "emitted by the film itself", "absorbed by the film", "reflected from the front and back surfaces of the thin film"]', 3, 'Light reflected from the top surface and light reflected from the bottom surface take different path lengths, so they interfere. Different colours interfere constructively at different thicknesses.', '{"0": "The colours come from interference, not from dispersion in prisms.", "1": "The film does not emit light; it reflects it.", "2": "Absorption removes light; it cannot make bright coloured bands."}'),
  ('PHY-0120', 'Physical optics', 'Thin films', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 50 (§15.6)', 'Two glass plates enclose a thin wedge of air. For light at nearly normal incidence, the path difference between waves reflected from the two surfaces of the air film at a point where its thickness is t is', '["2t", "t", "t/2", "4t"]', 0, 'The wave reflected from the lower surface crosses the air film twice (down and back), so it travels an extra 2t.', '{"1": "The light crosses the film twice, not once.", "2": "The extra path is longer than the thickness, not shorter.", "3": "It crosses the film twice, not four times."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0121', 'Physical optics', 'Thin films', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 51 (§15.6)', 'Where two glass plates touch (zero air thickness), a dark fringe is seen instead of the expected bright one. The book explains this by', '["the glass absorbing all light at the contact line", "a half-cycle phase shift of one of the reflected waves on reflection", "the air film being thickest at the contact line", "light travelling faster in glass"]', 1, 'The path difference there is zero, yet the fringe is dark. So one reflected wave must undergo a half-cycle (180°) phase shift on reflection, making the two waves cancel.', '{"0": "The glass does not absorb all the light there.", "2": "The air film thickness is zero at the contact line.", "3": "Light travels more slowly, not faster, in glass, and speed alone does not explain the dark fringe."}'),
  ('PHY-0122', 'Physical optics', 'Newton''s rings', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 51 (§15.7)', 'Newton''s rings are produced when', '["light passes through a diffraction grating", "X-rays are reflected from a crystal", "a plano-convex lens rests on a flat glass plate and is viewed in monochromatic light", "light passes through two narrow slits"]', 2, 'A thin air film forms between the curved lens surface and the flat plate. Reflections from its top and bottom interfere and form concentric bright and dark rings.', '{"0": "A grating gives straight orders of maxima, not rings.", "1": "X-ray reflection from crystal planes is Bragg diffraction.", "3": "Two slits give straight Young''s fringes."}'),
  ('PHY-0123', 'Physical optics', 'Newton''s rings', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 51 (§15.7)', 'Which is a use of Newton''s rings mentioned in the book?', '["Measuring the speed of sound", "Finding the half-life of a radioactive source", "Measuring the charge on an electron", "Finding the radius of curvature of a lens"]', 3, 'Newton''s rings can be used to find the wavelength of monochromatic light, the radius of curvature of lenses, and surface irregularities in precision optics.', '{"0": "Newton''s rings are an optical interference effect, not an acoustic one.", "1": "Half-life is measured with radiation detectors.", "2": "The electron''s charge was found by Millikan''s oil-drop experiment."}'),
  ('PHY-0124', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 52 (§15.8)', 'The bending of light around obstacles and its spreading into the geometrical shadow is called', '["diffraction", "refraction", "polarization", "dispersion"]', 0, 'Diffraction is the spreading of waves around edges and obstacles into the region of geometrical shadow.', '{"1": "Refraction is bending as light passes from one medium to another.", "2": "Polarization restricts the direction of vibration of a transverse wave.", "3": "Dispersion is the splitting of white light into colours by a prism."}'),
  ('PHY-0125', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction grating', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 52 (§15.8)', 'If the number of slits is increased while the slit spacing stays the same, the principal maxima of the pattern', '["move to larger angles", "stay in the same positions but become much narrower and sharper", "become broader", "disappear"]', 1, 'The maxima occur in the same directions as for two slits, d sin θ = nλ, but become progressively narrower, so their positions (and so wavelengths) can be measured very precisely.', '{"0": "The positions are set by d and λ, which have not changed.", "2": "More slits make the maxima narrower, not broader.", "3": "The maxima become sharper, not fainter to the point of vanishing."}'),
  ('PHY-0126', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction grating', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 52 (§15.8)', 'According to the book, the first diffraction grating was constructed by', '["Thomas Young, using two slits", "Newton, using a prism", "Fraunhofer, using fine wires", "the Braggs, using a crystal"]', 2, 'Fraunhofer made the first grating from fine wires. Today gratings are made by ruling grooves with a diamond point or by photographic reduction.', '{"0": "Young used two slits, not a grating of many slits.", "1": "A prism disperses light by refraction; it is not a grating.", "3": "The Braggs used crystals as natural gratings for X-rays, long after the first optical grating."}'),
  ('PHY-0127', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction grating', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 53 (§15.8)', 'A grating has 500 lines per millimetre. What is its grating spacing d?', '["5.0 × 10⁻⁴ m", "2.0 × 10⁻³ m", "5.0 × 10⁻⁷ m", "2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m"]', 3, 'd is the reciprocal of the number of lines per unit length: d = 1/(500 mm⁻¹) = 0.002 mm = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m.', '{"0": "This is 500 × 10⁻⁶, which multiplies instead of taking the reciprocal.", "1": "2.0 × 10⁻³ m is 1/500 in metres, but the 500 lines are per millimetre, not per metre.", "2": "5.0 × 10⁻⁷ m would be 2000 lines per millimetre."}'),
  ('PHY-0128', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction grating', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 53 (§15.8)', 'Light of wavelength 500 nm falls normally on a grating with spacing d = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m. At what angle is the second-order maximum?', '["30°", "14.5°", "60°", "90°"]', 0, 'd sin θ = nλ, so sin θ = 2 × 500 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.50, giving θ = 30°.', '{"1": "14.5° is the first-order angle (sin θ = 0.25).", "2": "60° has sin θ ≈ 0.87, which does not match nλ/d = 0.5.", "3": "90° would need sin θ = 1, i.e. n = 4."}'),
  ('PHY-0129', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction grating', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 53 (§15.8)', 'A grating with spacing 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m is lit with light of wavelength 600 nm. What is the highest order of maximum that can be seen?', '["2", "3", "4", "33"]', 1, 'sin θ cannot exceed 1, so n ≤ d/λ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 600 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.33. The highest whole order is n = 3.', '{"0": "Order 3 still has sin θ = 0.9, which is possible.", "2": "Order 4 would need sin θ = 1.2, which is impossible.", "3": "33 comes from a power-of-ten slip in d/λ."}'),
  ('PHY-0130', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction grating', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 53 (§15.8)', 'When white light passes through a diffraction grating, which colour in each order is deviated through the largest angle?', '["Violet", "Blue", "Red", "All colours are deviated equally"]', 2, 'sin θ = nλ/d, so longer wavelengths are deviated more. Red, at the long-wavelength end, lies at the largest angle in each order.', '{"0": "Violet has the shortest wavelength, so it is deviated least.", "1": "Blue has a shorter wavelength than red, so its angle is smaller.", "3": "Different wavelengths give different angles; that is how a grating forms a spectrum."}'),
  ('PHY-0131', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction grating', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 53 (§15.8)', 'For a grating to deviate visible light through large angles, its spacing d should be', '["much larger than a millimetre", "much smaller than the size of an atom", "equal to the distance to the screen", "of the same order of magnitude as the wavelength of the light"]', 3, 'sin θ is proportional to λ/d. For substantial deviation d must be comparable to λ; visible-light gratings have about 1000 slits per mm, so d is of the order of 1000 nm.', '{"0": "With d far larger than λ, sin θ would be tiny and the orders would crowd together.", "1": "d smaller than λ would allow no orders except the central one.", "2": "The screen distance does not set the angles."}'),
  ('PHY-0132', 'Physical optics', 'X-ray diffraction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 53 (§15.9)', 'Why can a crystal act as a diffraction grating for X-rays but an ordinary ruled grating cannot?', '["The spacing of atoms in a crystal (about 10⁻¹⁰ m) is of the same order as X-ray wavelengths.", "Crystals are transparent to all light.", "X-rays travel faster through crystals.", "Ruled gratings absorb all X-rays completely."]', 0, 'X-rays have wavelengths of about 10⁻¹⁰ m. Diffraction needs a grating spacing of that order, which the regular rows of atoms in a crystal provide; ruled gratings are far too coarse.', '{"1": "Transparency is not the reason; spacing is.", "2": "Speed is not the issue; X-rays travel at the speed of light.", "3": "The issue is that ruled spacings are far larger than X-ray wavelengths."}'),
  ('PHY-0133', 'Physical optics', 'X-ray diffraction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 54 (§15.9)', 'Bragg''s equation for the reflection of X-rays from crystal planes of spacing d is', '["d sin θ = nλ", "2d sin θ = nλ", "d cos θ = nλ", "2d cos θ = nλ"]', 1, 'The ray reflected from the lower plane travels an extra path 2d sin θ. Reinforcement needs this to be a whole number of wavelengths: 2d sin θ = nλ.', '{"0": "d sin θ = nλ is the grating equation; Bragg reflection involves a path difference of 2d sin θ.", "2": "θ in Bragg''s equation is measured from the plane, so it uses sin θ, not cos θ.", "3": "The path difference uses sin θ, not cos θ."}'),
  ('PHY-0134', 'Physical optics', 'X-ray diffraction', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 54 (§15.9)', 'X-rays reflected from crystal planes 0.20 nm apart give a first-order maximum at a glancing angle of 30°. What is their wavelength?', '["0.10 nm", "0.40 nm", "0.20 nm", "0.35 nm"]', 2, 'From 2d sin θ = nλ with n = 1: λ = 2 × 0.20 nm × sin 30° = 2 × 0.20 × 0.5 = 0.20 nm.', '{"0": "0.10 nm leaves out the factor 2.", "1": "0.40 nm leaves out sin 30° = 0.5.", "3": "0.35 nm uses cos 30° instead of sin 30°."}'),
  ('PHY-0135', 'Physical optics', 'X-ray diffraction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 54 (§15.9)', 'X-ray diffraction has been used to find the structure of which important biological molecule mentioned in the book?', '["Glucose", "Water", "Sodium chloride in blood", "DNA (the double helix)"]', 3, 'The book notes that X-ray diffraction helped find the structures of haemoglobin and the double helix of DNA.', '{"0": "The book does not name glucose.", "1": "The book does not name water.", "2": "Sodium chloride is a crystal, but the book''s biological examples are haemoglobin and DNA."}'),
  ('PHY-0136', 'Physical optics', 'Interference', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 45 (§15.3 (Coherence))', 'Haloes of light seen around street lamps or the Moon on a foggy night are given in the book as an everyday example of', '["interference of light", "photoelectric emission", "radioactive decay", "total internal reflection"]', 0, 'The book gives haloes around lamps or the Moon in fog, and light and dark bands seen through fabric, as interference effects we see in everyday life.', '{"1": "The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons by light, not haloes.", "2": "Radioactive decay has nothing to do with these haloes.", "3": "The book gives these as interference effects, not total internal reflection."}'),
  ('PHY-0137', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential difference', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 58 (§16.1)', 'The electric potential difference between two points is defined as', '["the force per unit charge at a point", "the work done per unit charge in moving a charge between the two points", "the charge per unit volt", "the work done per unit time"]', 1, 'Potential difference ΔV = W/q: work per unit charge. Its unit is the volt, 1 V = 1 J C⁻¹.', '{"0": "Force per unit charge is the electric field intensity.", "2": "Charge per volt is capacitance.", "3": "Work per unit time is power."}'),
  ('PHY-0138', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential difference', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 58 (§16.1)', 'In the relation E = −ΔV/Δr, the negative sign shows that', '["the electric field points from lower to higher potential", "the potential is always negative", "the electric field points from higher to lower potential", "the field is always zero where the potential is high"]', 2, 'The field is the negative of the potential gradient: moving along the field, the potential falls.', '{"0": "This is the opposite of what the sign means.", "1": "The sign refers to the gradient, not to the value of the potential.", "3": "A high potential does not mean zero field."}'),
  ('PHY-0139', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential difference', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 58 (§16.1)', 'A 12 V battery is connected across two parallel metal plates 4.0 mm apart. What is the electric field between them?', '["48 N C⁻¹", "3.0 N C⁻¹", "0.33 N C⁻¹", "3000 N C⁻¹"]', 3, 'In a uniform field E = ΔV/d = 12 V ÷ 0.004 m = 3000 V m⁻¹ = 3000 N C⁻¹.', '{"0": "48 comes from multiplying V by d (in mm) instead of dividing.", "1": "3.0 leaves the distance in millimetres instead of converting to metres.", "2": "0.33 divides d (in mm) by V, which inverts the formula."}'),
  ('PHY-0140', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential difference', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 58 (§16.1)', 'The unit V m⁻¹ for electric field is equivalent to', '["N C⁻¹", "J C⁻¹", "C N⁻¹", "N m C⁻¹"]', 0, '1 V m⁻¹ = 1 J C⁻¹ m⁻¹ = 1 N m C⁻¹ m⁻¹ = 1 N C⁻¹.', '{"1": "J C⁻¹ is the volt itself, the unit of potential, not of field.", "2": "This is the reciprocal of the field unit.", "3": "N m C⁻¹ equals J C⁻¹, the volt, the unit of potential."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0141', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 59 (§16.2)', 'The electric potential at a point in an electric field is the work done in', '["bringing a unit negative charge from the point to infinity at high speed", "bringing a unit positive charge from infinity to that point", "moving any charge around a closed loop", "moving a unit positive charge along the field for 1 m"]', 1, 'Taking the potential at infinity as zero, the potential at a point is the work done per unit positive charge in bringing it from infinity to that point, keeping it in equilibrium.', '{"0": "The definition uses a unit positive charge brought in from infinity, kept in equilibrium.", "2": "Around a closed loop in an electrostatic field the work is zero.", "3": "That gives the field strength in a uniform field, not the potential."}'),
  ('PHY-0142', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 59 (§16.2)', 'Electric potential and potential difference are', '["vector quantities", "both measured in N C⁻¹", "scalar quantities", "always positive"]', 2, 'Both are defined as work (a scalar) divided by charge (a scalar), so they are scalars.', '{"0": "They have no direction; the electric field is the vector quantity.", "1": "Their unit is the volt (J C⁻¹); N C⁻¹ is the field unit.", "3": "The potential due to a negative charge is negative."}'),
  ('PHY-0143', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 60 (§16.2)', 'What is the electric potential at a distance of 0.30 m from a point charge of +2.0 nC? (1/4πε₀ = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻²)', '["200 V", "18 V", "6.0 V", "60 V"]', 3, 'V = (1/4πε₀)(q/r) = 9 × 10⁹ × 2.0 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.30 = 60 V.', '{"0": "200 V divides by r² (0.09) instead of r; that gives the field, in N C⁻¹.", "1": "18 V multiplies by r instead of dividing.", "2": "6.0 V has the power of ten wrong."}'),
  ('PHY-0144', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 60 (§16.2)', 'Charges of +4 nC and −4 nC are placed a small distance apart. At the point midway between them, the electric potential is', '["zero, because the potentials of the two charges cancel", "twice the potential of one charge", "positive, because the positive charge is nearer", "infinite"]', 0, 'Potential is a scalar and adds algebraically. At the midpoint the two contributions are equal in size and opposite in sign, so the total is zero, even though the field there is not zero.', '{"1": "The contributions have opposite signs, so they do not add up.", "2": "Both charges are the same distance from the midpoint.", "3": "The potential is finite at any point away from the charges."}'),
  ('PHY-0145', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 61 (§16.2 (Example))', 'Charges of +6 μC and −2 μC are 40 cm apart. At what point between them is the electric potential zero?', '["10 cm from the positive charge", "30 cm from the positive charge", "20 cm from each charge", "There is no such point between them."]', 1, 'Zero potential needs k(6 μC)/x = k(2 μC)/(40 − x). So 6(40 − x) = 2x, 240 = 8x and x = 30 cm from the positive charge (10 cm from the negative one).', '{"0": "10 cm from the positive charge would put the point next to the larger charge, where its potential dominates.", "2": "At the midpoint the larger positive charge gives the bigger contribution.", "3": "For unlike charges there is always a zero-potential point between them."}'),
  ('PHY-0146', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 61 (§16.3)', 'A positive charge is released from rest in an electric field. It moves', '["from lower to higher potential, gaining kinetic energy", "from higher to lower potential, losing kinetic energy", "from higher to lower potential, gaining kinetic energy", "only at right angles to the field"]', 2, 'A free positive charge accelerates towards lower potential. Its potential energy falls by qΔV and appears as kinetic energy.', '{"0": "A negative charge moves from lower to higher potential, not a positive one.", "1": "The lost potential energy becomes kinetic energy, so it speeds up.", "3": "A free charge accelerates along the field, not across it."}'),
  ('PHY-0147', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 62 (§16.3)', 'An electron is accelerated from rest through a potential difference of 200 V. The kinetic energy it gains is', '["200 J", "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J", "1.25 × 10²¹ J", "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁷ J"]', 3, 'K.E. = qV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C × 200 V = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁷ J, which is 200 eV.', '{"0": "200 is the energy in electronvolts, not in joules.", "1": "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is 1 eV, the energy gained through only 1 V.", "2": "This divides 200 by the electron''s charge instead of multiplying."}'),
  ('PHY-0148', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 62 (§16.3)', 'An electron (m = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg, e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C) starts from rest and is accelerated through 100 V. Its final speed is about', '["5.9 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹", "3.5 × 10¹³ m s⁻¹", "4.2 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹", "5.9 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹"]', 0, '½mv² = eV, so v = √(2eV/m) = √(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100 ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹) ≈ 5.9 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "3.5 × 10¹³ is v², which must be square-rooted (and it is faster than light).", "2": "4.2 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ is √(eV/m); the factor 2 is missing.", "3": "5.9 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹ has the power of ten wrong."}'),
  ('PHY-0149', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 63 (§16.3)', 'What is the electric potential energy of two charges of +3 μC and +4 μC placed 0.20 m apart? (1/4πε₀ = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻²)', '["2.7 J", "0.54 J", "0.108 J", "5.4 × 10⁻⁷ J"]', 1, 'U = (1/4πε₀)(Qq/r) = 9 × 10⁹ × 3 × 10⁻⁶ × 4 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 0.20 = 0.54 J.', '{"0": "2.7 J divides by r² (0.04) instead of r.", "2": "0.108 J multiplies by r instead of dividing.", "3": "5.4 × 10⁻⁷ J has the powers of ten wrong."}'),
  ('PHY-0150', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 62 (§16.3)', 'In assembling two point charges, the first charge Q is brought from infinity to point A. How much work is done in this step?', '["QV, where V is the potential at A due to Q", "Q²/4πε₀r", "Zero, because there is no electric field at A yet", "Infinite"]', 2, 'Before Q arrives there is no other charge, so no field opposes it and no work is needed. Work is needed only for the second charge, which moves in Q''s field.', '{"0": "Q does not move in its own field; no potential exists at A before it is placed.", "1": "This is the total potential energy of two charges, not the work for the first one.", "3": "No force opposes the first charge, so the work is zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0151', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 63 (§16.3)', 'Two positive point charges are moved so that their separation becomes three times larger. Their electric potential energy becomes', '["three times its original value", "one-ninth of its original value", "nine times its original value", "one-third of its original value"]', 3, 'U = Qq/4πε₀r is inversely proportional to r. Tripling r divides U by 3.', '{"0": "Moving like charges apart lowers their potential energy.", "1": "U depends on 1/r, not 1/r²; force follows 1/r².", "2": "Increasing the separation cannot increase U for like charges."}'),
  ('PHY-0152', 'Electrostatics', 'Capacitance of a sphere', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 63 (§16.4)', 'The capacitance of an isolated spherical conductor is defined as', '["the charge stored on it per unit potential at its surface", "the potential at its surface per unit charge", "the charge stored per unit radius", "the energy stored per unit charge"]', 0, 'C = Q/V: the charge on the sphere per unit electric potential at its surface. Its unit is the farad.', '{"1": "This is 1/C, the reciprocal of capacitance.", "2": "Capacitance is charge per volt, not per metre.", "3": "Energy per unit charge is a potential, not a capacitance."}'),
  ('PHY-0153', 'Electrostatics', 'Capacitance of a sphere', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 64 (§16.4)', 'What is the capacitance of an isolated metal sphere of radius 9.0 cm in air? (1/4πε₀ = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻²)', '["1.0 × 10⁻⁹ F", "1.0 × 10⁻¹¹ F", "8.1 × 10⁸ F", "1.0 × 10⁻¹³ F"]', 1, 'C = 4πε₀R = R ÷ (9 × 10⁹) = 0.090 ÷ 9 × 10⁹ = 1.0 × 10⁻¹¹ F (10 pF).', '{"0": "1.0 × 10⁻⁹ F uses R = 9 (in cm) without converting to metres.", "2": "8.1 × 10⁸ F multiplies R by 9 × 10⁹ instead of dividing.", "3": "1.0 × 10⁻¹³ F has the power of ten wrong by 100."}'),
  ('PHY-0154', 'Electrostatics', 'Capacitance of a sphere', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 64 (§16.4)', 'The capacitance of an isolated spherical conductor', '["decreases as the radius increases", "increases as more charge is placed on it", "increases with the radius of the sphere and does not depend on the charge on it", "is the same for all spheres"]', 2, 'C = 4πε₀ε_rR depends only on the radius and the medium. A larger sphere stores more charge at the same surface potential.', '{"0": "C is directly proportional to R.", "1": "Adding charge raises the potential in proportion, so Q/V stays the same.", "3": "C depends on R, so different spheres have different capacitances."}'),
  ('PHY-0155', 'Electrostatics', 'Parallel plate capacitor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 65 (§16.5)', 'A capacitor has a capacitance of 1 farad if', '["a current of 1 A flows through it for 1 s", "it stores 1 J of energy at 1 V", "its plates are 1 m apart", "a charge of 1 C on one plate produces a potential difference of 1 V between the plates"]', 3, 'C = Q/V, so 1 F = 1 C V⁻¹: 1 coulomb of charge gives 1 volt across the plates.', '{"0": "1 A for 1 s is 1 C of charge, but that says nothing about the voltage.", "1": "A 1 F capacitor at 1 V stores ½CV² = 0.5 J, not 1 J.", "2": "Plate spacing alone does not define the farad."}'),
  ('PHY-0156', 'Electrostatics', 'Parallel plate capacitor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 65 (§16.5)', 'A parallel plate capacitor in air has plates of area 0.020 m² separated by 1.0 mm. Its capacitance is about (ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² F m⁻¹)', '["177 pF", "17.7 pF", "1.77 pF", "0.177 μF"]', 0, 'C = ε₀A/d = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 0.020 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻³ = 1.77 × 10⁻¹⁰ F = 177 pF.', '{"1": "17.7 pF is ten times too small.", "2": "1.77 pF is a hundred times too small.", "3": "0.177 μF is a thousand times too large."}'),
  ('PHY-0157', 'Electrostatics', 'Parallel plate capacitor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 65 (§16.5)', 'The plate area of a parallel plate capacitor is doubled and the plate separation is also doubled. Its capacitance', '["doubles", "stays the same", "halves", "becomes four times"]', 1, 'C = ε₀A/d. Doubling A doubles C and doubling d halves it, so the two changes cancel.', '{"0": "Doubling d halves C, which cancels the effect of doubling A.", "2": "Doubling A doubles C, which cancels the effect of doubling d.", "3": "The two changes act in opposite directions."}'),
  ('PHY-0158', 'Electrostatics', 'Dielectrics', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 65 (§16.5)', 'A dielectric of relative permittivity 5 completely fills the space between the plates of a capacitor that had a capacitance of 4 μF in air. The new capacitance is', '["0.8 μF", "9 μF", "20 μF", "4 μF"]', 2, 'A dielectric increases capacitance by the factor ε_r: C = ε_r × C_air = 5 × 4 μF = 20 μF.', '{"0": "0.8 μF divides by ε_r, but a dielectric increases capacitance.", "1": "9 μF adds ε_r to C instead of multiplying.", "3": "A dielectric changes the capacitance."}'),
  ('PHY-0159', 'Electrostatics', 'Dielectrics', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 66 (§16.5)', 'The dielectric constant (relative permittivity) of a material is defined as the ratio', '["C with vacuum ÷ C with the material", "charge on the plates ÷ potential difference", "energy stored ÷ charge stored", "C with the material between the plates ÷ C with vacuum between them"]', 3, 'ε_r = C_med/C_vac, the factor by which filling the gap with the insulator raises the capacitance.', '{"0": "This inverted ratio would be less than 1.", "1": "Q/V is the capacitance itself, not the dielectric constant.", "2": "Energy ÷ charge gives a potential, not a ratio of capacitances."}'),
  ('PHY-0160', 'Electrostatics', 'Dielectrics', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 66 (§16.5)', 'When a dielectric slab is placed between the plates of an isolated charged capacitor, the potential difference falls. The book explains this by', '["polarization of the dielectric, which puts charges on its faces opposite in sign to the charges on the nearby plates", "free electrons flowing through the dielectric from one plate to the other", "the dielectric removing charge from the plates", "the plates moving further apart"]', 0, 'In the field, the electrons of each dielectric atom shift slightly opposite to the field. Opposite charges appear on the dielectric faces next to the plates, which weakens the field and so the potential difference.', '{"1": "A dielectric is an insulator with no free electrons.", "2": "The charge on the isolated plates stays the same; only V changes.", "3": "The plate separation does not change."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0161', 'Electrostatics', 'Dielectrics', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 66 (§16.5)', 'When an atom of a dielectric is placed in an electric field, its electrons', '["leave the atom and flow as a current", "are displaced slightly opposite to the field relative to the nucleus", "move in the direction of the field", "are not affected at all"]', 1, 'The bound electrons shift slightly opposite to the field direction while the nucleus is pulled the other way. The atom becomes polarized.', '{"0": "Electrons in a dielectric are bound and cannot flow freely.", "2": "Negative electrons are pushed opposite to the field.", "3": "The field does distort the atom; that is polarization."}'),
  ('PHY-0162', 'Electrostatics', 'Combination of capacitors', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 67 (§16.6)', 'Capacitors of 2 μF, 3 μF and 5 μF are connected in parallel. Their equivalent capacitance is', '["0.97 μF", "30 μF", "10 μF", "3.3 μF"]', 2, 'In parallel, C_e = C₁ + C₂ + C₃ = 2 + 3 + 5 = 10 μF.', '{"0": "0.97 μF is the series result, from 1/C = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/5.", "1": "30 μF multiplies the values.", "3": "3.3 μF is the average, which is not how capacitors combine."}'),
  ('PHY-0163', 'Electrostatics', 'Combination of capacitors', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 67 (§16.6)', 'When capacitors are connected in parallel across a battery, which quantity is the same for each capacitor?', '["The charge on it", "The energy stored in it", "Its capacitance", "The potential difference across it"]', 3, 'Each capacitor is connected directly across the same two terminals, so each has the same potential difference V. The charges differ according to Q = CV.', '{"0": "In parallel the charge on each capacitor depends on its capacitance.", "1": "Energy ½CV² differs when the capacitances differ.", "2": "The capacitors can have any capacitances."}'),
  ('PHY-0164', 'Electrostatics', 'Combination of capacitors', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 68 (§16.6)', 'Capacitors of 6 μF and 3 μF are connected in series. Their equivalent capacitance is', '["2 μF", "9 μF", "4.5 μF", "18 μF"]', 0, 'In series, 1/C_e = 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/6 + 2/6 = 3/6, so C_e = 2 μF. It is smaller than the smallest capacitor.', '{"1": "9 μF is the parallel result.", "2": "4.5 μF is the average.", "3": "18 μF is the product, not the series combination."}'),
  ('PHY-0165', 'Electrostatics', 'Combination of capacitors', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 69 (§16.6)', 'Capacitors of 6 μF and 3 μF are connected in series across a 12 V battery. What is the potential difference across the 3 μF capacitor?', '["4 V", "8 V", "6 V", "12 V"]', 1, 'C_e = 2 μF, so Q = C_eV = 2 μF × 12 V = 24 μC on each capacitor. V across 3 μF = Q/C = 24 μC ÷ 3 μF = 8 V (and 4 V across 6 μF).', '{"0": "4 V is across the 6 μF capacitor; the smaller capacitor takes the larger voltage.", "2": "6 V assumes the voltage is shared equally, which needs equal capacitances.", "3": "12 V is the total; in series the voltage is divided."}'),
  ('PHY-0166', 'Electrostatics', 'Combination of capacitors', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 68 (§16.6)', 'In a series combination of capacitors, every capacitor has the same', '["potential difference", "capacitance", "charge", "stored energy"]', 2, 'The battery puts +Q on the first plate, and induction gives each following plate an equal and opposite charge, so each capacitor carries the same charge Q.', '{"0": "The voltage divides according to V = Q/C, so it differs when the capacitances differ.", "1": "The capacitors can have different capacitances.", "3": "Energy Q²/2C differs when the capacitances differ."}'),
  ('PHY-0167', 'Electrostatics', 'Combination of capacitors', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 67 (§16.6 (Example))', 'A 20 μF capacitor charged to 50 V is connected in parallel to an uncharged 30 μF capacitor. What is the final common potential difference?', '["30 V", "50 V", "25 V", "20 V"]', 3, 'Charge is conserved: Q = 20 μF × 50 V = 1000 μC. Shared over 20 + 30 = 50 μF, V = 1000 μC ÷ 50 μF = 20 V.', '{"0": "30 V would need a total charge of 1500 μC.", "1": "50 V ignores the charge flowing onto the second capacitor.", "2": "25 V assumes the voltage simply halves, which needs equal capacitances."}'),
  ('PHY-0168', 'Electrostatics', 'Energy in a capacitor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 70 (§16.7)', 'The energy stored in a charged capacitor can be found from a graph of potential difference V against charge Q. It is equal to', '["the area under the V–Q graph", "the slope of the V–Q graph", "the intercept on the V axis", "the product of the slope and the area"]', 0, 'Each small charge ΔQ needs work VΔQ, a thin strip under the line. Adding the strips gives the area of the triangle, ½QV.', '{"1": "The slope of V against Q is 1/C, not the energy.", "2": "The graph passes through the origin, so the intercept is zero.", "3": "This has no physical meaning here."}'),
  ('PHY-0169', 'Electrostatics', 'Energy in a capacitor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 71 (§16.7)', 'A 20 μF capacitor is charged to 200 V. How much energy does it store?', '["0.80 J", "0.40 J", "4.0 × 10⁻³ J", "2.0 × 10⁻³ J"]', 1, 'W = ½CV² = ½ × 20 × 10⁻⁶ × (200)² = ½ × 20 × 10⁻⁶ × 40 000 = 0.40 J.', '{"0": "0.80 J leaves out the ½.", "2": "4.0 × 10⁻³ J is CV, the charge in coulombs, not the energy.", "3": "2.0 × 10⁻³ J is ½CV, which forgets to square V."}'),
  ('PHY-0170', 'Electrostatics', 'Energy in a capacitor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 71 (§16.7)', 'The charge on a capacitor of fixed capacitance is tripled. The energy it stores becomes', '["three times as large", "one-third as large", "nine times as large", "unchanged"]', 2, 'W = Q²/2C, so the energy is proportional to Q². Tripling Q gives 3² = 9 times the energy.', '{"0": "Energy depends on Q², not on Q.", "1": "More charge stores more energy.", "3": "The energy depends on the charge."}'),
  ('PHY-0171', 'Electrostatics', 'Energy in a capacitor', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 71 (§16.7)', 'Two identical capacitors, each of capacitance C, are connected to the same battery of voltage V, first in parallel and then in series. Energy stored in parallel ÷ energy stored in series =', '["2", "1", "1/4", "4"]', 3, 'Parallel: C_e = 2C, energy = ½(2C)V² = CV². Series: C_e = C/2, energy = ½(C/2)V² = CV²/4. The ratio is 4.', '{"0": "2 compares the equivalent capacitances wrongly (2C vs C).", "1": "The equivalent capacitance differs, so the energies cannot be equal.", "2": "This inverts the ratio; the parallel connection stores more."}'),
  ('PHY-0172', 'Electrostatics', 'Energy in a capacitor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 71 (§16.7)', 'Expressed in terms of the electric field E between its plates, the energy stored per unit volume in a capacitor with vacuum between the plates is', '["½ε₀E²", "ε₀E", "½ε₀E", "ε₀E²/d"]', 0, 'Substituting V = Ed and C = ε₀A/d into ½CV² gives ½ε₀E²(Ad). Dividing by the volume Ad gives an energy density of ½ε₀E².', '{"1": "ε₀E is the surface charge density σ, not energy per volume.", "2": "The field must be squared.", "3": "Dividing by d again gives the wrong units for energy per unit volume."}'),
  ('PHY-0173', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 73 (§16.8)', 'The time constant of an RC circuit with R = 2.0 MΩ and C = 5.0 μF is', '["0.4 s", "10 s", "2.5 s", "10 ms"]', 1, 'τ = RC = 2.0 × 10⁶ Ω × 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ F = 10 s.', '{"0": "0.4 s divides 2.0 by 5.0 (and drops the prefixes); τ is the product RC.", "2": "2.5 s divides 5.0 by 2.0 (and drops the prefixes); τ is the product RC.", "3": "10 ms has the power of ten wrong by a thousand: mega × micro = 1."}'),
  ('PHY-0174', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 73 (§16.8)', 'When a capacitor charges through a resistor, how much of its final charge q₀ does it hold after one time constant RC?', '["About 0.37 q₀", "About 0.50 q₀", "About 0.63 q₀", "The whole of q₀"]', 2, 'The book defines the time constant as the time to deposit 0.63 of the equilibrium charge. (During discharge, 0.37 of the charge is left after one RC.)', '{"0": "0.37 is what remains after one time constant of discharge.", "1": "Half the charge is reached after 0.69 RC, a little less than one time constant.", "3": "A charging capacitor only approaches q₀ gradually and never reaches it at t = RC."}'),
  ('PHY-0175', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 75 (§16.9)', 'A charged capacitor discharges through a resistor. What fraction of its initial charge remains after two time constants (t = 2RC)?', '["About 0.37", "About 0.26", "Zero", "About 0.14"]', 3, 'Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC) = Q₀e^(−2) ≈ 0.135 Q₀, about 0.14 of the initial charge.', '{"0": "0.37 is the fraction left after one time constant.", "1": "0.26 subtracts 0.37 twice from 1, but decay is multiplicative, not subtractive.", "2": "Exponential decay never reaches exactly zero in a finite time."}'),
  ('PHY-0176', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 76 (§16.9)', 'A 100 μF capacitor discharges through a 10 kΩ resistor. About how long does it take for its charge to fall to half?', '["0.69 s", "1.0 s", "0.50 s", "1.44 s"]', 0, 'τ = RC = 10⁴ Ω × 100 × 10⁻⁶ F = 1.0 s. The half-time is t½ = 0.69RC = 0.69 s.', '{"1": "1.0 s is one time constant, after which 0.37 of the charge remains, not 0.5.", "2": "Halving the charge does not take exactly half a time constant.", "3": "1.44 s is RC/0.69, which inverts the factor."}'),
  ('PHY-0177', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 74 (§16.9)', 'During the discharge of a capacitor through a resistor, the current', '["stays constant until the capacitor is empty", "decreases exponentially, like the charge and voltage", "increases exponentially", "decreases linearly to zero"]', 1, 'Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC), V = V₀e^(−t/RC) and I = I₀e^(−t/RC): all three fall exponentially, steeply at first and then more slowly.', '{"0": "The current falls as the voltage driving it falls.", "2": "The current is largest at the start and then decreases.", "3": "The decay is exponential, not a straight line."}'),
  ('PHY-0178', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 75 (§16.9)', 'In the book''s water-tank model of a discharging capacitor, the rate of flow of water through the narrow pipe corresponds to', '["the charge", "the capacitance", "the current", "the resistance"]', 2, 'The water level and pressure are like the charge and voltage; the flow rate through the pipe is like the current, which is fast at first and slows as the level drops.', '{"0": "The amount of water (level) is like the charge.", "1": "The tank''s size would be like capacitance, but the flow rate is not.", "3": "The narrowness of the pipe is like resistance, not the flow rate."}'),
  ('PHY-0179', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 73 (§16.8)', 'Two capacitors discharge through resistors. Capacitor X has a smaller time constant RC than capacitor Y. Which statement is true?', '["Y discharges faster than X.", "Both discharge at the same rate.", "X never discharges.", "X discharges faster than Y."]', 3, 'A smaller time constant means a faster response, so the charge on X falls more quickly.', '{"0": "A larger time constant means slower discharge.", "1": "Different time constants mean different rates.", "2": "Every capacitor discharges through a resistor; the time constant only sets how fast."}'),
  ('PHY-0180', 'Electrostatics', 'Uses of capacitors', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 77 (§16.10)', 'In a camera flashgun, a capacitor is used to', '["store energy and then discharge rapidly, giving the large current that ionizes xenon gas in the flash tube", "keep the battery from running down", "convert light into electricity", "slow down the flash"]', 0, 'The capacitor charges from the battery, then dumps its energy quickly when the shutter is pressed. The high current ionizes the xenon, which emits a bright burst of light.', '{"1": "The capacitor draws its energy from the battery.", "2": "A flashgun produces light; it does not convert light into electricity.", "3": "Rapid discharge makes the flash short and bright, not slow."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0181', 'Electrostatics', 'Uses of capacitors', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 77 (§16.10)', 'The ''start capacitor'' in a refrigerator or air-conditioner compressor', '["stays connected all the time to cool the refrigerator", "gives the motor a boost of energy to start, and is then disconnected by a relay", "stores the refrigerant gas", "measures the temperature"]', 1, 'It acts like a ''jump start'' for the single-phase compressor motor; once the motor reaches a certain speed a relay disconnects it. A ''run capacitor'' may stay connected to improve efficiency.', '{"0": "That describes a run capacitor, not a start capacitor.", "2": "A capacitor stores electrical energy, not gas.", "3": "A capacitor is not a thermometer."}'),
  ('PHY-0182', 'Electrostatics', 'Uses of capacitors', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 77 (§16.10)', 'In the medical field, the book mentions that capacitors are used to', '["produce X-rays directly", "measure blood pressure", "store the energy for a shock that restores a normal heart rhythm in cardiac arrest", "kill bacteria by heating"]', 2, 'A capacitor stores electrical energy and releases it as a life-saving shock to the heart. Capacitors also filter noise in imaging machines and in ECG and EEG machines.', '{"0": "X-rays are produced in an X-ray tube; capacitors only help in the circuits.", "1": "Blood pressure is not measured with a capacitor''s stored energy.", "3": "The book does not mention this use."}'),
  ('PHY-0183', 'Electrostatics', 'Uses of capacitors', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 77 (§16.10)', 'Capacitors used with resistors in refrigerators and air conditioners to delay the switching on of power make use of', '["the dielectric strength of air", "the capacitance of an isolated sphere", "the energy density of a magnetic field", "the time constant RC of the circuit"]', 3, 'An RC combination takes a time set by RC to charge, so it can delay triggering the power supply and protect the device from power fluctuations.', '{"0": "The delay comes from charging time, not from the breakdown of air.", "1": "These are circuit capacitors, not isolated spheres.", "2": "A capacitor stores energy in an electric field, not a magnetic field."}'),
  ('PHY-0184', 'Alternating current', 'AC and its characteristics', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 82 (§17.1)', 'An alternating current is one that', '["varies sinusoidally and reverses its direction every half-cycle", "flows in one direction with a constant value", "flows in one direction but in pulses", "increases steadily with time"]', 0, 'AC varies as a sine (or cosine) function of time; its direction reverses after every half-cycle while the peak value stays the same in every cycle.', '{"1": "A constant, one-way current is direct current.", "2": "One-way pulses are pulsating DC, such as the output of a rectifier.", "3": "A steadily increasing current does not alternate."}'),
  ('PHY-0185', 'Alternating current', 'AC and its characteristics', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 82 (§17.1)', 'The mains supply in Pakistan has a frequency of 50 Hz. What is its time period?', '["50 ms", "20 ms", "2 ms", "0.5 s"]', 1, 'T = 1/f = 1 ÷ 50 Hz = 0.02 s = 20 ms.', '{"0": "50 ms would correspond to 20 Hz.", "2": "2 ms would correspond to 500 Hz.", "3": "0.5 s would correspond to 2 Hz."}'),
  ('PHY-0186', 'Alternating current', 'AC and its characteristics', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 82 (§17.1)', 'During one cycle of a sinusoidal alternating voltage, where does the voltage change most rapidly?', '["At the positive peak", "At the negative peak", "At the zero (crossover) points", "It changes at the same rate throughout."]', 2, 'The book notes that the changes occur most rapidly at the zero crossover points and most slowly at the peaks, where the sine curve is flat.', '{"0": "At a peak the curve is momentarily flat, so the change is slowest there.", "1": "The negative peak is also flat, so the change is slowest there too.", "3": "A sine wave''s slope changes continuously through the cycle."}'),
  ('PHY-0187', 'Alternating current', 'AC and its characteristics', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 83 (§17.1)', 'The voltage of a supply is V = 311 sin(2π × 50t) volts. What is the instantaneous voltage at t = 2.5 ms?', '["311 V", "zero", "about 156 V", "about 220 V"]', 3, 'At t = 2.5 ms, ωt = 2π × 50 × 0.0025 = π/4 rad. V = 311 sin 45° = 311 × 0.707 ≈ 220 V.', '{"0": "311 V is the peak, reached at t = 5 ms (ωt = π/2).", "1": "The voltage is zero at t = 0 and t = 10 ms, not at 2.5 ms.", "2": "156 V is 311 × 0.5, which would be at ωt = 30°."}'),
  ('PHY-0188', 'Alternating current', 'Average and peak values', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 82 (§17.1)', 'The average value of a sinusoidal alternating voltage taken over one complete cycle is', '["zero", "0.637 V₀", "0.707 V₀", "V₀"]', 0, 'The positive and negative half-cycles are mirror images, so they cancel exactly and the full-cycle average is zero. That is why the average is usually taken over a half-cycle.', '{"1": "0.637 V₀ is the average over one half-cycle (or of a full-wave rectified wave).", "2": "0.707 V₀ is the rms value.", "3": "V₀ is the peak value."}'),
  ('PHY-0189', 'Alternating current', 'Average and peak values', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 83 (§17.1)', 'The peak current of a full-wave rectified sine wave is 10 A. Its average value is about', '["7.07 A", "6.37 A", "5.00 A", "10 A"]', 1, 'For a full-wave rectified sine wave, I_av = 0.637 I₀ = 0.637 × 10 = 6.37 A.', '{"0": "7.07 A is the rms value, 0.707 I₀.", "2": "5.00 A is half the peak, which is not the average of a sine curve.", "3": "10 A is the peak, reached only for an instant."}'),
  ('PHY-0190', 'Alternating current', 'Average and peak values', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 83 (§17.1)', 'An alternating voltage has a peak value of 12 V. Its peak-to-peak value is', '["12 V", "8.5 V", "24 V", "6 V"]', 2, 'For a symmetrical sine wave, V_p-p = 2V₀ = 2 × 12 = 24 V, the distance from the positive to the negative peak.', '{"0": "12 V is the peak value, measured from zero.", "1": "8.5 V is the rms value, 0.707 × 12.", "3": "6 V is half the peak value."}'),
  ('PHY-0191', 'Alternating current', 'RMS values', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 83 (§17.1)', 'The rms (effective) value of an alternating current is', '["the largest value the current reaches", "the average current over a full cycle", "the current at the zero-crossing point", "the steady direct current that would produce the same power (heating) in a resistor"]', 3, 'The rms value is the constant current that dissipates the same power in a resistor as the alternating current. For a sine wave, I_rms = I₀/√2 = 0.707 I₀.', '{"0": "That is the peak value.", "1": "The full-cycle average of a sine wave is zero.", "2": "At a zero crossing the current is zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0192', 'Alternating current', 'RMS values', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 84 (§17.1)', 'An alternating current is I = 10 sin(314t) amperes. What are its rms value and frequency?', '["7.07 A and about 50 Hz", "10 A and 314 Hz", "7.07 A and 314 Hz", "14.1 A and about 50 Hz"]', 0, 'I_rms = I₀/√2 = 10 × 0.707 = 7.07 A. ω = 314 rad s⁻¹, so f = ω/2π = 314 ÷ 6.28 ≈ 50 Hz.', '{"1": "10 A is the peak value and 314 is ω in rad s⁻¹, not f in Hz.", "2": "314 is the angular frequency; it must be divided by 2π.", "3": "14.1 A multiplies by √2 instead of dividing."}'),
  ('PHY-0193', 'Alternating current', 'RMS values', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 84 (§17.1)', 'The household supply is quoted as 220 V. This is its rms value. Its peak value is about', '["220 V", "311 V", "156 V", "440 V"]', 1, 'V₀ = √2 × V_rms = 1.414 × 220 ≈ 311 V.', '{"0": "220 V is the rms value itself.", "2": "156 V comes from dividing by √2 instead of multiplying.", "3": "440 V is the peak-to-peak value of a 220 V peak wave, not the peak of a 220 V rms wave."}'),
  ('PHY-0194', 'Alternating current', 'Mean and maximum power', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 85 (§17.2)', 'For a sinusoidal alternating current in a resistive load, the mean power is', '["equal to the maximum power", "zero", "half the maximum power", "0.707 times the maximum power"]', 2, 'P = I²R, and the mean of I² over a cycle is I₀²/2. So P_mean = ½I₀²R = ½P_max.', '{"0": "Power reaches its maximum only for an instant twice each cycle.", "1": "A resistor dissipates power in both half-cycles, so the mean is not zero.", "3": "0.707 applies to rms current or voltage, not to power."}'),
  ('PHY-0195', 'Alternating current', 'Mean and maximum power', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 85 (§17.2)', 'An alternating voltage of peak value 100 V is applied across a 20 Ω resistor. What is the mean power dissipated?', '["500 W", "354 W", "125 W", "250 W"]', 3, 'Maximum power = V₀²/R = 100² ÷ 20 = 500 W. Mean power = ½ × 500 = 250 W (equivalently V_rms²/R = 70.7² ÷ 20).', '{"0": "500 W is the maximum (peak) power, not the mean.", "1": "354 W multiplies the peak power by 0.707 instead of 0.5.", "2": "125 W halves the power twice."}'),
  ('PHY-0196', 'Alternating current', 'Phase', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 86 (§17.3)', 'The current in a circuit is I = I₀ sin(θ + φ) while the voltage is V = V₀ sin θ, with φ positive. The current', '["leads the voltage by φ", "lags the voltage by φ", "is in phase with the voltage", "is always zero when the voltage is zero"]', 0, 'At θ = 0 the voltage is zero but the current is already I₀ sin φ; the current reached zero earlier by angle φ, so it leads the voltage by φ.', '{"1": "Lagging would be I = I₀ sin(θ − φ).", "2": "In phase needs φ = 0.", "3": "With φ ≠ 0 the current is not zero when the voltage is zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0197', 'Alternating current', 'Phase', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 87 (§17.3)', 'In the vector (phasor) representation of an alternating quantity, the rotating vector', '["rotates clockwise at twice the frequency", "rotates anticlockwise with the same angular frequency as the alternating quantity", "is fixed and does not rotate", "has a length equal to the frequency"]', 1, 'The vector''s length represents the peak (or rms) value, and it rotates anticlockwise with angular frequency ω, the same as the alternating quantity.', '{"0": "The book''s convention is anticlockwise rotation at ω.", "2": "The vector rotates; its projection gives the instantaneous value.", "3": "Its length represents the peak or rms value, not the frequency."}'),
  ('PHY-0198', 'Alternating current', 'AC through a resistor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 88 (§17.5)', 'In a purely resistive AC circuit, the current and voltage are', '["90° out of phase, with current leading", "90° out of phase, with current lagging", "in phase", "180° out of phase"]', 2, 'V = V₀ sin ωt and I = (V₀/R) sin ωt reach their maximum and minimum values together, so they are in phase.', '{"0": "Current leads voltage by 90° in a pure capacitor.", "1": "Current lags voltage by 90° in a pure inductor.", "3": "A resistor does not reverse the current relative to the voltage."}'),
  ('PHY-0199', 'Alternating current', 'AC through an inductor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 90 (§17.6)', 'In a purely inductive AC circuit, the current', '["leads the voltage by 90°", "is in phase with the voltage", "lags behind the voltage by 180°", "lags behind the voltage by 90°"]', 3, 'With I = I₀ sin ωt, the applied voltage is V = ωLI₀ cos ωt = V₀ sin(ωt + π/2). So the voltage leads and the current lags by 90°.', '{"0": "Current leads voltage in a capacitor, not an inductor.", "1": "Current and voltage are in phase only in a resistor.", "2": "The phase difference in a pure inductor is 90°, not 180°."}'),
  ('PHY-0200', 'Alternating current', 'AC through an inductor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 91 (§17.6)', 'What is the inductive reactance of a 0.20 H inductor at 50 Hz?', '["about 63 Ω", "10 Ω", "about 0.016 Ω", "about 31 Ω"]', 0, 'X_L = 2πfL = 2 × 3.14 × 50 × 0.20 ≈ 62.8 Ω.', '{"1": "10 Ω is f × L; the factor 2π is missing.", "2": "0.016 Ω is 1/(2πfL), the formula for a capacitor''s reactance in the wrong place.", "3": "31 Ω is πfL; the factor 2 is missing."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0201', 'Alternating current', 'AC through an inductor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 91 (§17.6)', 'The inductive reactance of a coil', '["is infinite for DC and decreases with frequency", "is zero for DC and increases in proportion to the frequency", "does not depend on frequency", "is the same as the coil''s resistance"]', 1, 'X_L = 2πfL. For DC (f = 0) X_L = 0, and X_L grows as f increases, so an inductor passes DC easily but opposes high-frequency AC.', '{"0": "That describes a capacitor''s reactance.", "2": "X_L is directly proportional to f.", "3": "Reactance comes from inductance, not from the wire''s resistance."}'),
  ('PHY-0202', 'Alternating current', 'AC through an inductor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 91 (§17.6)', 'The average power dissipated in a pure inductor connected to an AC supply is', '["equal to V_rms × I_rms", "half of V₀I₀", "zero, because the energy stored in its magnetic field is returned to the source each cycle", "maximum at resonance"]', 2, 'With a 90° phase difference, energy taken from the source to build the magnetic field is returned when the field collapses, so the net power per cycle is zero.', '{"0": "V_rms × I_rms is the apparent power; the true power is V_rms I_rms cos 90° = 0.", "1": "½V₀I₀ is the mean power for a resistor, where V and I are in phase.", "3": "A pure inductor dissipates no power at any frequency."}'),
  ('PHY-0203', 'Alternating current', 'AC through an inductor', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 91 (§17.6 (Example))', 'An rms current of 20 mA at 100 Hz flows through a 0.50 H coil of negligible resistance. What is the rms voltage across the coil?', '["about 1.0 V", "about 314 V", "about 6.4 × 10⁻⁵ V", "about 6.3 V"]', 3, 'X_L = 2πfL = 2 × 3.14 × 100 × 0.50 ≈ 314 Ω. V_rms = I_rms X_L = 0.020 × 314 ≈ 6.3 V.', '{"0": "1.0 V is I × f × L (0.02 × 100 × 0.5), missing the factor 2π.", "1": "314 is the reactance in ohms, not the voltage.", "2": "6.4 × 10⁻⁵ V divides the current by the reactance instead of multiplying."}'),
  ('PHY-0204', 'Alternating current', 'Choke', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 92 (§17.7)', 'Why is a choke coil preferred to a resistor for limiting alternating current?', '["Its resistance is very small, so its power factor is nearly zero and it wastes almost no power as heat.", "It has a very large resistance that converts the current into heat.", "It blocks DC completely.", "It changes AC into DC."]', 0, 'A choke has a large inductance (iron core) but little resistance. The phase difference is nearly 90°, so cos θ ≈ 0 and almost no power is dissipated, unlike in a resistor.', '{"1": "A resistor limits current by turning energy into heat; a choke avoids this.", "2": "An inductor passes DC easily; it opposes changing current.", "3": "Converting AC to DC is rectification, done by diodes."}'),
  ('PHY-0205', 'Alternating current', 'Choke', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 92 (§17.7)', 'A choke is made of', '["a few turns of thin wire on a plastic core", "many turns of thick copper wire wound on a laminated soft iron core", "two parallel metal plates with a dielectric", "a semiconductor PN junction"]', 1, 'The book describes a choke as a coil of thick copper wire wound closely in many turns over a laminated soft iron core, which gives a high inductance.', '{"0": "A few turns on a non-magnetic core would give a small inductance.", "2": "Parallel plates with a dielectric form a capacitor.", "3": "A PN junction is a diode."}'),
  ('PHY-0206', 'Alternating current', 'AC through a capacitor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 93 (§17.8)', 'In a purely capacitive AC circuit, the current', '["lags the voltage by 90°", "is in phase with the voltage", "leads the voltage by 90°", "is zero at all times"]', 2, 'With V = V₀ sin ωt, the charge q = CV, and the current I = ωCV₀ cos ωt = I₀ sin(ωt + π/2). The current leads by 90°.', '{"0": "Current lags voltage in an inductor.", "1": "Current and voltage are in phase only in a resistor.", "3": "Charge flows on and off the plates, so an alternating current does flow."}'),
  ('PHY-0207', 'Alternating current', 'AC through a capacitor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 94 (§17.8)', 'What is the capacitive reactance of a 50 μF capacitor at 50 Hz?', '["about 0.016 Ω", "about 127 Ω", "400 Ω", "about 64 Ω"]', 3, 'X_C = 1/(2πfC) = 1 ÷ (2 × 3.14 × 50 × 50 × 10⁻⁶) ≈ 63.7 Ω.', '{"0": "0.016 is 2πfC, the reciprocal of the reactance.", "1": "127 Ω is 1/(πfC); the factor 2 is missing.", "2": "400 Ω is 1/(fC); the factor 2π is missing."}'),
  ('PHY-0208', 'Alternating current', 'AC through a capacitor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 94 (§17.8)', 'As the frequency of the AC supply connected to a capacitor is increased, its capacitive reactance', '["decreases, so the current increases", "increases, so the current decreases", "stays the same", "becomes infinite"]', 0, 'X_C = 1/(2πfC) is inversely proportional to f. Higher frequency means lower reactance and a larger current.', '{"1": "That is how an inductor behaves.", "2": "X_C depends on frequency.", "3": "X_C is infinite only for DC (f = 0)."}'),
  ('PHY-0209', 'Alternating current', 'AC through a capacitor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 92 (§17.7 (So you know?))', 'Which statement about a capacitor in a circuit is correct?', '["It allows DC but blocks AC.", "It blocks DC but allows AC to pass.", "It blocks both AC and DC completely.", "It allows both AC and DC equally."]', 1, 'For DC, f = 0 so X_C = 1/(2πfC) is infinite and no steady current flows. With AC the plates charge and discharge, so current flows.', '{"0": "That describes an inductor (choke).", "2": "AC does flow through a capacitor circuit.", "3": "A capacitor''s reactance is very different for DC and AC."}'),
  ('PHY-0210', 'Alternating current', 'Impedance', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 94 (§17.9)', 'An AC circuit draws an rms current of 4.0 A from a 220 V rms supply. Its impedance is', '["880 Ω", "0.018 Ω", "55 Ω", "78 Ω"]', 2, 'Z = V_rms/I_rms = 220 ÷ 4.0 = 55 Ω.', '{"0": "880 is V × I, which is power-like, not impedance.", "1": "0.018 is I/V, the reciprocal (admittance).", "3": "78 Ω uses the peak voltage (311 V) with the rms current."}'),
  ('PHY-0211', 'Alternating current', 'RC series circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 95 (§17.10)', 'A 30 Ω resistor and a capacitor of reactance 40 Ω are in series. The impedance of the circuit is', '["70 Ω", "10 Ω", "24 Ω", "50 Ω"]', 3, 'The capacitor''s voltage is 90° out of phase with the resistor''s, so they add as vectors: Z = √(R² + X_C²) = √(900 + 1600) = 50 Ω.', '{"0": "70 Ω adds R and X_C directly, ignoring the 90° phase difference.", "1": "10 Ω subtracts them.", "2": "24 Ω is RX_C/(R + X_C), a parallel-type formula."}'),
  ('PHY-0212', 'Alternating current', 'RC series circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 96 (§17.10)', 'In an RC series circuit with R = X_C, the current', '["leads the applied voltage by 45°", "lags the applied voltage by 45°", "leads the applied voltage by 90°", "is in phase with the applied voltage"]', 0, 'tan θ = X_C/R = 1, so θ = 45°. In a capacitive circuit the current leads the voltage.', '{"1": "Current lags in an inductive (RL) circuit.", "2": "A 90° lead happens only in a pure capacitor (R = 0).", "3": "In phase would need X_C = 0."}'),
  ('PHY-0213', 'Alternating current', 'RL series circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 97 (§17.11)', 'A 6 Ω resistor and an inductor of reactance 8 Ω are in series. What are the impedance and the phase relation between current and voltage?', '["14 Ω, current leads voltage by about 53°", "10 Ω, current lags voltage by about 53°", "10 Ω, current leads voltage by about 37°", "2 Ω, current in phase with voltage"]', 1, 'Z = √(R² + X_L²) = √(36 + 64) = 10 Ω. tan θ = X_L/R = 8/6, θ ≈ 53°. In an RL circuit the current lags the voltage.', '{"0": "14 Ω adds R and X_L directly; and current lags in an inductive circuit.", "2": "Current lags, not leads, in an RL circuit; and tan θ = X_L/R gives 53°, not 37°.", "3": "2 Ω subtracts R and X_L, and the circuit is not purely resistive."}'),
  ('PHY-0214', 'Alternating current', 'Power in AC circuits', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 98 (§17.11)', 'A circuit draws 5.0 A rms from a 220 V rms supply with a power factor of 0.80. What power does it consume?', '["1100 W", "1375 W", "880 W", "176 W"]', 2, 'P = V I cos θ = 220 × 5.0 × 0.80 = 880 W.', '{"0": "1100 W is V × I, the apparent power, without the power factor.", "1": "1375 W divides by the power factor instead of multiplying.", "3": "176 W multiplies V by the power factor but leaves out the current."}'),
  ('PHY-0215', 'Alternating current', 'Power in AC circuits', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 98 (§17.11)', 'In the expression P = VI cos θ for an AC circuit, cos θ is called the', '["phase constant", "quality factor", "reactance", "power factor"]', 3, 'cos θ, where θ is the phase angle between voltage and current, is the power factor. It is 1 for a resistor and 0 for a pure inductor or capacitor.', '{"0": "The phase constant is θ itself (or φ), not its cosine.", "1": "The quality factor measures the sharpness of resonance.", "2": "Reactance is an opposition in ohms, not a factor."}'),
  ('PHY-0216', 'Alternating current', 'Power in AC circuits', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 98 (§17.11)', 'A solar panel gives 500 W of DC power, and the inverter has an efficiency of 90%. How much AC power is delivered?', '["450 W", "500 W", "555 W", "50 W"]', 0, 'P_AC = inverter efficiency × P_DC = 0.90 × 500 W = 450 W.', '{"1": "Some power is always lost in the inverter.", "2": "555 W divides by the efficiency, which would create energy.", "3": "50 W is the power lost, not the power delivered."}'),
  ('PHY-0217', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 100 (§17.12 (Example))', 'In an RLC series circuit, the rms voltages across R, L and C are 30 V, 60 V and 20 V. What is the rms voltage of the source?', '["110 V", "50 V", "70 V", "90 V"]', 1, 'V_L and V_C are opposite in phase, so their net is 60 − 20 = 40 V, which is 90° from V_R. V = √(30² + 40²) = 50 V.', '{"0": "110 V adds all three directly, ignoring the phases.", "2": "70 V adds V_R and the net reactive voltage directly.", "3": "90 V adds V_R and V_L and ignores V_C."}'),
  ('PHY-0218', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 99 (§17.12)', 'At resonance in an RLC series circuit,', '["X_L = X_C, the impedance is maximum and the current is minimum", "the current lags the voltage by 90°", "X_L = X_C, the impedance is minimum (Z = R) and the current is maximum", "the power factor is zero"]', 2, 'When X_L = X_C the reactances cancel, so Z = R (its smallest value), the current is largest, and voltage and current are in phase (power factor 1).', '{"0": "Maximum impedance and minimum current describe a parallel RLC circuit at resonance.", "1": "At resonance the phase angle is zero.", "3": "At resonance the power factor is 1."}'),
  ('PHY-0219', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 99 (§17.12)', 'What is the resonant frequency of an RLC series circuit with L = 10 mH and C = 10 μF?', '["about 3160 Hz", "about 50 Hz", "about 1.6 × 10⁻⁴ Hz", "about 503 Hz"]', 3, 'f = 1/(2π√(LC)) = 1 ÷ (2π√(10 × 10⁻³ × 10 × 10⁻⁶)) = 1 ÷ (2π × 3.16 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ 503 Hz.', '{"0": "3160 is 1/√(LC), the angular frequency ω in rad s⁻¹; divide by 2π for f.", "1": "50 Hz is the mains frequency, not this circuit''s resonant frequency.", "2": "This multiplies by 2π√(LC) instead of dividing."}'),
  ('PHY-0220', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 99 (§17.12)', 'In an RLC series circuit, both L and C are doubled. The resonant frequency', '["halves", "doubles", "stays the same", "becomes one-quarter"]', 0, 'f = 1/(2π√(LC)). LC becomes 4 times larger, √(LC) doubles, so f halves.', '{"1": "Larger L and C lower the resonant frequency.", "2": "f depends on the product LC, which has changed.", "3": "f depends on √(LC), so a factor of 4 in LC gives a factor of 2 in f."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0221', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 98 (§17.12)', 'At a frequency well above resonance, an RLC series circuit behaves mainly like', '["an RC circuit, because X_C is much larger than X_L", "an RL circuit, because X_L is much larger than X_C", "a pure resistor", "an open circuit"]', 1, 'At high frequency X_L = ωL is large and X_C = 1/ωC is small, so the inductance dominates and the circuit behaves as an RL circuit.', '{"0": "X_C dominates at low frequency, not high.", "2": "The circuit is purely resistive only at resonance.", "3": "Current still flows; the impedance is large but finite."}'),
  ('PHY-0222', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 100 (§17.12)', 'At resonance in an RLC series circuit, the voltages across the inductor and capacitor', '["are always zero", "add to give twice the applied voltage", "can each be much larger than the applied voltage, though they cancel each other", "are both equal to the applied voltage"]', 2, 'At resonance the current is large, so I X_L and I X_C can each exceed the source voltage (voltage amplification). Being opposite in phase, they cancel, and the source voltage appears across R.', '{"0": "A current flows through L and C, so their voltages are not zero.", "1": "They are 180° apart, so they cancel rather than add.", "3": "Each depends on I X_L = I X_C, which need not equal the applied voltage."}'),
  ('PHY-0223', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 100 (§17.12)', 'Series RLC circuits used at resonance in radio receivers act as', '["rectifiers that convert AC into DC", "transformers that step up the voltage", "batteries that store energy", "tuning circuits and band-pass filters that pass a chosen range of frequencies"]', 3, 'At resonance the circuit responds strongly to one band of frequencies, so it is used for tuning and as a band-pass filter.', '{"0": "Rectification needs diodes.", "1": "Transformers use mutual induction between coils.", "2": "An RLC circuit does not store energy like a battery."}'),
  ('PHY-0224', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 100 (§17.12)', 'The book compares a car''s wheel, spring and shock absorber to an RLC series circuit. The shock absorber, which dissipates energy, corresponds to the', '["resistance", "inductance", "capacitance", "AC source"]', 0, 'Damping and energy dissipation in the shock absorber are like the resistance in the circuit; the mass and spring set the resonant frequency, like L and C.', '{"1": "The mass (inertia) is the analogue of inductance.", "2": "The spring is the analogue of capacitance.", "3": "The road bumps that drive the motion are the analogue of the source."}'),
  ('PHY-0225', 'Alternating current', 'RLC parallel circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 101 (§17.13)', 'At resonance, a parallel RLC circuit has', '["minimum impedance and maximum current from the source", "maximum impedance and minimum current from the source", "zero impedance", "a power factor of zero"]', 1, 'In a parallel RLC circuit the impedance is largest at resonance, so the source current is smallest, although the branch currents through L and C can be large.', '{"0": "That describes a series RLC circuit at resonance.", "2": "The impedance is at its maximum, not zero.", "3": "At resonance the power factor is 1."}'),
  ('PHY-0226', 'Alternating current', 'RLC parallel circuit', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 101 (§17.13)', 'Admittance (Y) of an AC circuit is', '["the square of its impedance", "the sum of its resistances", "the reciprocal of its impedance", "the product of its reactances"]', 2, 'Y = 1/Z. In a parallel circuit, the admittances of the branches add.', '{"0": "Admittance is not Z².", "1": "That would be resistance in series, not admittance.", "3": "Admittance is 1/Z, not a product of reactances."}'),
  ('PHY-0227', 'Alternating current', 'RLC parallel circuit', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 102 (§17.13)', 'The book gives which household device as an application of a parallel RLC circuit at resonance?', '["A mobile phone battery", "An electric bell", "A filament bulb", "An induction cooktop"]', 3, 'In an induction cooktop the parallel RLC circuit produces a high-frequency current in the coil, which induces currents in the metal cookware and heats it.', '{"0": "A phone battery stores DC; it is not an RLC resonance device.", "1": "An electric bell works by an electromagnet and a make-and-break contact.", "2": "A filament bulb is a resistive load."}'),
  ('PHY-0228', 'Alternating current', 'Rectification', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 102 (§17.14)', 'Rectification is the process of', '["converting alternating current into direct current", "converting direct current into alternating current", "increasing the voltage of AC", "storing charge in a capacitor"]', 0, 'A rectifier uses diodes, which conduct in only one direction, to change AC into unidirectional (DC) current.', '{"1": "DC to AC conversion is done by an inverter.", "2": "Stepping up AC voltage is done by a transformer.", "3": "Storing charge is what a capacitor does."}'),
  ('PHY-0229', 'Alternating current', 'Rectification', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 103 (§17.14)', 'In a half-wave rectifier with one diode and a load resistor,', '["current flows through the load during both halves of the cycle", "current flows through the load only during one half of each input cycle", "no current ever flows through the load", "the output is a smooth, steady DC"]', 1, 'The diode is forward biased in one half-cycle (it conducts) and reverse biased in the other (it blocks), so the output is pulsating DC with gaps.', '{"0": "Both halves are used in full-wave rectification.", "2": "The diode conducts in the forward-biased half-cycle.", "3": "The output is pulsating, not smooth, without a filter."}'),
  ('PHY-0230', 'Alternating current', 'Rectification', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 104 (§17.14)', 'In a full-wave bridge rectifier, how many diodes conduct during each half-cycle of the input?', '["One", "Three", "Two", "Four"]', 2, 'The four diodes are arranged in pairs; in each half-cycle one pair is forward biased and conducts in series while the other pair is reverse biased.', '{"0": "A single diode conducting is the half-wave case.", "1": "The bridge works with pairs of diodes.", "3": "Only one pair conducts at a time; the other pair is reverse biased."}'),
  ('PHY-0231', 'Alternating current', 'Rectification', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 106 (§17.14)', 'A full-wave rectifier is connected to the 50 Hz mains. What is the frequency of the ripple in its output?', '["50 Hz", "25 Hz", "200 Hz", "100 Hz"]', 3, 'Both halves of each input cycle produce an output pulse, so there are two pulses per input cycle. The ripple frequency is 2f = 100 Hz.', '{"0": "50 Hz is the ripple frequency of a half-wave rectifier, which gives one pulse per cycle.", "1": "Rectification does not lower the frequency.", "2": "There are two pulses per cycle, not four."}'),
  ('PHY-0232', 'Alternating current', 'Rectification', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 105 (§17.14)', 'A smoothing capacitor is connected across the load of a full-wave rectifier. How does it smooth the output?', '["It charges as the rectified voltage rises and discharges through the load as it falls.", "It blocks the DC part of the output.", "It reverses the negative half-cycles.", "It increases the ripple frequency."]', 0, 'Connected in parallel with the load, the capacitor stores charge near each peak and supplies the load between peaks, filling in the dips.', '{"1": "A capacitor across the load does not block DC to the load.", "2": "The diodes, not the capacitor, deal with the negative half-cycles.", "3": "The ripple frequency is set by the rectifier (2f), not by the capacitor."}'),
  ('PHY-0233', 'Alternating current', 'Rectification', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 106 (§17.14)', 'Using V_ripple = I/(2fC), what is the ripple voltage of a 50 Hz full-wave rectifier with a 1000 μF smoothing capacitor and a load current of 0.50 A?', '["10 V", "5.0 V", "2.5 V", "0.005 V"]', 1, 'V_ripple = I/(2fC) = 0.50 ÷ (2 × 50 × 1000 × 10⁻⁶) = 0.50 ÷ 0.10 = 5.0 V.', '{"0": "10 V uses I/(fC), leaving out the factor 2 for full-wave.", "2": "2.5 V uses I/(4fC).", "3": "0.005 V treats 1000 μF as 1 F."}'),
  ('PHY-0234', 'Alternating current', 'Self-induction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 106 (§17.16)', 'Self-induction is the production of an induced emf in a coil', '["due to a steady current in a nearby coil", "when it is heated", "due to a change of current in the same coil", "only when a magnet is moved near it"]', 2, 'When the current in a coil changes, its own magnetic flux changes, and this induces an emf in the coil itself.', '{"0": "A steady current produces no change of flux; and a nearby coil gives mutual induction.", "1": "Heating does not induce an emf.", "3": "A moving magnet can induce an emf, but that is not self-induction."}'),
  ('PHY-0235', 'Alternating current', 'Self-induction', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 106 (§17.16)', 'The self-inductance of a coil can be increased by', '["reducing the number of turns", "reducing its area of cross-section", "using an air core instead of iron", "winding it on a ferromagnetic iron core"]', 3, 'L depends on the number of turns, the area of cross-section and the core. An iron core greatly increases the flux and hence the inductance compared with air.', '{"0": "Fewer turns mean less flux linkage and a smaller L.", "1": "A smaller area means less flux and a smaller L.", "2": "An air core gives a much smaller inductance than iron."}'),
  ('PHY-0236', 'Alternating current', 'Self-induction', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 107 (§17.16)', 'The current in a 0.30 H coil falls steadily from 5.0 A to 1.0 A in 0.20 s. What is the magnitude of the self-induced emf?', '["6.0 V", "1.2 V", "0.24 V", "7.5 V"]', 0, 'ε = L ΔI/Δt = 0.30 × (4.0 ÷ 0.20) = 0.30 × 20 = 6.0 V.', '{"1": "1.2 V is L × ΔI, not divided by the time.", "2": "0.24 V multiplies by the time instead of dividing.", "3": "7.5 V uses the final current (1.0 A) incorrectly; ΔI is 4.0 A."}'),
  ('PHY-0237', 'Alternating current', 'Self-induction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 107 (§17.16)', 'A coil has a self-inductance of 1 henry if', '["a current of 1 A flows when 1 V is applied", "an emf of 1 V is induced in it when its current changes at 1 A s⁻¹", "it stores 1 J of energy at 1 A", "it has 1 turn of wire"]', 1, 'L = ε/(ΔI/Δt), so 1 H = 1 V s A⁻¹: an emf of 1 V for a rate of change of current of 1 A s⁻¹.', '{"0": "1 V per 1 A is 1 ohm of resistance.", "2": "A 1 H coil carrying 1 A stores ½LI² = 0.5 J.", "3": "Inductance is not set by the number of turns alone."}'),
  ('PHY-0238', 'Alternating current', 'Energy in an inductor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 108 (§17.16)', 'How much energy is stored in the magnetic field of a 2.0 H inductor carrying a steady current of 3.0 A?', '["18 J", "6.0 J", "9.0 J", "3.0 J"]', 2, 'U = ½LI² = ½ × 2.0 × 3.0² = 9.0 J.', '{"0": "18 J leaves out the ½.", "1": "6.0 J is L × I, forgetting to square the current.", "3": "3.0 J is ½LI, forgetting to square the current."}'),
  ('PHY-0239', 'Alternating current', 'Energy in an inductor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 109 (§17.16)', 'The energy stored per unit volume in the magnetic field inside a solenoid is', '["½μ₀B²", "½ε₀E²", "μ₀nI", "B²/2μ₀"]', 3, 'Using L = μ₀n²Aℓ and B = μ₀nI in ½LI² gives U = (B²/2μ₀)(Aℓ); dividing by the volume Aℓ gives B²/2μ₀.', '{"0": "μ₀ must be in the denominator, not the numerator.", "1": "½ε₀E² is the energy density of an electric field.", "2": "μ₀nI is the magnetic field B itself, not an energy density."}'),
  ('PHY-0240', 'Alternating current', 'Mutual induction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 109 (§17.17)', 'Mutual induction is the production of an emf in one coil', '["by a changing current in a neighbouring coil", "by a steady current in the same coil", "by heating a neighbouring coil", "by a constant magnetic field"]', 0, 'When the current in the primary coil changes, its changing flux links the secondary coil and induces an emf there.', '{"1": "Change of current in the same coil is self-induction, and a steady current induces nothing.", "2": "Heating does not induce an emf.", "3": "Only a changing flux induces an emf."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0241', 'Alternating current', 'Mutual induction', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 110 (§17.17)', 'The current in a primary coil changes at 40 A s⁻¹. If the mutual inductance between the coils is 0.50 H, what emf is induced in the secondary?', '["80 V", "20 V", "0.0125 V", "40 V"]', 1, 'ε_s = M ΔI_p/Δt = 0.50 × 40 = 20 V.', '{"0": "80 V divides 40 by 0.50 instead of multiplying.", "2": "0.0125 V divides M by the rate.", "3": "40 V leaves out M."}'),
  ('PHY-0242', 'Alternating current', 'Mutual induction', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 110 (§17.17)', 'Which of these does NOT affect the mutual inductance of a pair of coils?', '["The distance between the coils", "The relative orientation of the coils", "The current flowing in the primary coil", "The permeability of the core material"]', 2, 'M depends on the numbers of turns, the areas, the core material (permeability), the distance and the orientation, but not on the current; the current only sets the induced emf through M ΔI/Δt.', '{"0": "Moving the coils apart reduces the flux linking them, so it changes M.", "1": "Turning one coil changes how much flux links it, so it changes M.", "3": "An iron core increases the flux linkage and hence M."}'),
  ('PHY-0243', 'Alternating current', 'Mutual induction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 110 (§17.17)', 'Which device works on the principle of mutual induction?', '["A diode", "A filament lamp", "A capacitor", "A transformer"]', 3, 'A transformer uses the changing flux of the primary coil to induce an emf in the secondary. The book also mentions motors, generators, pacemakers and metal detectors.', '{"0": "A diode rectifies current using a PN junction.", "1": "A lamp heats a filament by its resistance.", "2": "A capacitor stores energy in an electric field."}'),
  ('PHY-0244', 'Alternating current', 'Self-induction', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 107 (§17.16)', 'The dimensions of self-inductance (the henry) are', '["[ML²T⁻²A⁻²]", "[ML²T⁻³A⁻²]", "[MLT⁻²A⁻¹]", "[ML²T⁻²]"]', 0, '1 H = 1 V s A⁻¹ and 1 V = 1 kg m² s⁻³ A⁻¹, so 1 H = kg m² s⁻² A⁻², i.e. [ML²T⁻²A⁻²].', '{"1": "[ML²T⁻³A⁻²] is the dimension of resistance (the ohm).", "2": "[MLT⁻²A⁻¹] is the dimension of B × length (N A⁻¹), not of inductance.", "3": "[ML²T⁻²] is the dimension of energy."}'),
  ('PHY-0245', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Quantum theory of radiation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 115 (§18.1)', 'According to Max Planck''s quantum theory, energy is radiated or absorbed', '["continuously, in any amount", "in discrete packets (quanta), each of energy E = hf", "only by charged particles at rest", "in packets whose energy depends only on the intensity"]', 1, 'Planck proposed that radiation is emitted or absorbed in quanta, each of energy proportional to its frequency: E = hf, with h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.', '{"0": "Continuous emission is the classical idea Planck replaced.", "2": "Radiation comes from accelerated (oscillating) charges, not charges at rest.", "3": "The energy of each quantum depends on frequency; intensity only sets how many quanta."}'),
  ('PHY-0246', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Quantum theory of radiation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 118 (§18.2 (Example))', 'What is the energy of a photon of light of wavelength 500 nm? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J)', '["about 4.0 eV", "about 1.2 eV", "about 2.5 eV", "about 25 eV"]', 2, 'E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸) ÷ (500 × 10⁻⁹) = 3.98 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Dividing by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J eV⁻¹ gives about 2.5 eV.', '{"0": "4.0 is the energy in units of 10⁻¹⁹ J; it still has to be converted to eV.", "1": "1.2 eV is half the correct value.", "3": "25 eV has the power of ten wrong."}'),
  ('PHY-0247', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'The photon', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 115 (§18.1)', 'What is the momentum of a photon of wavelength 663 nm? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s)', '["1.0 × 10⁻³⁶ kg m s⁻¹", "4.4 × 10⁻⁴⁰ kg m s⁻¹", "3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ kg m s⁻¹", "1.0 × 10⁻²⁷ kg m s⁻¹"]', 3, 'p = h/λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ 663 × 10⁻⁹ = 1.0 × 10⁻²⁷ kg m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This treats 663 nm as 663 m.", "1": "4.4 × 10⁻⁴⁰ multiplies h by λ instead of dividing.", "2": "3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ is close to the photon''s energy in joules (hc/λ), not its momentum."}'),
  ('PHY-0248', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'The photon', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 115 (§18.1)', 'A photon has no rest mass. Its momentum p is related to its energy E by', '["p = E/c", "p = Ec", "p = E/c²", "p = 0"]', 0, 'Using E = mc² and p = mc for a photon gives pc = E, so p = E/c = hf/c = h/λ.', '{"1": "Ec has the wrong units for momentum.", "2": "E/c² is a mass, not a momentum.", "3": "A photon carries momentum even though its rest mass is zero; this is why light can exert a force."}'),
  ('PHY-0249', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'The photon', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 115 (§18.1)', 'Why are quantum (particle) effects not noticed for a mass oscillating on a spring in everyday life?', '["Large objects have no energy.", "The energy steps hf are far too small to be detected for such large systems.", "Planck''s constant is zero for large objects.", "Springs do not obey the quantum theory."]', 1, 'The book notes quantum theory applies to any system, but for everyday objects the energy steps are far too small to detect; quantum effects matter at the atomic scale.', '{"0": "An oscillating mass certainly has energy.", "2": "h is a universal constant; it is the same for every system.", "3": "Quantum theory applies to all systems; the effects are just too small to see."}'),
  ('PHY-0250', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 115 (§18.2)', 'The photoelectric effect is the', '["emission of light when electrons strike a metal", "increase in resistance of a metal when light falls on it", "emission of electrons from a metal surface when light of suitable frequency falls on it", "bending of light around a metal edge"]', 2, 'When light of suitable frequency (or wavelength) falls on a metal, electrons called photoelectrons are emitted.', '{"0": "Light emitted when electrons hit a target is the reverse process (as in X-ray production).", "1": "Photoelectric emission is about electrons leaving the surface, not resistance.", "3": "Bending around edges is diffraction."}'),
  ('PHY-0251', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 116 (§18.2)', 'In a photoelectric experiment, the photocurrent becomes zero when the stopping potential is 2.0 V. What is the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons?', '["2.0 J", "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J", "8.0 × 10⁻²⁰ J", "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"]', 3, '(K.E.)max = eV₀ = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C × 2.0 V = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, i.e. 2.0 eV.', '{"0": "2.0 is the energy in electronvolts, not joules.", "1": "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is 1 eV, not 2 eV.", "2": "8.0 × 10⁻²⁰ J divides e by the voltage instead of multiplying."}'),
  ('PHY-0252', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 116 (§18.2)', 'Light of a fixed frequency above the threshold falls on a metal. If the intensity of the light is doubled,', '["the photocurrent increases but the stopping potential stays the same", "the stopping potential doubles", "the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons doubles", "no electrons are emitted"]', 0, 'More intense light means more photons per second, so more electrons are emitted and the current rises. Each photon still has energy hf, so (K.E.)max and the stopping potential are unchanged.', '{"1": "The stopping potential depends on frequency, not intensity.", "2": "(K.E.)max = hf − φ does not depend on intensity.", "3": "The frequency is above threshold, so emission continues."}'),
  ('PHY-0253', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 116 (§18.2)', 'Light of constant intensity but increasing frequency (above the threshold) is shone on a metal. The stopping potential', '["decreases", "increases, because the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons increases", "stays the same", "becomes zero"]', 1, 'eV₀ = (K.E.)max = hf − φ, so a higher frequency gives a larger maximum kinetic energy and a larger stopping potential.', '{"0": "Higher-frequency photons carry more energy, so the stopping potential rises.", "2": "The stopping potential depends on frequency.", "3": "Above threshold, (K.E.)max is positive, so V₀ is not zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0254', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 117 (§18.2)', 'Blue light causes photoelectric emission from sodium, but even very intense red light does not. This shows that', '["red light carries more energy per photon than blue light", "sodium emits electrons only in the dark", "emission needs light above a threshold frequency, whatever its intensity", "intensity alone decides whether electrons are emitted"]', 2, 'Below the threshold frequency f₀ no electrons are emitted, however intense the light. Red light is below sodium''s threshold; blue light is above it.', '{"0": "Red light has a lower frequency, so each of its photons has less energy than a blue photon.", "1": "Emission happens under suitable light, not in the dark.", "3": "Intense red light gives no emission, so intensity alone does not decide."}'),
  ('PHY-0255', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 117 (§18.2)', 'Which observation of the photoelectric effect could NOT be explained by the classical wave theory of light?', '["More intense light gives a larger photocurrent.", "Light carries energy.", "Light travels in straight lines in a uniform medium.", "The maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons does not increase with intensity."]', 3, 'Wave theory predicted that brighter light should give electrons more energy and that any frequency would work if you waited. Neither is observed; this needs the photon model.', '{"0": "A larger current with more intense light is consistent with both theories.", "1": "Wave theory also says light carries energy.", "2": "Rectilinear propagation is not one of the photoelectric observations."}'),
  ('PHY-0256', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 117 (§18.2)', 'Photons of energy 5.0 eV fall on a metal of work function 2.3 eV. What is the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons?', '["2.7 eV", "7.3 eV", "5.0 eV", "2.3 eV"]', 0, 'Einstein''s equation: (K.E.)max = hf − φ = 5.0 − 2.3 = 2.7 eV.', '{"1": "7.3 eV adds the work function instead of subtracting it.", "2": "5.0 eV ignores the energy needed to escape the metal.", "3": "2.3 eV is the work function, the energy used up in escaping."}'),
  ('PHY-0257', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 117 (§18.2)', 'The work function of a metal is 3.3 eV. What is its threshold frequency? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J)', '["about 5.0 × 10³³ Hz", "about 8.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz", "about 1.3 × 10⁻¹⁵ Hz", "about 3.8 × 10⁻⁷ Hz"]', 1, 'f₀ = φ/h = (3.3 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) ÷ 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ = 5.28 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ≈ 8.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.', '{"0": "5.0 × 10³³ Hz treats 3.3 eV as 3.3 J; the work function must be converted to joules.", "2": "1.3 × 10⁻¹⁵ is h/φ, which inverts the formula.", "3": "3.8 × 10⁻⁷ is hc/φ, the threshold wavelength in metres, not a frequency."}'),
  ('PHY-0258', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 117 (§18.2)', 'What is the threshold wavelength for a metal of work function 2.0 eV? (hc = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ J m, 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J)', '["about 310 nm", "about 1240 nm", "about 620 nm", "about 400 nm"]', 2, 'λ₀ = hc/φ = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ (2.0 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) ≈ 6.2 × 10⁻⁷ m ≈ 620 nm.', '{"0": "310 nm would be the threshold for a 4.0 eV work function.", "1": "1240 nm would be for a 1.0 eV work function.", "3": "400 nm would need a work function of about 3.1 eV."}'),
  ('PHY-0259', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 117 (§18.2)', 'A graph of the maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons against the frequency of the incident light is a straight line. Its slope equals', '["the work function φ", "the threshold frequency f₀", "the charge on an electron", "Planck''s constant h"]', 3, '(K.E.)max = hf − φ has the form y = mx + c, so the slope is h. The line cuts the frequency axis at the threshold frequency f₀.', '{"0": "φ appears as the (negative) intercept, not the slope.", "1": "f₀ is where the line meets the frequency axis.", "2": "The slope has units of J s, which is h, not a charge."}'),
  ('PHY-0260', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 118 (§18.2)', 'Not all photoelectrons leave the metal with the maximum kinetic energy given by Einstein''s equation, because', '["some lose energy in collisions with atoms before they escape", "some absorb two photons", "some photons have more energy than others in monochromatic light", "the work function changes from moment to moment"]', 0, 'Electrons from just below the surface lose some energy in atomic collisions on the way out. Only those that escape with the full surplus energy have (K.E.)max.', '{"1": "Each electron absorbs a single photon.", "2": "In monochromatic light every photon has the same energy hf.", "3": "The work function is a fixed property of the metal."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0261', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 118 (§18.2)', 'Albert Einstein received the 1921 Nobel Prize in physics for', '["the theory of relativity", "his explanation of the photoelectric effect", "discovering the electron", "explaining Compton scattering"]', 1, 'The book states that Einstein was awarded the 1921 Nobel Prize for explaining the photoelectric effect.', '{"0": "The Nobel citation was for the photoelectric effect, not relativity.", "2": "The electron was discovered by J. J. Thomson.", "3": "Compton received the 1927 Nobel Prize for the Compton effect."}'),
  ('PHY-0262', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Compton effect', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 119 (§18.3)', 'In the Compton effect, X-rays scattered by loosely bound electrons in graphite are found to have', '["a shorter wavelength than the incident X-rays", "exactly the same wavelength", "a longer wavelength than the incident X-rays", "no wavelength at all"]', 2, 'The X-ray photon gives some energy to the recoiling electron, so the scattered photon has less energy and a longer wavelength.', '{"0": "A shorter wavelength would mean the photon gained energy.", "1": "Compton found a definite increase in wavelength.", "3": "Scattered X-rays are still waves with a wavelength."}'),
  ('PHY-0263', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Compton effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 120 (§18.3)', 'The Compton shift is Δλ = (h/m₀c)(1 − cos θ). At what scattering angle is the shift equal to the Compton wavelength h/m₀c (2.43 pm)?', '["0°", "180°", "45°", "90°"]', 3, 'At θ = 90°, cos θ = 0, so Δλ = h/m₀c = 2.43 × 10⁻¹² m.', '{"0": "At 0°, 1 − cos θ = 0, so there is no shift.", "1": "At 180°, 1 − cos θ = 2, so the shift is 2h/m₀c = 4.86 pm.", "2": "At 45°, 1 − cos θ ≈ 0.29, so the shift is about 0.71 pm."}'),
  ('PHY-0264', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Compton effect', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 120 (§18.3)', 'What is the largest possible Compton shift for X-rays scattered by free electrons? (h/m₀c = 2.43 pm)', '["4.86 pm, at θ = 180°", "2.43 pm, at θ = 90°", "1.21 pm, at θ = 60°", "zero, at θ = 0°"]', 0, 'Δλ = (h/m₀c)(1 − cos θ) is largest when cos θ = −1 (backscattering, θ = 180°): Δλ = 2 × 2.43 = 4.86 pm.', '{"1": "2.43 pm at 90° is not the maximum; the photon can be scattered further back.", "2": "1.21 pm at 60° is smaller still.", "3": "Zero shift is the smallest, not the largest."}'),
  ('PHY-0265', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Compton effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 120 (§18.3)', 'Compton treated the scattering of an X-ray photon by an electron like a collision of two billiard balls, in which', '["only energy is conserved", "both energy and momentum are conserved", "only momentum is conserved", "neither energy nor momentum is conserved"]', 1, 'Compton''s analysis uses conservation of energy and of both components of momentum. Agreement with experiment confirmed the particle-like behaviour of X-rays.', '{"0": "Momentum is also conserved, in both x and y directions.", "2": "Energy is also conserved; the electron gains the energy the photon loses.", "3": "Both conservation laws hold and give the Compton formula."}'),
  ('PHY-0266', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Pair production and annihilation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 121 (§18.3)', 'When an electron and a positron annihilate each other at rest, they produce', '["one γ-ray photon of 1.02 MeV", "a neutron", "two γ-ray photons, each of about 0.51 MeV", "an α-particle"]', 2, 'e⁺ + e⁻ → γ + γ. Two photons are needed to conserve momentum; each carries the rest-mass energy of one particle, about 0.51 MeV.', '{"0": "A single photon could not conserve momentum when the pair is at rest.", "1": "Annihilation converts the mass into radiation, not into a neutron.", "3": "Annihilation produces photons, not α-particles."}'),
  ('PHY-0267', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Pair production and annihilation', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 122 (§18.4 (Example))', 'Each photon from electron–positron annihilation at rest has an energy of 0.51 MeV. Its wavelength is about (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)', '["2.4 × 10⁻⁹ m", "1.2 × 10⁻¹² m", "4.9 × 10⁻¹² m", "2.4 × 10⁻¹² m"]', 3, 'E = 0.51 × 10⁶ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 8.16 × 10⁻¹⁴ J. λ = hc/E = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 8.16 × 10⁻¹⁴ ≈ 2.4 × 10⁻¹² m (2.43 pm, equal to the Compton wavelength).', '{"0": "2.4 × 10⁻⁹ m treats MeV as keV.", "1": "1.2 × 10⁻¹² m would be for a 1.02 MeV photon.", "2": "4.9 × 10⁻¹² m would be for a 0.255 MeV photon."}'),
  ('PHY-0268', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Pair production and annihilation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 121 (§18.3)', 'In pair production, a high-energy γ-ray photon', '["changes into an electron and a positron", "changes into two protons", "splits into two lower-energy photons", "is absorbed by a nucleus, which then emits an α-particle"]', 0, 'In pair production the photon''s energy becomes the rest-mass energy and kinetic energy of an electron–positron pair (E = mc²). It is the reverse of annihilation.', '{"1": "Two protons would need far more energy, and charge would not be conserved.", "2": "Splitting into photons is not pair production.", "3": "That describes a nuclear reaction, not pair production."}'),
  ('PHY-0269', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave nature of particles', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 121 (§18.4)', 'In 1924, Louis de Broglie proposed that a particle of mass m moving with speed v has a wavelength', '["λ = mv/h", "λ = h/mv", "λ = hmv", "λ = h/m"]', 1, 'de Broglie applied the photon relation p = h/λ to particles: λ = h/p = h/mv.', '{"0": "This inverts the relation.", "2": "Multiplying h by momentum does not give a length.", "3": "Speed must be included; λ depends on momentum, not on mass alone."}'),
  ('PHY-0270', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave nature of particles', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 121 (§18.4)', 'What is the de Broglie wavelength of an electron moving at 2.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, m = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg)', '["about 7.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m", "about 3.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ m", "about 3.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ m", "about 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ m"]', 2, 'λ = h/mv = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ (9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 2.0 × 10⁶) = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ 1.82 × 10⁻²⁴ ≈ 3.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.', '{"0": "7.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m is for an electron at 1.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ (the book''s example).", "1": "3.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ m has the power of ten wrong.", "3": "1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ m would be for a speed of 4.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹."}'),
  ('PHY-0271', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave nature of particles', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 121 (§18.4)', 'We do not observe diffraction of a flying rifle bullet because', '["it has no momentum", "bullets are not made of atoms", "its wavelength is far too large", "its de Broglie wavelength is far too small to detect"]', 3, 'For a 20 g bullet at 330 m s⁻¹, λ = h/mv is about 10⁻³⁴ m, far too small for any interference or diffraction to be noticed.', '{"0": "A moving bullet has a large momentum; that is why λ is tiny.", "1": "Bullets are made of atoms; the issue is the size of λ.", "2": "A large mass gives a tiny, not a large, wavelength."}'),
  ('PHY-0272', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave nature of particles', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 122 (§18.4)', 'An electron is accelerated from rest through 150 V. Its de Broglie wavelength is about (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, m = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg, e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)', '["1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m", "1.0 × 10⁻⁸ m", "7.1 × 10⁻¹¹ m", "1.4 × 10⁻¹⁰ m"]', 0, 'mv = √(2meV) = √(2 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 150) ≈ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁴ kg m s⁻¹. λ = h/mv ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.', '{"1": "1.0 × 10⁻⁸ m has the power of ten wrong by 100.", "2": "7.1 × 10⁻¹¹ m is the wavelength for 300 V, not 150 V.", "3": "1.4 × 10⁻¹⁰ m is h/√(meV); the factor 2 inside the square root is missing."}'),
  ('PHY-0273', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave nature of particles', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 122 (§18.4)', 'If the accelerating voltage of an electron beam is made four times larger, the de Broglie wavelength of the electrons', '["doubles", "halves", "becomes one-quarter", "stays the same"]', 1, 'λ = h/√(2meV) ∝ 1/√V. Four times V gives √4 = 2, so λ halves.', '{"0": "Faster electrons have shorter, not longer, wavelengths.", "2": "λ depends on 1/√V, not 1/V.", "3": "λ depends on the accelerating voltage."}'),
  ('PHY-0274', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave nature of particles', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 122 (§18.4)', 'An electron and a proton have the same kinetic energy. Which has the shorter de Broglie wavelength?', '["The electron, because it has the smaller mass", "Both have the same wavelength", "The proton, because λ = h/√(2mK) and it has the larger mass", "Neither has a de Broglie wavelength"]', 2, 'λ = h/p and p = √(2mK). For equal K, the heavier proton has the larger momentum and so the shorter wavelength.', '{"0": "Smaller mass means smaller momentum at the same K, so a longer wavelength.", "1": "Different masses give different momenta at the same K.", "3": "All moving particles have a de Broglie wavelength."}'),
  ('PHY-0275', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Davisson–Germer experiment', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 122 (§18.4)', 'The Davisson–Germer experiment showed that electrons are diffracted by a nickel crystal. This confirmed', '["the particle nature of light", "that electrons have no mass", "that X-rays are electrons", "the wave nature of electrons, with the wavelength predicted by de Broglie"]', 3, 'The scattered electrons came off in peaks, like X-ray diffraction by a crystal, and the measured wavelength matched λ = h/mv.', '{"0": "This experiment is about electrons, not light.", "1": "The calculation uses the electron''s mass.", "2": "X-rays are electromagnetic waves, not electrons; electrons only behaved similarly."}'),
  ('PHY-0276', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave–particle duality', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 123 (§18.5)', 'Which pair of phenomena shows the PARTICLE nature of electromagnetic radiation?', '["The photoelectric effect and the Compton effect", "Interference and diffraction", "Polarization and interference", "Diffraction and refraction"]', 0, 'The photoelectric effect and Compton scattering need light to be treated as photons. Interference and diffraction show its wave nature.', '{"1": "Interference and diffraction show wave nature.", "2": "Polarization and interference are wave properties.", "3": "Diffraction is a wave property, and refraction is explained by waves too."}'),
  ('PHY-0277', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave–particle duality', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 123 (§18.5)', 'Bohr''s principle of complementarity states that', '["light is only a wave and matter is only made of particles", "both wave and particle aspects are needed to describe radiation and matter, but both cannot be revealed in a single experiment", "both wave and particle aspects always appear together in every experiment", "the wave and particle aspects cancel each other"]', 1, 'Both aspects are needed for a full description, but an experiment reveals one or the other, never both at the same time.', '{"0": "Duality says both radiation and matter have both aspects.", "2": "Only one aspect shows in any one experiment.", "3": "The aspects complement each other; they do not cancel."}'),
  ('PHY-0278', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Electron microscope', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 124 (§18.6)', 'An electron microscope can show much finer detail than an optical microscope because', '["electrons are larger than photons", "it uses brighter light", "the de Broglie wavelength of its fast electrons is much shorter than the wavelength of visible light", "electrons travel faster than light"]', 2, 'Resolution is limited by wavelength. Fast electrons have wavelengths far shorter than visible light, so much finer details can be resolved.', '{"0": "Size is not the point; the wavelength is.", "1": "An electron microscope does not use light at all.", "3": "Nothing with mass travels faster than light."}'),
  ('PHY-0279', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Electron microscope', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 124 (§18.6)', 'In an electron microscope, increasing the speed of the electrons', '["lengthens their wavelength and reduces the resolution", "has no effect on the resolution", "makes the electrons behave only as particles", "shortens their wavelength and increases the resolution"]', 3, 'λ = h/mv: faster electrons have shorter wavelengths, so the microscope can resolve smaller details.', '{"0": "Higher speed shortens the wavelength.", "1": "Resolution depends on wavelength, which depends on speed.", "2": "They still behave as waves; the wavelength just gets shorter."}'),
  ('PHY-0280', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Electron microscope', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 124 (§18.6)', 'In an electron microscope, the electron beam is focused by', '["electromagnetic (magnetic) lenses", "glass lenses", "concave mirrors", "prisms"]', 0, 'The condenser, objective and projector lenses of an electron microscope are electromagnetic lenses, which bend the paths of electrons.', '{"1": "Glass lenses focus light, not electrons.", "2": "Mirrors are used in optical instruments for light.", "3": "Prisms disperse light; they do not focus electrons."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0281', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 125 (§18.7)', 'In the spectrum of atomic hydrogen, the Lyman series lies in the', '["visible region", "ultraviolet region", "infrared region", "radio region"]', 1, 'The Lyman series (transitions ending on n = 1) lies in the ultraviolet. The Balmer series is visible and the Paschen series is infrared.', '{"0": "The visible lines belong to the Balmer series.", "2": "The Paschen series lies in the infrared.", "3": "Hydrogen''s main series are in the UV, visible and IR, not radio."}'),
  ('PHY-0282', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 125 (§18.7)', 'The Paschen series of hydrogen is formed by electron transitions that end on', '["n = 1", "n = 2", "n = 3", "n = 4"]', 2, 'For the Paschen series, 1/λ = R_H(1/3² − 1/n²) with n = 4, 5, 6 …, so all transitions end on n = 3; the lines are in the infrared.', '{"0": "Transitions ending on n = 1 form the Lyman series.", "1": "Transitions ending on n = 2 form the Balmer series.", "3": "Transitions ending on n = 4 form the Brackett series, which the book does not discuss."}'),
  ('PHY-0283', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 125 (§18.7)', 'Who identified, in 1885, the series of four visible lines in the spectrum of atomic hydrogen?', '["J. R. Rydberg", "Niels Bohr", "Max Planck", "J. J. Balmer"]', 3, 'The visible series in hydrogen was identified by Balmer in 1885. Rydberg later expressed the wavelengths in a general formula.', '{"0": "Rydberg gave the mathematical formula with the Rydberg constant.", "1": "Bohr explained the spectrum with his model of the atom later.", "2": "Planck introduced energy quanta."}'),
  ('PHY-0284', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 126 (§18.7)', 'The energy levels of hydrogen are E₂ = −3.40 eV and E₃ = −1.51 eV. What is the energy of the photon emitted when the electron drops from n = 3 to n = 2?', '["1.89 eV", "4.91 eV", "3.40 eV", "1.51 eV"]', 0, 'hf = E_upper − E_lower = −1.51 − (−3.40) = 1.89 eV. This is the red line of the Balmer series.', '{"1": "4.91 eV adds the magnitudes instead of taking the difference.", "2": "3.40 eV is the energy needed to ionize from n = 2.", "3": "1.51 eV is the energy needed to ionize from n = 3."}'),
  ('PHY-0285', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 126 (§18.7)', 'When white light passes through a cool gas and is then analysed, dark lines appear in the spectrum. These dark lines', '["are at wavelengths not found in the emission spectrum of the gas", "are at the same wavelengths as the bright lines in the emission spectrum of that gas", "show that the gas emits white light", "are caused by diffraction in the spectrometer"]', 1, 'The gas atoms absorb only photons whose energies match their level differences. These are exactly the wavelengths emitted when the atoms fall back, so the absorption lines match the emission lines.', '{"0": "The absorbed wavelengths are the same as the emitted ones.", "2": "Dark lines show absorption, not emission of white light.", "3": "The lines come from the gas atoms, not from the instrument."}'),
  ('PHY-0286', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 126 (§18.7 (For your information))', 'To excite a gas atom from one level to a higher level, which statement is correct according to the book?', '["Both photons and electrons must have exactly the energy difference.", "An electron must have exactly the energy difference, but a photon can have more.", "A photon must have exactly the energy difference, but an electron with kinetic energy equal to or greater than it can do the job.", "Only photons can excite atoms."]', 2, 'A photon is absorbed whole, so its energy must match E_upper − E_lower. A colliding electron can give up just part of its kinetic energy, so it only needs at least that much.', '{"0": "An electron can keep its leftover kinetic energy, so it need not match exactly.", "1": "This reverses the two cases.", "3": "Colliding electrons can also excite atoms, as in a discharge tube."}'),
  ('PHY-0287', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Uncertainty principle', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 127 (§18.8)', 'Heisenberg''s uncertainty principle states that', '["the energy of a particle can never be measured", "measuring instruments are always faulty", "position can be known exactly only if momentum is also known exactly", "the position and momentum of a particle cannot both be measured exactly at the same time"]', 3, 'Δx Δp ≈ h: the more precisely position is known, the less precisely momentum can be known, and vice versa. This comes from wave–particle duality, not from faulty instruments.', '{"0": "Energy can be measured; the limit is on knowing E and time together exactly.", "1": "The uncertainty is fundamental, not a fault of the instrument.", "2": "Knowing one more precisely makes the other less precise."}'),
  ('PHY-0288', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Uncertainty principle', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 128 (§18.8 (Example))', 'An electron is confined to a region 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m wide. Using Δx Δp ≈ h, the uncertainty in its speed is about (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, m = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg)', '["3.6 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹", "7.3 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹", "1.8 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹", "3.6 × 10³ m s⁻¹"]', 0, 'Δv = h/(mΔx) = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ (9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁰) ≈ 3.6 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "7.3 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ is for a region 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m wide (the book''s example).", "2": "1.8 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ would be for a region 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m wide.", "3": "3.6 × 10³ m s⁻¹ has the power of ten wrong."}'),
  ('PHY-0289', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Uncertainty principle', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 128 (§18.8)', 'An excited atom stays in a state for about 1.0 × 10⁻⁹ s. Using ΔE Δt ≈ h, the uncertainty in the energy of the emitted photon is about (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s)', '["6.6 × 10⁻⁴³ J", "6.6 × 10⁻²⁵ J", "1.5 × 10²⁴ J", "6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J"]', 1, 'ΔE ≈ h/Δt = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻⁹ ≈ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁵ J.', '{"0": "6.6 × 10⁻⁴³ J multiplies h by Δt instead of dividing.", "2": "1.5 × 10²⁴ J is Δt/h, the reciprocal.", "3": "6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J is just h, ignoring Δt."}'),
  ('PHY-0290', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Uncertainty principle', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 127 (§18.8)', 'Why is the uncertainty principle unimportant for a flying tennis ball but very important for an electron?', '["Tennis balls have no momentum.", "The uncertainty principle applies only to charged particles.", "A photon hitting the ball hardly changes its motion, but one photon can drastically change an electron''s motion.", "Electrons have no position."]', 2, 'The uncertainty is negligible for large objects, but for tiny particles like electrons the act of observing with light changes their momentum a lot.', '{"0": "A flying tennis ball has a large momentum.", "1": "The principle applies to all particles, charged or not.", "3": "Electrons do have positions; they just cannot be known together with momentum exactly."}'),
  ('PHY-0291', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'The nucleus', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 134 (Introduction)', 'How many neutrons are there in a nucleus of uranium-235 (²³⁵₉₂U)?', '["92", "235", "327", "143"]', 3, 'The number of neutrons N = A − Z = 235 − 92 = 143.', '{"0": "92 is the atomic number Z, the number of protons.", "1": "235 is the mass number A, the total number of nucleons.", "2": "327 adds A and Z instead of subtracting."}'),
  ('PHY-0292', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'The nucleus', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 134 (Introduction)', 'The neutron, predicted by Rutherford in 1920, was discovered in 1932 by', '["James Chadwick", "Ernest Rutherford", "Otto Hahn", "J. J. Thomson"]', 0, 'Rutherford predicted an uncharged nuclear particle; James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932.', '{"1": "Rutherford predicted it and discovered the nucleus, but did not discover the neutron.", "2": "Otto Hahn (with Strassmann) discovered nuclear fission.", "3": "J. J. Thomson discovered the electron."}'),
  ('PHY-0293', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Mass defect and binding energy', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 134 (§19.1)', 'The mass defect of a nucleus is', '["the mass of the electrons around the nucleus", "the difference between the total mass of its separate protons and neutrons and the mass of the nucleus", "the mass lost when the nucleus emits an α-particle", "the mass of the nucleus divided by its mass number"]', 1, 'Δm = Zm_p + Nm_n − M_nucleus. The nucleus is always lighter than its separate nucleons; this missing mass corresponds to the binding energy.', '{"0": "Electrons are outside the nucleus and are not part of the definition.", "2": "The mass defect concerns forming the nucleus from nucleons, not α-decay.", "3": "That is an average mass per nucleon, not the mass defect."}'),
  ('PHY-0294', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Mass defect and binding energy', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 134 (§19.1)', 'The binding energy of a nucleus is', '["the energy needed to remove all the electrons from the atom", "the kinetic energy of the nucleus", "the energy needed to break the nucleus into its separate protons and neutrons", "the energy released when an electron joins the atom"]', 2, 'Binding energy is the work needed to separate a nucleus into its nucleons. It equals the mass defect times c²: B.E. = Δm c².', '{"0": "Removing electrons involves ionization energies, which are far smaller.", "1": "Binding energy is not motion energy of the nucleus as a whole.", "3": "That is an atomic (electron) process, not a nuclear one."}'),
  ('PHY-0295', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Mass defect and binding energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 134 (§19.1)', 'The mass defect of a nucleus is 1.0 × 10⁻²⁹ kg. What is its binding energy? (c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, 1 MeV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ J)', '["about 9.0 MeV", "about 1.9 MeV", "about 56 MeV", "about 5.6 MeV"]', 3, 'B.E. = Δm c² = 1.0 × 10⁻²⁹ × (3 × 10⁸)² = 9.0 × 10⁻¹³ J. In MeV: 9.0 × 10⁻¹³ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ ≈ 5.6 MeV.', '{"0": "9.0 is the energy in units of 10⁻¹³ J; it still has to be converted to MeV.", "1": "1.9 MeV uses c instead of c² (and then rescales).", "2": "56 MeV has the power of ten wrong."}'),
  ('PHY-0296', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Mass-energy equivalence', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 134 (§19.1)', 'If 1.0 g of mass were completely converted into energy, how much energy would be released? (c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)', '["9.0 × 10¹³ J", "9.0 × 10¹⁶ J", "3.0 × 10⁵ J", "3.0 × 10⁸ J"]', 0, 'E = mc² = 1.0 × 10⁻³ kg × (3 × 10⁸)² = 9.0 × 10¹³ J.', '{"1": "9.0 × 10¹⁶ J is for 1 kg, not 1 g.", "2": "3.0 × 10⁵ J uses c instead of c².", "3": "3.0 × 10⁸ J is c × 1 kg, not mc²."}'),
  ('PHY-0297', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Mass defect and binding energy', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 135 (§19.1)', 'One atomic mass unit (1 u) is defined as', '["the mass of one hydrogen atom exactly", "one-twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom, about 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg", "the mass of one electron", "one-sixteenth of the mass of an oxygen-16 atom, about 1.66 × 10⁻²⁴ kg"]', 1, '1 u = 1/12 of the mass of a ¹²C atom = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg (1.66 × 10⁻²⁴ g).', '{"0": "A hydrogen atom is about 1.008 u, not exactly 1 u.", "2": "An electron is only about 1/1836 of a proton''s mass.", "3": "The standard is carbon-12, and 1.66 × 10⁻²⁴ is the value in grams, not kilograms."}'),
  ('PHY-0298', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Binding energy per nucleon', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 136 (§19.1)', 'Which nucleus has the highest binding energy per nucleon (about 8.7 MeV) and so is the most stable?', '["Uranium-235", "Helium-4", "Iron-56", "Hydrogen-1"]', 2, 'The binding energy per nucleon curve peaks at about 8.7 MeV near iron-56, the most stable nuclide.', '{"0": "Uranium is a heavy nucleus, about 7.6 MeV per nucleon, lower than iron.", "1": "Helium-4 is unusually stable for a light nucleus, but still below iron.", "3": "A single proton has no binding energy at all."}'),
  ('PHY-0299', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Binding energy per nucleon', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 135 (§19.1)', 'A carbon-12 nucleus has a total binding energy of about 92 MeV. Its binding energy per nucleon is about', '["15.3 MeV", "92 MeV", "1104 MeV", "7.7 MeV"]', 3, 'Binding energy per nucleon = total B.E. ÷ A = 92 ÷ 12 ≈ 7.7 MeV.', '{"0": "15.3 MeV divides by 6 (the number of protons) instead of 12.", "1": "92 MeV is the total binding energy, not per nucleon.", "2": "1104 multiplies by 12 instead of dividing."}'),
  ('PHY-0300', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Binding energy per nucleon', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 136 (§19.1)', 'According to the book, energy is released in any nuclear reaction in which', '["the binding energy per nucleon of the products is greater than that of the reactants", "the products have a larger total mass than the reactants", "the binding energy per nucleon of the products decreases", "the number of nucleons increases"]', 0, 'If the products are more tightly bound (higher binding energy per nucleon), mass is lost and released as energy. This happens in both fission of heavy nuclei and fusion of light nuclei.', '{"1": "A larger product mass would mean energy is absorbed, not released.", "2": "A decrease means the products are less tightly bound, which absorbs energy.", "3": "The number of nucleons stays the same in a nuclear reaction."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0301', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fission', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 137 (§19.2)', 'A heavy nucleus with 240 nucleons and binding energy 7.6 MeV per nucleon splits into fragments with an average binding energy of 8.4 MeV per nucleon. About how much energy is released?', '["2016 MeV", "192 MeV", "0.8 MeV", "1824 MeV"]', 1, 'The gain in binding energy is 8.4 − 7.6 = 0.8 MeV per nucleon. For 240 nucleons: 0.8 × 240 = 192 MeV.', '{"0": "2016 MeV is the total binding energy of the fragments (8.4 × 240), not the energy released.", "2": "0.8 MeV is the gain per nucleon, not for the whole nucleus.", "3": "1824 MeV is the binding energy of the original nucleus (7.6 × 240)."}'),
  ('PHY-0302', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fission', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 136 (§19.2)', 'Nuclear fission was discovered by Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann when they bombarded uranium-235 with', '["fast protons", "α-particles", "slow neutrons", "γ-rays"]', 2, 'When slow neutrons hit U-235, the nucleus split into barium and krypton with about three neutrons and a large release of energy.', '{"0": "Protons are repelled by the nucleus; the book''s fission is by slow neutrons.", "1": "α-particles are not used in the fission reaction described.", "3": "γ-rays are not what triggers fission here."}'),
  ('PHY-0303', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fission', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 136 (§19.2)', 'Approximately how much energy is released in the fission of one uranium-235 nucleus?', '["2 MeV", "17.6 MeV", "2000 MeV", "200 MeV"]', 3, 'The book states that about 200 MeV is given out per fission, mostly as kinetic energy of the fragments.', '{"0": "2 MeV is far too small; that is closer to the energy of a single fission neutron.", "1": "17.6 MeV is the energy of the deuterium–tritium fusion reaction.", "2": "2000 MeV is ten times too large."}'),
  ('PHY-0304', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fission', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 137 (§19.2)', 'Which two nuclides undergo fission most easily with slow neutrons and are mostly used for fission?', '["Uranium-235 and plutonium-239", "Uranium-238 and thorium-232", "Iron-56 and nickel-62", "Deuterium and tritium"]', 0, 'The book notes that fission takes place very easily with slow neutrons in U-235 and Pu-239.', '{"1": "U-238 is not readily fissioned by slow neutrons; that is why fuel is enriched in U-235.", "2": "Iron and nickel are the most tightly bound nuclei and do not release energy by fission.", "3": "Deuterium and tritium are fusion fuels."}'),
  ('PHY-0305', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fission', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 137 (§19.2)', 'The fragments produced in the fission of uranium-235', '["are always barium and krypton", "are radioactive", "are stable and harmless", "are always two identical nuclei"]', 1, 'Many different pairs of fragments can be formed, and all of these fission products are radioactive.', '{"0": "Ba and Kr are one possible pair; others are also produced.", "2": "The fission fragments are radioactive, which is why spent fuel is dangerous.", "3": "The fragments are of roughly equal size but not identical, and they vary from fission to fission."}'),
  ('PHY-0306', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Chain reaction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 137 (§19.2)', 'A fission chain reaction is possible because', '["each fission releases a proton that splits the next nucleus", "the fragments fuse back together", "each fission releases two or three neutrons that can cause further fissions", "uranium nuclei split on their own every second"]', 2, 'Each fission of U-235 releases 2–3 neutrons. If enough of them are absorbed by other U-235 nuclei, the fission sustains itself.', '{"0": "Neutrons, not protons, carry on the chain.", "1": "The fragments do not fuse; fission splits nuclei.", "3": "The chain depends on neutrons released by fission, not on spontaneous splitting."}'),
  ('PHY-0307', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Chain reaction', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 138 (§19.2)', 'If the mass of uranium-235 is much greater than the critical mass, the chain reaction', '["stops quickly", "proceeds at a steady rate", "does not start", "grows very rapidly and can produce an explosion, as in an atom bomb"]', 3, 'With much more than the critical mass, more than one neutron per fission causes further fissions, so the reaction grows explosively.', '{"0": "The reaction dies out when the mass is less than critical.", "1": "A steady rate needs exactly one effective neutron per fission (critical mass).", "2": "With so much fissile material the reaction certainly starts."}'),
  ('PHY-0308', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Chain reaction', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 138 (§19.2)', 'In a controlled chain reaction in a nuclear reactor, on average, how many neutrons from each fission go on to cause another fission?', '["One", "None", "Two", "Three"]', 0, 'In a controlled reaction exactly one neutron per fission, on average, causes another fission, so energy is released at a steady rate.', '{"1": "If none did, the chain reaction would stop.", "2": "Two per fission would make the reaction grow rapidly.", "3": "Three per fission would make it grow explosively."}'),
  ('PHY-0309', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 139 (§19.2)', 'In a nuclear reactor, rods of cadmium or boron are moved in or out of the core in order to', '["slow down neutrons without absorbing them", "absorb neutrons and so control the rate of the chain reaction", "produce extra neutrons", "carry heat to the heat exchanger"]', 1, 'Control rods absorb neutrons, reducing the number available for fission. Dropping them fully into the core shuts the reactor down.', '{"0": "Slowing neutrons is the job of the moderator.", "2": "Control rods remove neutrons; they do not produce them.", "3": "Heat is carried by the coolant."}'),
  ('PHY-0310', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 140 (§19.3)', 'In a reactor, the moderator is a material of low atomic mass, such as water or heavy water. Its purpose is to', '["absorb all the neutrons", "speed up slow neutrons", "slow down the fast neutrons from fission so that they can cause further fission", "shield people outside from radiation"]', 2, 'Fission neutrons are very fast and not effective at causing fission in U-235. Collisions with light nuclei in the moderator slow them down.', '{"0": "Absorbing neutrons is the job of the control rods.", "1": "The moderator slows neutrons; it never speeds them up.", "3": "Shielding is done by thick steel and concrete."}'),
  ('PHY-0311', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 140 (§19.3)', 'Heavy water, used as a moderator at the Karachi Nuclear Power Plant (KANUPP), differs from ordinary water in that its hydrogen is', '["tritium (³₁H) instead of ¹₁H", "replaced by oxygen", "replaced by helium", "deuterium (²₁H) instead of ¹₁H"]', 3, 'Heavy water is made with ²₁H (deuterium), a heavy isotope of hydrogen, instead of ordinary hydrogen ¹₁H.', '{"0": "Tritium is a different, radioactive isotope; heavy water contains deuterium.", "1": "Heavy water still contains hydrogen and oxygen.", "2": "Heavy water contains no helium."}'),
  ('PHY-0312', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 140 (§19.3)', 'Natural uranium contains only about 0.7% uranium-235. Reactor fuel is enriched to a few percent of U-235 because', '["U-235 is the isotope that undergoes fission readily with slow neutrons", "U-238 is too radioactive to handle", "U-235 is cheaper than U-238", "enrichment removes all radioactivity from the fuel"]', 0, 'The chain reaction relies on U-235, which fissions easily with slow neutrons. Natural uranium has too little U-235, so the fuel is enriched to about 2.4–3%.', '{"1": "U-238 is actually less radioactive (longer half-life) than U-235.", "2": "Enrichment is expensive; it is done because of fission, not cost.", "3": "Enriched fuel is still radioactive."}'),
  ('PHY-0313', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 139 (§19.3)', 'In a nuclear power station, the reactor plays the same part as which part of a thermal (coal or oil) power station?', '["The turbine", "The furnace", "The generator", "The cooling tower"]', 1, 'The reactor produces heat by fission, just as the furnace produces heat by burning fuel. The heat makes steam to drive the turbine and generator.', '{"0": "The turbine is driven by steam in both types of station.", "2": "The generator converts mechanical energy into electricity in both.", "3": "Cooling is needed in both, but the reactor is the heat source, like the furnace."}'),
  ('PHY-0314', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 140 (§19.3)', 'In a pressurized water reactor, the function of the heat exchanger is to', '["absorb neutrons from the core", "slow down fission neutrons", "use the heat carried from the core by the high-pressure coolant to turn ordinary water into steam", "store the spent fuel"]', 2, 'The coolant carries heat from the core to the heat exchanger, where it produces high-temperature steam from ordinary water to drive the turbine.', '{"0": "That is the job of the control rods.", "1": "That is the job of the moderator.", "3": "Spent fuel is stored elsewhere, not in the heat exchanger."}'),
  ('PHY-0315', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear waste', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 141 (§19.3)', 'According to the book, the best place found so far to store long-lived radioactive waste is', '["the open ocean", "ordinary landfill sites", "river beds near the power station", "the bottom of old salt mines, deep below the Earth''s surface"]', 3, 'Deep old salt mines are very dry and thousands of metres below the surface, so the waste can decay without polluting the environment.', '{"0": "Waste must not be dumped into oceans.", "1": "It must not be left where it could contaminate soil or water.", "2": "It must not get into water supplies."}'),
  ('PHY-0316', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fusion', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 141 (§19.4)', 'A nuclear reaction in which two light nuclei join to form a heavier nucleus is called', '["nuclear fusion", "nuclear fission", "radioactive decay", "a chain reaction"]', 0, 'Fusion is the joining of two light nuclei into a heavier one, with the release of energy because the product is more tightly bound.', '{"1": "Fission is the splitting of a heavy nucleus.", "2": "Radioactive decay is the spontaneous emission of radiation by an unstable nucleus.", "3": "A chain reaction is a self-sustaining sequence of fissions."}'),
  ('PHY-0317', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fusion', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 142 (§19.4)', 'The fusion of deuterium and tritium, ²₁H + ³₁H → ⁴₂He + ¹₀n, releases about', '["200 MeV", "17.6 MeV", "0.51 MeV", "3.3 MeV"]', 1, 'The book gives 17.6 MeV for the D–T fusion reaction.', '{"0": "About 200 MeV is released in the fission of one U-235 nucleus.", "2": "0.51 MeV is the rest-mass energy of an electron.", "3": "About 3.3 MeV is released in a deuterium–deuterium reaction, not D–T."}'),
  ('PHY-0318', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fusion', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 141 (§19.4)', 'Fusion is difficult to start because', '["light nuclei have no binding energy", "fusion absorbs more energy than it releases", "the positively charged nuclei repel each other strongly, so very high speeds (temperatures) are needed to bring them close together", "neutrons repel each other"]', 2, 'Work must be done against the electrostatic repulsion of the nuclei. Temperatures of about 10 million °C are needed, which is why it is called a thermonuclear reaction.', '{"0": "Light nuclei do have binding energy; fusion makes them more tightly bound.", "1": "Fusion releases more energy per nucleon than fission once it starts.", "3": "Neutrons are uncharged; the repulsion is between the positive nuclei."}'),
  ('PHY-0319', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fusion', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 142 (§19.4)', 'Where does nuclear fusion take place naturally?', '["In nuclear power stations on Earth", "In the Earth''s core", "In uranium mines", "In the cores of stars, including the Sun"]', 3, 'Fusion occurs in the cores of stars. On Earth it has not yet been controlled for producing electricity.', '{"0": "Present-day nuclear power stations use fission, not fusion.", "1": "The Earth''s core is not hot or dense enough for fusion.", "2": "Uranium mines contain fissile material, not fusion reactions."}'),
  ('PHY-0320', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fusion', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 141 (§19.4)', 'Compared with fission, a fusion reaction', '["can release more energy per nucleon but is harder to start and has not yet been controlled for power generation", "releases less energy per nucleon and is easier to control", "needs no energy to start", "produces long-lived radioactive fission fragments"]', 0, 'Fusion gives more energy per nucleon, but needs extremely high temperatures to start and has not been controlled like fission to produce electricity.', '{"1": "Fusion releases more energy per nucleon and is harder, not easier, to control.", "2": "A very large amount of energy is needed to start fusion.", "3": "Fusion does not produce fission fragments."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0321', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive decay', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 143 (§19.5)', 'Fluctuations in the count rate from a radioactive source are evidence that radioactive decay is', '["caused by the detector", "random", "affected by temperature", "always faster in the morning"]', 1, 'We cannot predict when a particular nucleus will decay. The random nature of decay shows up as fluctuations in the count rate.', '{"0": "The fluctuations come from the decay process itself, not the detector.", "2": "Radioactive decay is not affected by temperature.", "3": "Decay rate does not depend on the time of day."}'),
  ('PHY-0322', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive decay', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 143 (§19.5)', 'The SI unit of activity of a radioactive source is the becquerel (Bq), which equals', '["one decay per minute", "one gram of material decaying per second", "one decay per second", "one half-life per second"]', 2, 'Activity is the number of decays (particles emitted) per second; 1 Bq = 1 decay s⁻¹.', '{"0": "The becquerel uses seconds, not minutes.", "1": "Activity counts decays, not mass.", "3": "Half-life is a time, not a rate of decay."}'),
  ('PHY-0323', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 143 (§19.5)', 'The half-life of a radioactive substance is the time taken for', '["all of its nuclei to decay", "its mass number to halve", "half of one nucleus to decay", "its activity (or number of undecayed nuclei) to fall to half of any starting value"]', 3, 'Half-life is the time for the activity of a sample to drop to half of any starting value.', '{"0": "In theory a sample never decays completely; each half-life only halves it.", "1": "The mass number of a given nucleus does not halve; the number of undecayed nuclei does.", "2": "A single nucleus either decays or not; half-life is a statistical property of many nuclei."}'),
  ('PHY-0324', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 144 (§19.5)', 'A radioactive sample of 80 g has a half-life of 5 days. How much of it remains undecayed after 15 days?', '["10 g", "40 g", "20 g", "26.7 g"]', 0, '15 days is 3 half-lives: 80 → 40 → 20 → 10 g. Remaining = (1/2)³ × 80 = 10 g.', '{"1": "40 g is what remains after only one half-life.", "2": "20 g is what remains after two half-lives.", "3": "26.7 g divides by 3, but decay halves the amount each half-life."}'),
  ('PHY-0325', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 144 (§19.5)', 'A source has an activity of 1600 Bq and a half-life of 2 hours. What is its activity after 8 hours?', '["400 Bq", "100 Bq", "200 Bq", "0 Bq"]', 1, '8 hours is 4 half-lives, so the activity is 1600 × (1/2)⁴ = 1600 ÷ 16 = 100 Bq.', '{"0": "400 Bq divides by 4 (the number of half-lives) instead of by 2⁴.", "2": "200 Bq is after 3 half-lives (6 hours).", "3": "Activity halves each half-life; it never reaches exactly zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0326', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 145 (§19.5)', 'The decay constant of a radioactive isotope is 0.0231 per day. Its half-life is', '["0.016 days", "43 days", "30 days", "3.0 days"]', 2, 'T½ = 0.693/λ = 0.693 ÷ 0.0231 = 30 days.', '{"0": "0.016 multiplies 0.693 by λ instead of dividing.", "1": "43 days is 1/λ, the mean life, without the factor 0.693.", "3": "3.0 days has the decimal point in the wrong place."}'),
  ('PHY-0327', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 145 (§19.5)', 'A radioactive nuclide has a half-life of 693 s. Its decay constant is', '["1.0 × 10³ s⁻¹", "0.693 s⁻¹", "4.8 × 10⁵ s⁻¹", "1.0 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹"]', 3, 'λ = 0.693/T½ = 0.693 ÷ 693 s = 1.0 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹.', '{"0": "1.0 × 10³ inverts the formula.", "1": "0.693 s⁻¹ would be the decay constant for a 1 s half-life.", "2": "4.8 × 10⁵ multiplies 693 by 693."}'),
  ('PHY-0328', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Activity', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 144 (§19.5)', 'A sample contains 5.0 × 10¹⁰ undecayed nuclei with a decay constant of 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ s⁻¹. What is its activity?', '["1.0 × 10⁵ Bq", "2.5 × 10¹⁶ Bq", "4.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ Bq", "5.0 × 10¹⁰ Bq"]', 0, 'Activity A = λN = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 5.0 × 10¹⁰ = 1.0 × 10⁵ Bq.', '{"1": "2.5 × 10¹⁶ divides N by λ instead of multiplying.", "2": "4.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ divides λ by N.", "3": "5.0 × 10¹⁰ is the number of nuclei, not the decay rate."}'),
  ('PHY-0329', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive decay', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 145 (§19.5)', 'The number of undecayed nuclei is N = N₀e^(−λt). After a time t = 1/λ (one mean life), the fraction remaining is about', '["0.50", "0.37", "0.63", "0.69"]', 1, 'At t = 1/λ, N = N₀e^(−1) ≈ 0.37N₀.', '{"0": "0.50 is reached at t = T½ = 0.693/λ, a bit earlier.", "2": "0.63 is the fraction that has decayed, not the fraction remaining.", "3": "0.693 is the constant in T½ = 0.693/λ, not a fraction remaining."}'),
  ('PHY-0330', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive decay', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 143 (§19.5)', 'Which statement about radioactive decay is correct?', '["A sample decays completely after two half-lives.", "We can predict exactly when a given nucleus will decay.", "The number of nuclei decaying in a given time is proportional to the number of undecayed nuclei present.", "Every radioactive element has the same half-life."]', 2, 'ΔN ∝ −NΔt: the more nuclei present, the more decay in a given interval. This gives the exponential decay curve.', '{"0": "After two half-lives a quarter remains; in theory decay never finishes.", "1": "Decay of an individual nucleus is random and cannot be predicted.", "3": "Half-lives range from minutes (U-239, 23.5 min) to billions of years (U-238, 4.5 × 10⁹ y)."}'),
  ('PHY-0331', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive decay', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 145 (§19.5)', 'Artificially radioactive isotopes are produced by', '["heating stable elements to high temperatures", "dissolving elements in acid", "exposing elements to visible light", "bombarding stable nuclei with high-energy particles such as neutrons"]', 3, 'Bombarding a stable element with high-energy particles makes its nuclei unstable, so they become radioactive.', '{"0": "Heating does not change a nucleus.", "1": "Chemical reactions do not affect nuclear stability.", "2": "Visible light photons have far too little energy to change a nucleus."}'),
  ('PHY-0332', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive tracers', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 147 (§19.6)', 'Which radioactive isotope is used to check whether a person''s thyroid gland is working properly?', '["Iodine-131", "Cobalt-60", "Carbon-14", "Uranium-235"]', 0, 'The thyroid gland absorbs iodine, so radio-iodine is used as a tracer for it; iodine-131 is also used to treat thyroid cancer.', '{"1": "Cobalt is absorbed by the liver according to the book; cobalt-60 is better known for radiotherapy.", "2": "Carbon-14 is used for dating, not for thyroid tests.", "3": "U-235 is a reactor fuel, not a medical tracer."}'),
  ('PHY-0333', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive tracers', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 147 (§19.6)', 'A radioactive tracer is useful because', '["it is chemically completely different from the normal element", "it behaves chemically like the normal isotope, but its location can be followed by detecting the radiation it emits", "it emits no radiation inside the body", "it makes the body produce more of the element"]', 1, 'A radioactive isotope behaves just like the stable isotope in chemical and biological processes, but its radiation lets detectors follow where it goes.', '{"0": "If it behaved differently it would not trace the normal process.", "2": "A tracer is useful precisely because it emits radiation that can be detected.", "3": "A tracer only follows the process; it does not increase production."}'),
  ('PHY-0334', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive tracers', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 147 (§19.6)', 'Which radioactive isotope does the book mention for studying the circulation of blood?', '["Iodine-131", "Phosphorus-32", "Sodium-24", "Plutonium-239"]', 2, 'The book says radioactive sodium-24 can be used to study blood circulation.', '{"0": "Iodine-131 is used for the thyroid gland.", "1": "Phosphorus-32 is mentioned for skin cancer treatment and bone uptake.", "3": "Plutonium-239 is a reactor fuel, not a tracer."}'),
  ('PHY-0335', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive tracers', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 146 (§19.6)', 'How do radioactive tracers help farmers decide the proper amount of fertilizer?', '["The radiation kills pests in the soil.", "The radiation makes the fertilizer dissolve faster.", "The radioisotope replaces the fertilizer completely.", "A small amount of radioisotope mixed with the fertilizer shows how much is taken up by the plant."]', 3, 'With a tracer mixed into the fertilizer, a radiation detector measures how much the plant absorbs, so the right amount can be used.', '{"0": "The book''s use is measuring uptake, not killing pests.", "1": "Radiation is not used to dissolve fertilizer.", "2": "Only a small amount of radioisotope is added as a marker."}'),
  ('PHY-0336', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Annihilation reactions', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 148 (§19.7)', 'When an electron and a positron at rest annihilate, the two γ-ray photons travel in opposite directions because', '["the total momentum must remain zero", "they have opposite charges", "one is emitted by the electron and one by the positron", "energy must be conserved"]', 0, 'The pair had zero total momentum, so the two photons (each about 0.51 MeV) must move in opposite directions to keep the total momentum zero.', '{"1": "Photons have no charge.", "2": "The photons come from the pair as a whole, not one from each particle.", "3": "Energy conservation sets their total energy (1.02 MeV), not their directions."}'),
  ('PHY-0337', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Annihilation reactions', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 147 (§19.7)', 'The W⁺, W⁻ and Z bosons that carry the electroweak force, discovered in high-energy collisions, were predicted by', '["Albert Einstein", "Prof. Abdus Salam (with others)", "James Chadwick", "Max Planck"]', 1, 'The book notes that the W and Z bosons, the carriers of the electroweak force, were predicted by Prof. Abdus Salam.', '{"0": "Einstein''s work was on relativity and the photoelectric effect.", "2": "Chadwick discovered the neutron.", "3": "Planck introduced energy quanta."}'),
  ('PHY-0338', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Applications of radiation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 147 (§19.6)', 'Improved varieties of crops such as rice, chickpea, wheat and cotton, with better disease resistance and yield, have been developed by', '["nuclear fusion", "electron diffraction", "radiation-induced mutations", "electromagnetic induction"]', 2, 'The book says radiation-induced mutations have produced crop varieties with better structure, disease resistance and yield.', '{"0": "Fusion is an energy-producing nuclear reaction, not a plant-breeding method.", "1": "Electron diffraction shows the wave nature of electrons.", "3": "Electromagnetic induction produces emf in coils."}'),
  ('PHY-0339', 'Medical physics', 'Radiation in medicine', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 153 (Introduction)', 'Which of the following radiations used in medicine is non-ionizing?', '["X-rays", "Gamma rays", "Positrons from a tracer", "Ultrasound"]', 3, 'The text classes ultrasound waves and radio waves as non-ionizing. X-rays, gamma rays and positrons are ionizing radiations.', '{"0": "X-rays can knock electrons out of atoms, so they are ionizing.", "1": "Gamma rays are high-energy photons and are ionizing.", "2": "Positrons are emitted by radioactive tracers and lead to ionizing gamma rays."}'),
  ('PHY-0340', 'Medical physics', 'Radiation in medicine', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 153 (Introduction)', 'X-rays and gamma rays interact with the atoms of tissue mainly through', '["the photoelectric effect and Compton scattering", "the piezoelectric effect and the Doppler effect", "nuclear fission and fusion", "total internal reflection and diffraction"]', 0, 'The text states that X-rays and gamma rays interact with matter through the photoelectric effect and Compton scattering; both transfer photon energy to electrons.', '{"1": "Piezoelectric and Doppler effects concern ultrasound, not X-rays.", "2": "Diagnostic photons do not cause fission or fusion in tissue.", "3": "These are wave-optics effects, not the absorption processes that make X-ray images."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0341', 'Medical physics', 'Radiation in medicine', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 153 (Introduction)', 'MRI produces detailed images of soft tissue by using', '["X-rays passing through the body", "radio waves interacting with atomic nuclei", "gamma rays from an injected tracer", "ultrasound echoes from tissue boundaries"]', 1, 'The text says radio waves are used in MRI, where they interact with atomic nuclei to image soft tissues without harmful ionizing radiation.', '{"0": "X-rays are used in radiography and CT, not MRI.", "2": "Gamma rays from tracers are used in nuclear medicine (PET, SPECT).", "3": "Echoes of ultrasound are used in ultrasound imaging, not MRI."}'),
  ('PHY-0342', 'Medical physics', 'Piezoelectric effect', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 153 (§20.1)', 'The piezoelectric effect was discovered in 1880 by', '["Marie and Pierre Curie", "Godfrey Hounsfield and Allan Cormack", "Pierre and Jacques Curie", "Wilhelm Röntgen and Max von Laue"]', 2, 'The brothers Pierre Curie and Jacques Curie found in 1880 that pressing crystals such as quartz, tourmaline and Rochelle salt produced electric charge.', '{"0": "Marie Curie is known for radioactivity, not the piezoelectric effect.", "1": "Hounsfield and Cormack invented the CT scanner in the early 1970s.", "3": "Röntgen discovered X-rays and von Laue showed X-ray diffraction."}'),
  ('PHY-0343', 'Medical physics', 'Piezoelectric effect', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 153 (§20.1)', 'Which of these is a piezoelectric crystal named in the text?', '["Sodium chloride", "Copper sulphate", "Diamond", "Rochelle salt (sodium potassium tartrate)"]', 3, 'Quartz, tourmaline and Rochelle salt (sodium potassium tartrate) are the crystals the Curie brothers used.', '{"0": "Common salt has a centre of symmetry, so it is not piezoelectric.", "1": "Copper sulphate is not one of the crystals named.", "2": "Diamond has a centre of symmetry and is not piezoelectric."}'),
  ('PHY-0344', 'Medical physics', 'Piezoelectric effect', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 154 (§20.1)', 'When an electric field is applied across a piezoelectric crystal, the crystal changes shape slightly. This is called the', '["converse piezoelectric effect", "direct piezoelectric effect", "photoelectric effect", "thermoelectric effect"]', 0, 'Applying an electric field to deform the crystal is the converse piezoelectric effect. Pressure producing an electric field is the direct effect.', '{"1": "The direct effect is the reverse: pressure produces a voltage.", "2": "The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons by light.", "3": "The thermoelectric effect turns a temperature difference into a voltage."}'),
  ('PHY-0345', 'Medical physics', 'Piezoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 154 (§20.1)', 'Crystals show the piezoelectric effect because they', '["contain free electrons like a metal", "have no centre of symmetry, so pressure shifts their positive and negative charges unequally", "are good conductors of heat", "are radioactive"]', 1, 'Piezoelectric crystals lack a centre of symmetry. Pressure disturbs the balance of their internal positive and negative charges, so an electric field appears on the surface.', '{"0": "Free electrons would short out any surface charge; piezoelectric crystals are insulators.", "2": "Heat conduction has nothing to do with the effect.", "3": "The crystals used are not radioactive."}'),
  ('PHY-0346', 'Medical physics', 'Piezoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 155 (§20.2)', 'In an ultrasound probe, the returning echoes are turned into electrical signals by the', '["converse piezoelectric effect", "Compton effect", "direct piezoelectric effect", "photoelectric effect"]', 2, 'The echo presses on the crystal, which produces a voltage: the direct piezoelectric effect. The converse effect is used to produce the outgoing pulse.', '{"0": "The converse effect turns voltage into vibration, which is how the pulse is sent, not received.", "1": "Compton scattering is a photon–electron interaction.", "3": "The photoelectric effect involves light releasing electrons."}'),
  ('PHY-0347', 'Medical physics', 'Piezoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 153 (§20.1)', 'The same transducer can both send and receive ultrasound because', '["it contains a separate loudspeaker and microphone", "ultrasound is electromagnetic and passes straight through it", "it switches between X-ray and sound modes", "an applied voltage makes the crystal vibrate, and incoming pressure waves make it produce a voltage"]', 3, 'The converse effect turns electrical pulses into vibrations (sending), and the direct effect turns returning pressure waves into voltage (receiving). The transducer switches to receive mode between pulses.', '{"0": "A single piezoelectric crystal does both jobs; no separate loudspeaker is needed.", "1": "Ultrasound is a mechanical (sound) wave, not electromagnetic.", "2": "An ultrasound probe does not produce X-rays."}'),
  ('PHY-0348', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 154 (§20.2)', 'The frequency range typically used for diagnostic ultrasound, as given in the text, is', '["1 MHz to 15 MHz", "20 Hz to 20 kHz", "1 kHz to 15 kHz", "1 GHz to 15 GHz"]', 0, 'Medical ultrasound typically uses 1 MHz–15 MHz, far above the upper limit of human hearing (about 20 kHz).', '{"1": "20 Hz–20 kHz is the range of human hearing, not ultrasound.", "2": "1–15 kHz is audible sound.", "3": "GHz frequencies would be absorbed almost at once in tissue; this is microwave territory."}'),
  ('PHY-0349', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 154 (§20.2)', 'Part of an ultrasound pulse is reflected at the boundary between two tissues because the tissues differ in', '["electric charge", "acoustic impedance", "atomic number only", "temperature"]', 1, 'Reflection happens where acoustic impedance changes. Acoustic impedance depends on tissue density and the speed of sound in it.', '{"0": "Tissues are electrically neutral; charge does not reflect sound.", "2": "Atomic number controls X-ray absorption, not sound reflection.", "3": "Small temperature differences in the body do not create echoes."}'),
  ('PHY-0350', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 155 (§20.2)', 'Which boundary would give the strongest echo in an ultrasound scan?', '["Liver and kidney", "Two layers of the same muscle", "Soft tissue and bone", "Blood and a blood vessel wall"]', 2, 'The bigger the difference in acoustic impedance, the stronger the echo. Soft tissue and bone differ greatly; the other pairs are soft tissues with similar impedance.', '{"0": "Liver and kidney are both soft tissue with similar impedance, so the echo is weak.", "1": "The same tissue has no impedance change, so almost nothing reflects.", "3": "Blood and vessel wall differ only slightly."}'),
  ('PHY-0351', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 155 (§20.2)', 'A sonographer switches from a 3 MHz probe to a 10 MHz probe. What is the main effect?', '["Deeper penetration but poorer resolution", "Both resolution and penetration improve", "The scan now uses ionizing radiation", "Better resolution but less depth of penetration"]', 3, 'Higher-frequency ultrasound gives better resolution but is absorbed more, so it penetrates less. Lower frequency goes deeper with less detail.', '{"0": "That describes lowering the frequency, not raising it.", "1": "There is a trade-off; both cannot improve together.", "2": "Ultrasound is non-ionizing at any frequency used."}'),
  ('PHY-0352', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 155 (§20.2)', 'Why is gel spread between the ultrasound probe and the skin?', '["To remove air gaps so the sound passes into the body instead of being reflected", "To cool the probe", "To absorb harmful radiation", "To make the skin conduct electricity"]', 0, 'Air has a very different acoustic impedance from skin, so even a thin air layer reflects most of the sound. The gel fills the gap and improves transmission.', '{"1": "The probe does not heat up enough to need cooling.", "2": "Ultrasound is not harmful ionizing radiation.", "3": "Ultrasound is a sound wave; skin conductivity is irrelevant."}'),
  ('PHY-0353', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 155 (§20.2)', 'On an ultrasound image, an area that appears bright indicates', '["a region that absorbs the most X-rays", "a region giving strong reflections (echoes)", "a region of high tracer uptake", "a region where no sound reached"]', 1, 'The computer uses the time delay and intensity of echoes. Stronger reflections are shown brighter.', '{"0": "X-ray absorption is the basis of radiography, not ultrasound.", "2": "Tracer uptake is shown in PET images.", "3": "Regions with no echo appear dark, not bright."}'),
  ('PHY-0354', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 155 (§20.2)', 'An ultrasound pulse reflects from an organ boundary and its echo returns 1.3 × 10⁻⁴ s after it was sent. If sound travels at 1540 m s⁻¹ in tissue, how deep is the boundary?', '["about 20 cm", "about 5 cm", "about 10 cm", "about 1.2 cm"]', 2, 'The pulse goes down and back, so depth = v t ÷ 2 = 1540 × 1.3 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 2 ≈ 0.10 m = 10 cm.', '{"0": "20 cm forgets that the echo time covers the trip there and back.", "1": "5 cm halves the depth a second time.", "3": "1.2 cm is a wrong power of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-0355', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 155 (§20.2)', 'What is the wavelength of 3 MHz ultrasound in soft tissue where sound travels at 1500 m s⁻¹?', '["5 mm", "0.05 mm", "4.5 × 10⁹ m", "0.5 mm"]', 3, 'λ = v ÷ f = 1500 ÷ (3 × 10⁶) = 5 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.5 mm.', '{"0": "5 mm is ten times too large.", "1": "0.05 mm is ten times too small.", "2": "4.5 × 10⁹ multiplies v and f instead of dividing."}'),
  ('PHY-0356', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 155 (§20.2)', 'Ultrasound can measure the speed of blood flow by detecting', '["the Doppler shift in the frequency of the reflected sound", "the change in X-ray intensity", "the annihilation of positrons", "the piezoelectric voltage in the blood"]', 0, 'Moving blood cells shift the frequency of the echo (Doppler effect). The size of the shift gives the flow speed.', '{"1": "X-rays are not used in ultrasound.", "2": "Positron annihilation belongs to PET.", "3": "Blood is not a piezoelectric crystal."}'),
  ('PHY-0357', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 155 (§20.2)', 'Echocardiography is the use of ultrasound to study', '["the growth of a fetus", "the structure and function of the heart in real time", "the prostate and urinary system", "bone fractures"]', 1, 'In cardiology, echocardiography lets doctors see the heart''s structure and function in real time.', '{"0": "Fetal monitoring is obstetric ultrasound.", "2": "The prostate and urinary system are studied in urology.", "3": "Bone reflects most ultrasound and fractures are best seen with X-rays."}'),
  ('PHY-0358', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 154 (§20.2)', 'Ultrasound is preferred over X-rays for routine checks of a fetus mainly because ultrasound', '["gives sharper images of bone", "penetrates further than X-rays", "does not use ionizing radiation, so it can be repeated safely", "travels at the speed of light"]', 2, 'Ultrasound is non-ionizing, non-invasive and repeatable, so it is safe for repeated scans during pregnancy.', '{"0": "X-rays, not ultrasound, give the best images of bone.", "1": "Penetration is not the reason; ultrasound is limited in depth.", "3": "Ultrasound is a sound wave and travels far slower than light."}'),
  ('PHY-0359', 'Medical physics', 'Production of X-rays', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 156 (§20.3)', 'In an X-ray tube, the electrons that strike the target are produced by', '["photoelectric emission from the target", "radioactive decay inside the tube", "ionization of air in the tube", "thermionic emission from a heated cathode"]', 3, 'The filament heats the cathode, which emits electrons. They are accelerated through a large potential difference towards the anode target.', '{"0": "The target is struck by electrons; it is not their source.", "1": "No radioactive source is used in an X-ray tube.", "2": "The tube is highly evacuated, so there is no air to ionize."}'),
  ('PHY-0360', 'Medical physics', 'Production of X-rays', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 156 (§20.3)', 'The target in an X-ray tube is usually made of tungsten because it', '["is a heavy element with a very high melting point", "is a light element that emits visible light", "is a good insulator", "is radioactive"]', 0, 'X-rays come from inner-shell transitions in heavy atoms, and most of the electron energy becomes heat, so a heavy, high-melting-point metal like tungsten is used (with cooling channels).', '{"1": "Light elements give low-energy photons, not X-rays.", "2": "Tungsten is a metal and conducts electricity.", "3": "Tungsten is not radioactive."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0361', 'Medical physics', 'Production of X-rays', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 156 (§20.3)', 'Electrons are accelerated from rest through a potential difference of 30 kV in an X-ray tube. With what kinetic energy do they strike the target?', '["4.8 × 10⁻¹⁸ J", "4.8 × 10⁻¹⁵ J", "1.9 × 10²³ J", "30 J"]', 1, 'K.E. = eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 30 000 = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁵ J (that is, 30 keV).', '{"0": "4.8 × 10⁻¹⁸ J uses 30 V instead of 30 kV.", "2": "1.9 × 10²³ divides V by e instead of multiplying.", "3": "30 J confuses the voltage in kV with an energy in joules."}'),
  ('PHY-0362', 'Medical physics', 'Production of X-rays', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'An X-ray tube operates at 25 kV. What is the shortest wavelength of X-rays it produces? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)', '["5.0 × 10⁻⁸ m", "2.5 × 10⁻¹¹ m", "5.0 × 10⁻¹¹ m", "1.2 × 10⁻⁶ m"]', 2, 'λ_min = hc ÷ eV = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸) ÷ (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 25 000) ≈ 4.97 × 10⁻¹¹ m ≈ 5.0 × 10⁻¹¹ m.', '{"0": "5.0 × 10⁻⁸ m uses 25 V instead of 25 kV.", "1": "2.5 × 10⁻¹¹ m is the value for 50 kV.", "3": "1.2 × 10⁻⁶ m is hc/e alone, without dividing by the voltage."}'),
  ('PHY-0363', 'Medical physics', 'Production of X-rays', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'The accelerating voltage of an X-ray tube is doubled. The cut-off (minimum) wavelength of the continuous spectrum', '["is doubled", "stays the same", "becomes four times smaller", "is halved"]', 3, 'λ_min = hc ÷ eV, so λ_min is inversely proportional to V. Doubling V halves λ_min.', '{"0": "λ_min decreases, not increases, when V rises.", "1": "The cut-off depends directly on the voltage.", "2": "λ_min varies as 1/V, not 1/V²."}'),
  ('PHY-0364', 'Medical physics', 'Production of X-rays', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'What is the highest frequency of X-rays from a tube run at 20 kV? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)', '["4.8 × 10¹⁸ Hz", "4.8 × 10¹⁵ Hz", "2.1 × 10⁻¹⁹ Hz", "6.2 × 10⁻¹¹ Hz"]', 0, 'hf_max = eV, so f_max = eV ÷ h = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 20 000) ÷ 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ≈ 4.8 × 10¹⁸ Hz.', '{"1": "4.8 × 10¹⁵ Hz uses 20 V instead of 20 kV.", "2": "2.1 × 10⁻¹⁹ Hz divides h by eV (the reciprocal).", "3": "6.2 × 10⁻¹¹ is the minimum wavelength in metres, not a frequency."}'),
  ('PHY-0365', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray spectra', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'The continuous part of an X-ray spectrum is produced by', '["electrons falling into vacancies in the K-shell", "electrons being sharply decelerated by the target (bremsstrahlung)", "nuclei of the target decaying", "positrons annihilating with electrons"]', 1, 'Bremsstrahlung (braking radiation) is emitted when fast electrons are strongly decelerated by the target; an accelerating charge radiates.', '{"0": "Transitions into the K-shell give the characteristic line spectrum.", "2": "The target is not radioactive.", "3": "Annihilation radiation is the basis of PET, not of X-ray tubes."}'),
  ('PHY-0366', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'The shortest wavelength in the continuous X-ray spectrum comes from electrons that', '["lose their energy over many small collisions", "knock out an L-shell electron", "lose all their kinetic energy in a single collision", "pass through the target without collision"]', 2, 'If an electron gives up all its kinetic energy at once, a single photon of maximum energy hf_max = eV is produced, giving λ_min. Electrons that lose energy in several steps give longer wavelengths.', '{"0": "Several collisions give several lower-energy photons of longer wavelength.", "1": "Knocking out an inner electron leads to characteristic lines, not the cut-off.", "3": "An electron that does not collide emits no X-ray."}'),
  ('PHY-0367', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'The wavelengths of the characteristic lines in an X-ray spectrum depend mainly on the', '["accelerating voltage", "current through the filament", "time of exposure", "material of the target"]', 3, 'Characteristic photon energies equal the differences between inner-shell energy levels, which are fixed for each element. So they depend on the target material.', '{"0": "The voltage sets the cut-off wavelength; once high enough, it does not move the characteristic lines.", "1": "The filament current changes the number of electrons (intensity), not the line wavelengths.", "2": "Exposure time affects the total dose, not the wavelengths."}'),
  ('PHY-0368', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'A K_β X-ray photon is emitted when an electron', '["drops from the M-shell into a vacancy in the K-shell", "drops from the L-shell into a vacancy in the K-shell", "drops from the M-shell into a vacancy in the L-shell", "is knocked out of the K-shell"]', 0, 'K-series lines end in the K-shell. L → K gives K_α, and M → K gives K_β, which has more energy because the M-shell is further from the K-shell.', '{"1": "L → K is the K_α line.", "2": "A transition ending in the L-shell gives an L-series line.", "3": "Knocking out the electron creates the vacancy; the photon comes from the electron that fills it."}'),
  ('PHY-0369', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray spectra', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'For the same target, how do the K_α and K_β X-ray photons compare?', '["K_α has higher energy and shorter wavelength than K_β", "K_β has higher energy and shorter wavelength than K_α", "they have equal energies", "K_β has higher energy and longer wavelength than K_α"]', 1, 'K_β comes from M → K, a bigger energy drop than L → K (K_α). Higher energy means shorter wavelength (E = hc/λ).', '{"0": "The L → K drop is smaller, so K_α has less energy.", "2": "The M and L levels differ, so the photon energies differ.", "3": "Higher energy means shorter, not longer, wavelength."}'),
  ('PHY-0370', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 156 (§20.3)', 'Transitions of electrons between the outer levels of hydrogen give infrared, visible or ultraviolet light, but inner-shell transitions in heavy atoms give X-rays because', '["heavy atoms have fewer electrons", "outer electrons of heavy atoms move faster", "inner electrons of heavy atoms are very tightly bound, so the energy differences are large", "X-rays come only from the nucleus"]', 2, 'Inner-shell electrons of heavy atoms are held strongly by the large nuclear charge, so filling a vacancy releases a large energy: an X-ray photon.', '{"0": "Heavy atoms have more electrons, not fewer.", "1": "The outer electrons are not the source of X-rays.", "3": "Characteristic X-rays come from electron transitions, not from the nucleus."}'),
  ('PHY-0371', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'The K_α line of a target has a photon energy of 8.0 keV. What is its wavelength? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)', '["1.6 × 10⁻⁷ m", "1.6 × 10⁻¹³ m", "1.3 × 10⁻¹⁵ m", "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ m"]', 3, 'E = 8.0 × 10³ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.28 × 10⁻¹⁵ J. λ = hc ÷ E = 1.989 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 1.28 × 10⁻¹⁵ ≈ 1.55 × 10⁻¹⁰ m ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.', '{"0": "1.6 × 10⁻⁷ m uses 8.0 eV instead of 8.0 keV.", "1": "1.6 × 10⁻¹³ m uses 8.0 MeV instead of 8.0 keV.", "2": "1.3 × 10⁻¹⁵ is the photon energy in joules, not the wavelength."}'),
  ('PHY-0372', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray imaging', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'The gradual loss of intensity of an X-ray beam as it passes through the body is called', '["attenuation", "amplification", "diffraction", "polarization"]', 0, 'As X-rays interact with atoms they lose energy; the intensity falls exponentially, I = I₀ e^(−μx). This is attenuation.', '{"1": "Amplification is an increase, not a loss.", "2": "Diffraction is the spreading of waves past edges.", "3": "Polarization concerns the direction of the field vibrations."}'),
  ('PHY-0373', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'An X-ray beam passes through 2.0 cm of a material whose attenuation coefficient is 50 m⁻¹. What fraction of the original intensity gets through? (e⁻¹ ≈ 0.37)', '["about 0.63", "about 0.37", "about 0.14", "zero"]', 1, 'μx = 50 × 0.020 = 1, so I ÷ I₀ = e⁻¹ ≈ 0.37.', '{"0": "0.63 is the fraction absorbed, not transmitted.", "2": "0.14 is e⁻², which would need μx = 2.", "3": "Exponential attenuation never reduces the intensity exactly to zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0374', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray imaging', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'For a certain X-ray energy, a material''s attenuation coefficient is 0.693 cm⁻¹. What thickness reduces the beam intensity to one quarter? (ln 2 = 0.693)', '["1.0 cm", "4.0 cm", "2.0 cm", "0.25 cm"]', 2, 'I = I₀/4 when e^(−μx) = 1/4, so μx = ln 4 = 2 ln 2 = 1.386. x = 1.386 ÷ 0.693 = 2.0 cm. (Each 1.0 cm halves the intensity.)', '{"0": "1.0 cm only halves the intensity.", "1": "4.0 cm reduces it to one-sixteenth.", "3": "0.25 cm treats the fraction as a thickness."}'),
  ('PHY-0375', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 158 (§20.3)', 'Hard (high-energy) X-rays are used for imaging bone, while soft (low-energy) X-rays are preferred for', '["metal implants", "the skull", "teeth fillings", "soft tissue such as the breast"]', 3, 'Contrast depends on X-ray energy. Low-energy X-rays show the small differences between soft tissues, so they are used for the breast.', '{"0": "Metal implants absorb strongly and are imaged with high-energy X-rays.", "1": "The skull is bone, imaged with hard X-rays.", "2": "Fillings are dense metal and stand out even with hard X-rays."}'),
  ('PHY-0376', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 158 (§20.3)', 'Barium or iodine compounds are given to patients before some X-ray examinations because they', '["have high atomic numbers and absorb X-rays strongly, increasing contrast", "emit positrons that are detected", "make the X-rays more penetrating", "reduce the radiation dose to zero"]', 0, 'Iodine (Z = 53) and barium (Z = 56) absorb X-rays well, so the organs they fill stand out clearly from surrounding tissue.', '{"1": "These contrast media are not radioactive tracers.", "2": "They absorb X-rays; they do not increase penetration.", "3": "They improve the image but do not remove the dose."}'),
  ('PHY-0377', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray imaging', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 158 (§20.3)', 'Image intensifiers are used in fluoroscopy (real-time X-ray imaging) mainly to', '["produce 3D images", "brighten the image so a lower radiation dose can be used", "change X-rays into ultrasound", "replace the X-ray tube"]', 1, 'Image intensifiers brighten the image, making it easier to see and helping reduce radiation exposure.', '{"0": "3D images come from CT, not image intensifiers.", "2": "There is no conversion to ultrasound.", "3": "An X-ray tube is still needed to produce the beam."}'),
  ('PHY-0378', 'Medical physics', 'CT scanning', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 158 (§20.4)', 'In an ordinary X-ray image of the chest it is hard to tell the front ribs from the back ribs because', '["the ribs do not absorb X-rays", "X-rays are reflected by the ribs", "the image is 2D, so structures at different depths overlap", "the ribs are radioactive"]', 2, 'A plain radiograph is a flat projection: structures along the same line of sight lie on top of each other. CT solves this by imaging slices.', '{"0": "Bone absorbs X-rays strongly; that is why ribs show at all.", "1": "X-ray imaging depends on absorption, not reflection.", "3": "Ribs are not radioactive."}'),
  ('PHY-0379', 'Medical physics', 'CT scanning', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 158 (§20.4)', 'How does a CT scanner build up a 3D image?', '["It takes one X-ray image and stretches it", "It listens for echoes from tissue boundaries", "It detects gamma rays from an injected tracer", "It takes X-ray images of one slice from many angles to get a 2D cross-section, then repeats for many slices and stacks them"]', 3, 'Many projections of one section give a 2D slice; moving along the body axis and repeating gives many slices, which the computer combines into a 3D image.', '{"0": "A single projection cannot give depth information.", "1": "That describes ultrasound.", "2": "That describes PET or SPECT."}'),
  ('PHY-0380', 'Medical physics', 'CT scanning', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 159 (§20.4)', 'The word ''tomography'' comes from the Greek word ''tomos'', which means', '["slice", "light", "picture", "radiation"]', 0, 'Tomos means slice: tomography images the body layer by layer.', '{"1": "Photos means light.", "2": "Graphein (to write or draw) is the second part of the word.", "3": "No part of the word means radiation."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0381', 'Medical physics', 'CT scanning', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 159 (§20.4)', 'The CT scanner was invented in the early 1970s by', '["Pierre and Jacques Curie", "Godfrey Hounsfield and Allan Cormack", "Wilhelm Röntgen", "Paul Dirac and Carl Anderson"]', 1, 'Hounsfield (England) and Cormack (South Africa) developed CT and shared the 1979 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.', '{"0": "The Curie brothers discovered the piezoelectric effect.", "2": "Röntgen discovered X-rays in 1895.", "3": "Dirac predicted and Anderson discovered the positron."}'),
  ('PHY-0382', 'Medical physics', 'CT scanning', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 159 (§20.4)', 'Which is a drawback of CT compared with ultrasound?', '["CT cannot show bone", "CT gives only flat 2D images", "CT exposes the patient to ionizing radiation", "CT cannot locate a tumour"]', 2, 'CT uses X-rays, which are ionizing and carry a small risk, so extra care is taken with pregnant women. Ultrasound uses no ionizing radiation.', '{"0": "CT shows bone very clearly.", "1": "CT gives cross-sections that combine into 3D images.", "3": "CT shows the size and position of a tumour, which helps target treatment."}'),
  ('PHY-0383', 'Medical physics', 'CT scanning', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 159 (§20.4)', 'CT is better than a plain X-ray at telling apart two tissues of similar density because it', '["uses sound waves", "uses a contrast-free radio signal", "uses lower-energy photons only", "builds cross-sectional images, so the tissues do not overlap"]', 3, 'CT removes overlap by imaging thin slices from many angles, so small density differences between neighbouring tissues can be seen.', '{"0": "CT uses X-rays, not sound.", "1": "CT does not use radio waves; MRI does.", "2": "The advantage comes from slicing and reconstruction, not from lower energy."}'),
  ('PHY-0384', 'Medical physics', 'PET scanning', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 161 (§20.5)', 'In a PET scan, the radioactive tracer injected into a vein emits', '["positrons", "neutrons", "alpha particles", "X-rays"]', 0, 'PET uses positron-emitting tracers. Each positron soon meets an electron and annihilates.', '{"1": "Neutron emitters are not used in PET.", "2": "Alpha emitters would be very damaging and are not used for imaging.", "3": "The tracer does not emit X-rays; the detected photons are gamma rays from annihilation."}'),
  ('PHY-0385', 'Medical physics', 'PET scanning', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 161 (§20.5)', 'When a positron annihilates with an electron at rest, two gamma photons are produced travelling in opposite directions. This is required by conservation of', '["charge only", "momentum", "mass only", "angular frequency"]', 1, 'The pair has almost no momentum before annihilation, so the two photons must have equal and opposite momenta: they fly apart back to back.', '{"0": "Charge is conserved (+e and −e give zero) but that does not fix the directions.", "2": "Mass is not conserved; it is converted into photon energy.", "3": "Angular frequency is not a conserved quantity."}'),
  ('PHY-0386', 'Medical physics', 'PET scanning', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 161 (§20.5)', 'What is the energy of each gamma photon produced when a positron and an electron, both nearly at rest, annihilate?', '["about 1.02 MeV", "about 0.26 MeV", "about 0.51 MeV", "about 931 MeV"]', 2, 'The total rest energy is 2m_e c² ≈ 1.02 MeV, shared equally by two photons, so each has m_e c² ≈ 0.51 MeV.', '{"0": "1.02 MeV is the total for both photons.", "1": "0.26 MeV halves the energy once too often.", "3": "931 MeV is the energy equivalent of 1 u, not of an electron."}'),
  ('PHY-0387', 'Medical physics', 'PET scanning', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 161 (§20.5)', 'Brighter areas on a PET image show regions where', '["bone is thicker", "more ultrasound was reflected", "tissues have the highest atomic number", "more tracer has collected because the tissue is more active"]', 3, 'The tracer builds up in very active tissue, such as many cancers or working parts of the brain and heart. More tracer means more annihilation events, so those areas look brighter.', '{"0": "Bone thickness matters in X-ray images, not PET.", "1": "Reflected ultrasound forms ultrasound images.", "2": "Atomic number controls X-ray absorption, not PET brightness."}'),
  ('PHY-0388', 'Medical physics', 'PET scanning', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 160 (§20.5)', 'PET is especially valuable for early cancer detection because it shows', '["metabolic (functional) activity before clear anatomical changes appear", "the exact density of bones", "only the outline of organs", "tissues without any radiation"]', 0, 'PET maps tracer uptake, which reflects the activity of cells, so disease can be seen before the anatomy changes.', '{"1": "Bone density is best shown by X-rays and CT.", "2": "PET gives functional, not just outline, information.", "3": "PET uses a radioactive tracer, so it does involve radiation."}'),
  ('PHY-0389', 'Medical physics', 'PET scanning', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 161 (§20.5)', 'In a PET scanner, the two photons from an annihilation are detected by opposite detectors, one 0.4 ns before the other. How far from the midpoint between the detectors did the annihilation occur? (c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)', '["12 cm, towards the detector that fired first", "6 cm, towards the detector that fired first", "6 cm, towards the detector that fired second", "at the midpoint"]', 1, 'Path difference = cΔt = 3 × 10⁸ × 0.4 × 10⁻⁹ = 0.12 m. The event is offset by half of this, 0.06 m, towards the detector that received its photon first.', '{"0": "12 cm is the path difference, not the offset from the midpoint.", "2": "The photon that arrives first travelled the shorter distance, so the event is nearer that detector.", "3": "A time difference means the paths are unequal, so it is not at the midpoint."}'),
  ('PHY-0390', 'Medical physics', 'PET scanning', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 160 (§20.5)', 'How does SPECT differ from PET?', '["SPECT uses ultrasound, while PET uses X-rays", "SPECT gives only 2D images, while PET uses MRI", "SPECT uses a gamma-emitting tracer recorded by a rotating gamma camera, while PET uses a positron-emitting tracer and pairs of annihilation photons", "SPECT uses no tracer"]', 2, 'In SPECT, a gamma-emitting tracer is imaged by a rotating gamma camera that records many views. PET uses positron emitters and detects the two back-to-back photons in coincidence.', '{"0": "Neither method uses ultrasound, and PET does not use an X-ray tube.", "1": "SPECT reconstructions give 3D information, and PET is not MRI.", "3": "SPECT depends on an administered radiopharmaceutical."}'),
  ('PHY-0391', 'Medical physics', 'Radiotherapy', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 161 (§20.5)', 'In brachytherapy, cancer is treated by', '["aiming a beam from a machine outside the body", "using ultrasound to heat the tumour", "injecting a positron-emitting tracer to image it", "placing a radioactive source inside or close to the tumour"]', 3, 'Brachytherapy puts the radioactive source inside or next to the tumour. Teletherapy delivers radiation from a machine outside the body.', '{"0": "An external beam is teletherapy.", "1": "The text describes radiotherapy with high-energy radiation, not ultrasound heating.", "2": "A positron tracer is used for PET imaging, not treatment."}'),
  ('PHY-0392', 'Medical physics', 'Radiotherapy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 161 (§20.5)', 'Which task belongs to a medical physicist in a cancer treatment team?', '["Planning treatment and calculating the dose distribution and treatment time", "Prescribing chemotherapy drugs", "Performing surgery", "Diagnosing disease from blood tests"]', 0, 'Medical physicists handle equipment quality assurance, treatment planning, treatment-time calculation and dose mapping, working with radiation oncologists.', '{"1": "Prescribing drugs is the oncologist''s job.", "2": "Surgery is done by surgeons.", "3": "Blood tests are interpreted by doctors and laboratory staff."}'),
  ('PHY-0393', 'Space and environment', 'Luminosity', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 166 (§21.1)', 'The SI unit of the luminosity of a star is the', '["W m⁻²", "watt", "candela", "kelvin"]', 1, 'Luminosity is the total power of electromagnetic radiation emitted by a star, so it is measured in watts.', '{"0": "W m⁻² is the unit of radiant flux intensity (power per unit area).", "2": "The candela is a unit of luminous intensity for human vision, not a star''s total power.", "3": "The kelvin measures temperature."}'),
  ('PHY-0394', 'Space and environment', 'Luminosity', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 166 (§21.1)', 'The luminosity of a star depends mainly on its', '["distance from the Earth", "colour as seen through a telescope only", "radius and surface temperature", "mass of its planets"]', 2, 'L = 4πr²σT⁴: a larger surface area and a hotter surface both increase the total power emitted.', '{"0": "Distance changes how bright the star looks, not how much power it emits.", "1": "Colour hints at temperature but radius matters too.", "3": "The star''s planets do not affect its power output."}'),
  ('PHY-0395', 'Space and environment', 'Stefan–Boltzmann law', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 166 (§21.1)', 'If the surface temperature of a star doubles while its radius stays the same, its luminosity becomes', '["2 times larger", "4 times larger", "8 times larger", "16 times larger"]', 3, 'L ∝ T⁴, so doubling T multiplies L by 2⁴ = 16.', '{"0": "That treats L as proportional to T.", "1": "That treats L as proportional to T² (the dependence on r, not T).", "2": "That treats L as proportional to T³."}'),
  ('PHY-0396', 'Space and environment', 'Stefan–Boltzmann law', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 166 (§21.1)', 'Two stars have the same surface temperature, but one has three times the radius of the other. The ratio of the larger star''s luminosity to the smaller star''s is', '["9", "3", "27", "81"]', 0, 'At equal T, L ∝ r². So L₁/L₂ = 3² = 9.', '{"1": "Luminosity depends on surface area (r²), not r.", "2": "27 = 3³ treats L as proportional to volume.", "3": "81 = 3⁴ applies the T⁴ dependence to the radius."}'),
  ('PHY-0397', 'Space and environment', 'Stefan–Boltzmann law', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 166 (§21.1)', 'A star''s radius doubles while its surface temperature halves. Its luminosity', '["stays the same", "falls to one quarter", "doubles", "falls to one half"]', 1, 'L ∝ r²T⁴ = (2)² × (½)⁴ = 4 × 1/16 = 1/4.', '{"0": "The T⁴ factor (1/16) outweighs the r² factor (4).", "2": "Doubling would need the r² factor to dominate.", "3": "One half uses T² instead of T⁴."}'),
  ('PHY-0398', 'Space and environment', 'Stefan–Boltzmann law', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 166 (§21.1)', 'Estimate the luminosity of a star of radius 1.0 × 10⁹ m and surface temperature 6000 K. (σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴)', '["about 7.3 × 10²⁵ W", "about 1.5 × 10²³ W", "about 9.2 × 10²⁶ W", "about 9.2 × 10¹⁷ W"]', 2, 'L = 4πr²σT⁴ = 4π × (1.0 × 10⁹)² × 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × (6000)⁴ ≈ 1.257 × 10¹⁹ × 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 1.296 × 10¹⁵ ≈ 9.2 × 10²⁶ W.', '{"0": "7.3 × 10²⁵ W leaves out the factor 4π.", "1": "1.5 × 10²³ W uses T³ instead of T⁴.", "3": "9.2 × 10¹⁷ W forgets to square the radius."}'),
  ('PHY-0399', 'Space and environment', 'Stefan–Boltzmann law', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 166 (§21.1)', 'Two stars have the same luminosity. Star X has twice the surface temperature of star Y. The ratio of the radius of X to the radius of Y is', '["1/2", "1/16", "4", "1/4"]', 3, 'Equal L means r²T⁴ is the same. r ∝ 1/T², so r_X/r_Y = 1/2² = 1/4.', '{"0": "1/2 uses r ∝ 1/T.", "1": "1/16 uses r ∝ 1/T⁴ (forgets the square root).", "2": "A hotter star of equal luminosity must be smaller, not larger."}'),
  ('PHY-0400', 'Space and environment', 'Radiant flux intensity', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 167 (§21.2)', 'Radiant flux intensity F at distance d from a star of luminosity L is', '["L ÷ 4πd²", "L × 4πd²", "L ÷ d", "4πd² ÷ L"]', 0, 'The power L spreads over a sphere of area 4πd², so the power per unit area is F = L/4πd².', '{"1": "Multiplying by the area gives a huge number with the wrong units.", "2": "Flux falls as 1/d², not 1/d.", "3": "This is the reciprocal of the correct formula."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0401', 'Space and environment', 'Radiant flux intensity', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 167 (§21.2)', 'A detector is moved from distance r to distance 3r from a star. The radiant flux intensity it measures becomes', '["one-third", "one-ninth", "three times", "one-sixth"]', 1, 'F ∝ 1/d², so tripling the distance reduces F by 3² = 9.', '{"0": "One-third assumes F ∝ 1/d.", "2": "Flux decreases with distance, it does not increase.", "3": "One-sixth multiplies by 2 instead of squaring."}'),
  ('PHY-0402', 'Space and environment', 'Radiant flux intensity', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 167 (§21.2)', 'A star of luminosity 4.0 × 10²⁶ W is 1.0 × 10¹¹ m from a planet. What is the radiant flux intensity at the planet?', '["about 4.0 × 10⁴ W m⁻²", "about 3.2 × 10¹⁴ W m⁻²", "about 3.2 × 10³ W m⁻²", "about 1.4 × 10³ W m⁻²"]', 2, 'F = L ÷ 4πd² = 4.0 × 10²⁶ ÷ (4π × 1.0 × 10²²) = 4.0 × 10²⁶ ÷ 1.257 × 10²³ ≈ 3.2 × 10³ W m⁻².', '{"0": "4.0 × 10⁴ W m⁻² leaves out the 4π.", "1": "3.2 × 10¹⁴ W m⁻² uses d instead of d².", "3": "1.4 × 10³ W m⁻² is the value at 1.5 × 10¹¹ m (the Earth–Sun distance), not 1.0 × 10¹¹ m."}'),
  ('PHY-0403', 'Space and environment', 'Radiant flux intensity', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 167 (§21.2)', 'Two stars look equally bright from Earth, but star P is 3 times farther away than star Q. How do their luminosities compare?', '["P is 3 times more luminous than Q", "Both have the same luminosity", "Q is 9 times more luminous than P", "P is 9 times more luminous than Q"]', 3, 'Equal flux means L/d² is the same. With d_P = 3d_Q, L_P = 9 L_Q.', '{"0": "Flux depends on 1/d², so the factor is 3², not 3.", "1": "Equal apparent brightness does not mean equal power when distances differ.", "2": "The farther star must be the more powerful one to look equally bright."}'),
  ('PHY-0404', 'Space and environment', 'Standard candles', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 168 (§21.3)', 'Which of these is used as a standard candle?', '["A Type Ia supernova", "The Moon", "A planet such as Jupiter", "Any star chosen at random"]', 0, 'Standard candles have known luminosity. Cepheid variable stars and Type Ia supernovae are the examples given.', '{"1": "The Moon only reflects sunlight and is not used for cosmic distances.", "2": "Planets shine by reflected light, not their own known luminosity.", "3": "A random star''s luminosity is not known in advance."}'),
  ('PHY-0405', 'Space and environment', 'Standard candles', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 167 (§21.3)', 'Cepheid variable stars became useful standard candles after it was found that they pulse in a predictable way. This was discovered in the 1910s by', '["Edwin Hubble", "Henrietta Swan Leavitt", "Wilhelm Wien", "Albert Einstein"]', 1, 'Henrietta Swan Leavitt found the predictable pulsation of Cepheids, which links their period to their luminosity.', '{"0": "Hubble later used Cepheids to measure galaxy distances; he did not discover the relation.", "2": "Wien is known for the displacement law of blackbody radiation.", "3": "Einstein did not discover the Cepheid relation."}'),
  ('PHY-0406', 'Space and environment', 'Standard candles', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 168 (§21.3)', 'Once the luminosity L of a standard candle is known and its flux F at Earth is measured, its distance d is found from', '["d = L ÷ 4πF", "d = 4πF ÷ L", "d = √(L ÷ 4πF)", "d = √(4πF ÷ L)"]', 2, 'From F = L/4πd², d² = L/4πF, so d = √(L/4πF).', '{"0": "This gives d², not d.", "1": "This is the reciprocal of d².", "3": "This is the reciprocal of d."}'),
  ('PHY-0407', 'Space and environment', 'Standard candles', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 168 (§21.3)', 'A standard candle has luminosity 4π × 10³⁰ W and its measured flux at Earth is 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ W m⁻². How far away is it?', '["1.0 × 10⁴⁰ m", "1.0 × 10¹⁰ m", "2.8 × 10¹⁹ m", "1.0 × 10²⁰ m"]', 3, 'd = √(L ÷ 4πF) = √(4π × 10³⁰ ÷ (4π × 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰)) = √(10⁴⁰) = 1.0 × 10²⁰ m.', '{"0": "1.0 × 10⁴⁰ is d², not d.", "1": "1.0 × 10¹⁰ takes the square root twice.", "2": "2.8 × 10¹⁹ takes the luminosity as 10³⁰ W, dropping its factor 4π."}'),
  ('PHY-0408', 'Space and environment', 'Blackbody radiation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 168 (§21.4)', 'A blackbody is an ideal object that', '["absorbs all radiation falling on it and emits radiation at all wavelengths perfectly", "reflects all radiation falling on it", "emits only visible light", "emits no radiation at any temperature"]', 0, 'A perfect blackbody is a perfect absorber and a perfect emitter. Its radiation depends only on its temperature.', '{"1": "A perfect reflector absorbs nothing; that is the opposite of a blackbody.", "2": "A blackbody emits a continuous range, including infrared and ultraviolet.", "3": "Every body above absolute zero emits radiation."}'),
  ('PHY-0409', 'Space and environment', 'Blackbody radiation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 168 (§21.4)', 'A good practical model of a blackbody is', '["a polished silver sphere", "a hollow cavity with a small hole and blackened inner walls", "a sheet of clear glass", "a white painted box"]', 1, 'Radiation entering the small hole bounces around inside and is trapped, so the hole absorbs almost everything; when heated, it emits blackbody radiation.', '{"0": "Polished silver reflects most radiation.", "2": "Glass transmits most visible light.", "3": "White surfaces reflect most light."}'),
  ('PHY-0410', 'Space and environment', 'Blackbody radiation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 168 (§21.4)', 'As an object is heated more strongly, its colour changes from dull red to yellow to white. This shows that', '["it begins to reflect more light", "its emission depends on its shape", "the peak of its emitted radiation shifts to shorter wavelengths", "its peak wavelength grows longer"]', 2, 'Hotter bodies emit most strongly at shorter wavelengths, so the glow moves from red towards white (and blue).', '{"0": "The glow is emitted, not reflected, light.", "1": "Blackbody radiation depends only on temperature, not on shape or material.", "3": "The peak wavelength becomes shorter, not longer."}'),
  ('PHY-0411', 'Space and environment', 'Wien''s displacement law', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 170 (§21.4)', 'Using Wien''s constant b = 2.9 × 10⁻³ m K, what is the surface temperature of a star whose radiation peaks at 725 nm?', '["400 K", "40 000 K", "2.1 × 10⁻⁹ K", "4000 K"]', 3, 'T = b ÷ λ_max = 2.9 × 10⁻³ ÷ 725 × 10⁻⁹ = 4000 K.', '{"0": "400 K takes the wavelength ten times too long (7250 nm).", "1": "40 000 K takes the wavelength ten times too short (72.5 nm).", "2": "2.1 × 10⁻⁹ K multiplies b by λ instead of dividing."}'),
  ('PHY-0412', 'Space and environment', 'Wien''s displacement law', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 170 (§21.4)', 'At what wavelength does a star of surface temperature 10 000 K radiate most strongly? (b = 2.9 × 10⁻³ m K)', '["290 nm, in the ultraviolet", "2.9 μm, in the infrared", "580 nm, in the visible", "29 nm, in the X-ray region"]', 0, 'λ_max = b ÷ T = 2.9 × 10⁻³ ÷ 10 000 = 2.9 × 10⁻⁷ m = 290 nm, which is ultraviolet.', '{"1": "2.9 μm is ten times too long.", "2": "580 nm is the peak for a star of 5000 K.", "3": "29 nm is ten times too short."}'),
  ('PHY-0413', 'Space and environment', 'Wien''s displacement law', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 169 (§21.4)', 'The human body (about 310 K) emits radiation that peaks at about', '["940 nm, in the visible", "9.4 μm, in the infrared", "94 μm, in the microwave", "0.94 nm, in the X-ray region"]', 1, 'λ_max = 2.9 × 10⁻³ ÷ 310 ≈ 9.4 × 10⁻⁶ m = 9.4 μm, in the infrared. This is why night-vision devices can detect people.', '{"0": "940 nm is ten times too short, and lies just outside the visible range.", "2": "94 μm is ten times too long.", "3": "0.94 nm would need a temperature of millions of kelvin."}'),
  ('PHY-0414', 'Space and environment', 'Wien''s displacement law', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 170 (§21.4)', 'Stars A and B have the same radius. The radiation of A peaks at 300 nm and that of B at 600 nm. The ratio of the luminosity of A to that of B is', '["2", "4", "16", "1/16"]', 2, 'By Wien''s law, T_A/T_B = λ_B/λ_A = 2. With equal radii, L ∝ T⁴, so L_A/L_B = 2⁴ = 16.', '{"0": "2 is the temperature ratio, not the luminosity ratio.", "1": "4 uses T² instead of T⁴.", "3": "A is the hotter star, so it is more luminous, not less."}'),
  ('PHY-0415', 'Space and environment', 'Radius of a star', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 170 (§21.5)', 'To estimate the radius of a star, astronomers first find its surface temperature from its spectrum and its luminosity from its flux and distance. Which law then gives the radius?', '["Hubble''s law", "Wien''s law alone", "Coulomb''s law", "The Stefan–Boltzmann law"]', 3, 'Wien''s law gives T; the inverse-square law gives L from flux and distance; then L = 4πr²σT⁴ gives r.', '{"0": "Hubble''s law gives the recession speed of galaxies.", "1": "Wien''s law gives only the temperature; it says nothing about size.", "2": "Coulomb''s law concerns electric charges."}'),
  ('PHY-0416', 'Space and environment', 'Absorption spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 170 (§21.6)', 'Dark lines in the spectrum of a star are produced when', '["atoms in the cooler outer layers absorb particular wavelengths from the continuous spectrum", "the star stops emitting at those wavelengths because of its shape", "dust between stars reflects those wavelengths back", "the hot surface emits only those wavelengths"]', 0, 'Light from the hot surface passes through the cooler gas layers, whose atoms absorb wavelengths matching their energy-level transitions, leaving dark lines.', '{"1": "Blackbody emission does not depend on shape.", "2": "The lines come from absorption in the star''s atmosphere.", "3": "Specific emitted wavelengths would give bright lines, not dark ones."}'),
  ('PHY-0417', 'Space and environment', 'Absorption spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 171 (§21.6)', 'The atoms that absorb light in a star''s atmosphere re-emit it at the same wavelengths, yet dark lines still appear because', '["the re-emitted photons have much longer wavelengths", "the re-emitted photons go off in all directions, so few travel towards the observer", "the re-emitted photons are absorbed by the star''s core", "the atoms do not re-emit any light"]', 1, 'Re-emission is scattered in every direction, so the beam heading towards Earth keeps a deficit at those wavelengths.', '{"0": "The text says the light is re-emitted at the same wavelengths.", "2": "Re-emitted photons are scattered outwards, not into the core.", "3": "The text says the absorbed energy is re-emitted."}'),
  ('PHY-0418', 'Space and environment', 'Redshift', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 171 (§21.7)', 'A spectral line from a galaxy is observed at a shorter wavelength than its laboratory value. This shows that the galaxy is', '["moving away from the Earth", "at rest relative to the Earth", "moving towards the Earth", "cooling down"]', 2, 'A negative Δλ is a blueshift, which means the source is approaching. A redshift (longer wavelength) means it is receding.', '{"0": "Moving away would make the wavelength longer (redshift).", "1": "A source at rest would show no shift.", "3": "A wavelength shift of a line indicates motion, not cooling."}'),
  ('PHY-0419', 'Space and environment', 'Redshift', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 171 (§21.7)', 'A line with laboratory wavelength 500 nm is observed at 510 nm in a galaxy''s spectrum. What is the galaxy''s speed of recession? (c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)', '["6.0 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹", "1.5 × 10¹⁰ m s⁻¹", "3.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹", "6.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹"]', 3, 'z = Δλ/λ = 10/500 = 0.02. v = zc = 0.02 × 3 × 10⁸ = 6.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "6.0 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹ has the power of ten wrong.", "1": "1.5 × 10¹⁰ m s⁻¹ multiplies by 50 instead of 0.02; it exceeds the speed of light.", "2": "3.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ halves the correct value."}'),
  ('PHY-0420', 'Space and environment', 'Redshift', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 171 (§21.7)', 'The redshift of light from distant galaxies is explained in the text as being caused mainly by', '["the stretching of space itself as the universe expands", "the galaxies being cooler than the Sun", "dust absorbing blue light", "the gravity of the Earth"]', 0, 'As space expands, the light waves travelling through it are stretched, so their wavelengths become longer.', '{"1": "Temperature changes the shape of the continuous spectrum but does not shift the lines.", "2": "Dust can redden light but cannot shift the wavelengths of spectral lines.", "3": "Earth''s gravity has a negligible effect on light from galaxies."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0421', 'Space and environment', 'Redshift', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 171 (§21.7)', 'Why does redshift being seen in every direction support an expanding universe rather than the Earth being at its centre?', '["Because the Earth is at the exact centre of the universe", "Because in an expanding space every observer sees other galaxies moving away, so there is no special centre", "Because galaxies are all moving towards the Earth", "Because the redshift is caused by the Sun"]', 1, 'In uniform expansion the distance between every pair of galaxies grows, so observers in any galaxy see the same pattern. The balloon model illustrates this.', '{"0": "The text says the Earth is not at the centre of the universe.", "2": "Approaching galaxies would show blueshift, not redshift.", "3": "The Sun cannot shift the light of distant galaxies."}'),
  ('PHY-0422', 'Space and environment', 'Hubble''s law', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 172 (§21.8)', 'On a graph of the recession velocity of galaxies against their distance, the slope of the straight line gives', '["the speed of light", "the age of a single star", "the Hubble constant", "Wien''s constant"]', 2, 'v = H₀d, so the slope v/d of the straight-line graph is the Hubble constant H₀.', '{"0": "The speed of light is not the slope of this graph.", "1": "The graph says nothing about the age of individual stars.", "3": "Wien''s constant belongs to blackbody radiation."}'),
  ('PHY-0423', 'Space and environment', 'Hubble''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 172 (§21.8)', 'Taking the Hubble constant as 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ s⁻¹, what is the recession speed of a galaxy 1.0 × 10²⁴ m away?', '["2.3 × 10⁴² m s⁻¹", "4.3 × 10⁴¹ m s⁻¹", "2.3 × 10⁻⁶ m s⁻¹", "2.3 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹"]', 3, 'v = H₀d = 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ × 1.0 × 10²⁴ = 2.3 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "2.3 × 10⁴² adds the exponents with the wrong sign.", "1": "4.3 × 10⁴¹ divides d by H₀.", "2": "2.3 × 10⁻⁶ divides H₀ by d."}'),
  ('PHY-0424', 'Space and environment', 'Hubble''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 172 (§21.8)', 'A galaxy is receding at 4.6 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. Using H₀ = 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ s⁻¹, its distance from the Earth is', '["2.0 × 10²⁴ m", "1.1 × 10⁻¹¹ m", "5.0 × 10⁻²⁵ m", "2.0 × 10⁻¹² m"]', 0, 'd = v ÷ H₀ = 4.6 × 10⁶ ÷ 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ = 2.0 × 10²⁴ m.', '{"1": "1.1 × 10⁻¹¹ multiplies v by H₀.", "2": "5.0 × 10⁻²⁵ divides H₀ by v.", "3": "2.0 × 10⁻¹² divides by 2.3 × 10¹⁸ instead of 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸."}'),
  ('PHY-0425', 'Space and environment', 'Hubble''s law', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 172 (§21.8)', 'The quantity 1/H₀ gives a rough estimate of the age of the universe. With H₀ = 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ s⁻¹ (1 year ≈ 3.16 × 10⁷ s), this is about', '["1.4 billion years", "14 billion years", "140 billion years", "4.3 × 10¹⁷ years"]', 1, '1/H₀ = 1 ÷ 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ ≈ 4.35 × 10¹⁷ s. Dividing by 3.16 × 10⁷ s per year gives about 1.4 × 10¹⁰ years, close to the 13.8 billion years quoted in the text.', '{"0": "1.4 billion years is ten times too small.", "2": "140 billion years is ten times too large.", "3": "4.3 × 10¹⁷ is the time in seconds, not years."}'),
  ('PHY-0426', 'Space and environment', 'Big Bang', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 173 (§21.8)', 'According to the Big Bang theory as described in the text, the universe began about', '["4.6 billion years ago when the Sun formed", "6000 years ago", "13.8 billion years ago from a very small, hot, dense state", "it has always existed in a static state"]', 2, 'Running the expansion backwards suggests that all matter was once compressed into a tiny, hot, dense state about 13.8 billion years ago.', '{"0": "4.6 billion years is about the age of the Solar System, not the universe.", "1": "This is far too young and has no scientific basis.", "3": "Hubble''s law overturned the idea of a static universe."}'),
  ('PHY-0427', 'Space and environment', 'Satellite remote sensing', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 174 (§21.8)', 'In satellite remote sensing, what is the first element of the process?', '["Interpretation of the processed image", "Transmission of data to a ground station", "Recording by the sensor", "A source of electromagnetic radiation that illuminates the target"]', 3, 'The steps are: source and illumination, interaction with the atmosphere, interaction with the target, recording by the sensor, transmission and reception, then interpretation.', '{"0": "Interpretation is the last step.", "1": "Transmission comes after the sensor has recorded the data.", "2": "Recording comes after the radiation has interacted with the target."}'),
  ('PHY-0428', 'Space and environment', 'Satellite remote sensing', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 173 (§21.8)', 'A major advantage of satellite remote sensing over ground-based methods is that it', '["gives wide coverage and repeated observations over time", "needs no electromagnetic radiation", "requires the sensor to touch the target", "works only at night"]', 0, 'Satellites view large areas repeatedly, which makes long-term monitoring of deforestation, ocean temperature and the atmosphere possible.', '{"1": "Remote sensing depends on detecting electromagnetic radiation.", "2": "The sensors are remote, not in contact with the target.", "3": "It is not limited to night-time."}'),
  ('PHY-0429', 'Space and environment', 'Earth''s climate system', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 174 (§21.9)', 'In the Earth''s climate system, the cryosphere is made up of', '["living things", "ice and permafrost", "the upper rocky layer of the Earth", "the air around the Earth"]', 1, 'The five components are atmosphere (air), hydrosphere (water), cryosphere (ice and permafrost), lithosphere (upper rocky layer) and biosphere (living things).', '{"0": "Living things form the biosphere.", "2": "The upper rocky layer is the lithosphere.", "3": "The air is the atmosphere."}'),
  ('PHY-0430', 'Space and environment', 'Earth''s climate system', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 174 (§21.9)', 'The hydrosphere component of the climate system refers to', '["frozen ground", "plants and animals", "the water of the Earth, such as oceans, lakes and rivers", "the Earth''s crust"]', 2, 'The hydrosphere is the water component. Ice is grouped separately in the cryosphere.', '{"0": "Frozen ground (permafrost) belongs to the cryosphere.", "1": "Plants and animals make up the biosphere.", "3": "The crust and upper rocks are the lithosphere."}'),
  ('PHY-0431', 'Space and environment', 'Ocean currents and winds', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 175 (§21.10)', 'Surface ocean currents are mostly caused by', '["differences in salinity at great depth", "earthquakes on the sea floor", "the Moon''s light", "winds pushing the top layer of water"]', 3, 'Wind drives the surface currents. Deep (vertical) circulation is the thermohaline circulation, driven by temperature and salinity.', '{"0": "Salinity and temperature drive the deep thermohaline circulation, not mainly the surface currents.", "1": "Earthquakes do not drive steady ocean currents.", "2": "Moonlight carries far too little energy to drive currents."}'),
  ('PHY-0432', 'Space and environment', 'Ocean currents and winds', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 175 (§21.10)', 'Large circular ocean current systems called gyres turn', '["clockwise in the northern hemisphere and anticlockwise in the southern hemisphere", "anticlockwise in the northern hemisphere and clockwise in the southern hemisphere", "clockwise in both hemispheres", "in random directions"]', 0, 'The Earth''s rotation deflects moving water to the right in the north and to the left in the south, so gyres turn clockwise in the north and anticlockwise in the south.', '{"1": "This reverses the two hemispheres.", "2": "Deflection is opposite in the two hemispheres, so the gyres turn opposite ways.", "3": "Gyres have a fixed sense of rotation set by the Earth''s spin."}'),
  ('PHY-0433', 'Space and environment', 'Ocean currents and winds', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 175 (§21.10)', 'Wind belts such as the trade winds, westerlies and polar easterlies form mainly because of', '["the Earth''s magnetic field", "uneven heating of the Earth''s surface together with its rotation", "tides caused by the Moon", "volcanic eruptions"]', 1, 'Uneven heating makes warm air rise and cooler air move in, and the Earth''s rotation turns these flows into distinct wind belts.', '{"0": "The magnetic field does not drive the winds.", "2": "Tides move water, not the global wind belts.", "3": "Volcanoes are local and irregular."}'),
  ('PHY-0434', 'Space and environment', 'Earth''s energy budget', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 176 (§21.11)', 'The Earth''s energy budget is the balance between', '["the energy of winds and ocean currents", "nuclear energy and chemical energy", "solar energy absorbed by the Earth and the energy it sends back into space", "the energy used by people and the energy they produce"]', 2, 'It compares the solar energy the Earth absorbs with the energy lost to space by reflection and emission of infrared radiation.', '{"0": "Winds and currents redistribute energy within the Earth, they are not the budget itself.", "1": "The budget is about radiation in and out, not nuclear versus chemical energy.", "3": "Human energy use is tiny compared with the solar flows."}'),
  ('PHY-0435', 'Space and environment', 'Earth''s energy budget', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 176 (§21.11)', 'When the Earth warms and ice melts, darker land and ocean are exposed. The effect on further warming is that', '["more sunlight is reflected, so the Earth cools again", "the energy budget is unaffected", "the Earth stops emitting infrared", "more sunlight is absorbed, so the warming is reinforced"]', 3, 'Ice and snow are bright and reflect sunlight. Darker surfaces absorb more, which warms the Earth further.', '{"0": "Darker surfaces reflect less, not more.", "1": "Changes in reflection change the balance of energy.", "2": "The Earth always emits infrared radiation."}'),
  ('PHY-0436', 'Space and environment', 'Earth''s energy budget', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 176 (§21.11)', 'Greenhouse gases cause global warming mainly by', '["trapping the outgoing infrared radiation from the Earth", "reflecting incoming sunlight back to space", "blocking all ultraviolet light", "absorbing heat from the oceans"]', 0, 'Incoming energy is balanced by outgoing infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases trap some of this infrared, so less escapes and the Earth warms.', '{"1": "Reflecting sunlight would cool the Earth, not warm it.", "2": "Ozone, not the greenhouse effect, is linked to absorbing ultraviolet.", "3": "The key process is trapping outgoing infrared, not drawing heat from the oceans."}'),
  ('PHY-0437', 'Space and environment', 'Energy imbalance and circulation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 177 (§21.11)', 'Why is the equator heated more strongly than the poles?', '["The equator is closer to the Sun", "Sunlight strikes the equator directly but reaches the poles at a slant, spreading over a larger area", "The poles have no atmosphere", "The poles receive no sunlight at any time"]', 1, 'Direct rays deliver more energy per unit area. Oblique rays at the poles spread the same energy over a larger area, so heating is weaker.', '{"0": "The difference in distance to the Sun is negligible.", "2": "The poles do have an atmosphere.", "3": "The poles do receive sunlight, though at a low angle."}'),
  ('PHY-0438', 'Space and environment', 'Energy imbalance and circulation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 177 (§21.11)', 'At the equator, strong heating produces', '["sinking cold air and a high-pressure zone", "no air movement at all", "rising warm air and a low-pressure zone", "permanent ice cover"]', 2, 'Warm air at the equator rises, forming low pressure. At the poles cold air sinks, forming high pressure. This imbalance drives global circulation.', '{"0": "Sinking cold air and high pressure occur at the poles.", "1": "The imbalance drives strong air movement.", "3": "The equator is the warmest part of the Earth."}'),
  ('PHY-0439', 'Space and environment', 'Atmospheric circulation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 177 (§21.12)', 'As an air mass moves from the equator towards a pole, its distance r from the Earth''s axis decreases. If its specific angular momentum rv stays constant, its speed v', '["decreases", "stays the same", "becomes zero", "increases"]', 3, 'rv is constant, so a smaller r means a larger v.', '{"0": "v must rise, not fall, when r falls with rv fixed.", "1": "v can only stay the same if r does.", "2": "v becomes larger, not zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0440', 'Space and environment', 'Atmospheric circulation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 178 (§21.12)', 'Winds in the southern hemisphere are deflected', '["to the left of their direction of motion", "to the right of their direction of motion", "straight upwards", "not at all"]', 0, 'The Coriolis effect deflects moving air to the right in the northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern hemisphere.', '{"1": "Deflection to the right happens in the northern hemisphere.", "2": "The Coriolis effect is a sideways, not vertical, deflection.", "3": "The Earth''s rotation does deflect moving air."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0441', 'Space and environment', 'Atmospheric circulation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 178 (§21.12)', 'The three atmospheric circulation cells in each hemisphere are the', '["Hadley, Wien and Hubble cells", "Hadley, Ferrel and Polar cells", "Ferrel, Kelvin and Curie cells", "Trade, Westerly and Easterly cells"]', 1, 'Deflected air flows form the Hadley cell, the Ferrel cell and the Polar cell in each hemisphere.', '{"0": "Wien and Hubble are linked to radiation and cosmology, not air cells.", "2": "Kelvin and Curie cells are not atmospheric cells.", "3": "Trade winds, westerlies and easterlies are wind belts, not the names of the cells."}'),
  ('PHY-0442', 'Space and environment', 'Atmospheric circulation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 178 (§21.12)', 'The Coriolis deflection of moving air', '["changes its speed but not its direction", "stops the air completely", "changes the direction of the air but not its speed", "makes the air rise vertically"]', 2, 'The text notes that the deflection does not affect the speed of the air but changes its path, giving curved wind patterns.', '{"0": "It is the direction that changes, not the speed.", "1": "The air keeps moving along a curved path.", "3": "The deflection is sideways, not upwards."}'),
  ('PHY-0443', 'Space and environment', 'Salinity and ocean circulation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 178 (§21.13)', 'Saltier sea water is denser than less salty water because dissolved salt', '["reduces the mass of the water", "increases the volume a lot", "raises the water''s temperature", "adds mass without increasing the volume much"]', 3, 'Salt increases the mass of the water while its volume hardly changes, so density rises.', '{"0": "Dissolving salt adds mass; it does not remove it.", "1": "A large volume increase would lower the density.", "2": "Salt does not heat the water."}'),
  ('PHY-0444', 'Space and environment', 'Salinity and ocean circulation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 178 (§21.13)', 'Surface water near the poles sinks to the ocean depths because it becomes', '["colder and saltier, and so denser", "warmer and fresher, and so less dense", "lighter than the water below", "frozen solid"]', 0, 'Cooling and sea-ice formation (which leaves salt behind) make the water colder and saltier. Being denser, it sinks.', '{"1": "Warmer, fresher water is less dense and stays at the surface.", "2": "Water sinks because it is denser, not lighter.", "3": "Sea ice floats; it is the dense unfrozen water that sinks."}'),
  ('PHY-0445', 'Space and environment', 'Thermohaline circulation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 179 (§21.14)', 'About how long does the thermohaline circulation (the global conveyor belt) take to complete one full cycle through the world''s oceans?', '["about 1 year", "about 1000 years", "about 10 years", "about 1 million years"]', 1, 'The text states that the global conveyor belt takes about 1000 years to cycle water through all the ocean basins.', '{"0": "One year is far too short for deep-ocean circulation.", "2": "Ten years is still far too short.", "3": "One million years is far too long."}'),
  ('PHY-0446', 'Space and environment', 'Thermohaline circulation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 178 (§21.14)', 'In the thermohaline circulation, heat is carried from the tropics to the polar regions by', '["cold, dense deep water flowing poleward", "sea ice drifting towards the equator", "warm, less dense surface water flowing poleward", "rain carried by the trade winds"]', 2, 'Warm surface water moves towards the poles carrying heat. There it cools, becomes denser, sinks and returns towards the equator at depth.', '{"0": "Cold deep water flows back towards the equator, not poleward.", "1": "Sea ice is not the main carrier of heat.", "3": "Rain is not the mechanism described for this circulation."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

